window.DM_BANK_EXT=[{id:"5002-001",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Reading",q:"Which text structure organizes information by listing reasons and supporting details about a single topic?",options:["Description","Cause and effect","Problem and solution","Sequence"],answer:0,explain:"A descriptive structure presents a topic and then lists characteristics, features, or supporting details. Cause and effect shows relationships between events, while sequence organizes events in time order."},{id:"5002-002",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Reading",q:"Read the passage: 'The caterpillar chewed through the leaf with methodical persistence. Each morning, it left a growing scar of consumption behind.' What literary device is primarily used in this passage?",options:["Simile","Personification","Onomatopoeia","Hyperbole"],answer:1,explain:"The passage attributes human qualities (methodical persistence) to the caterpillar, which is personification. There is no exaggeration for hyperbole, no comparison using like or as for simile, and no words that imitate sounds for onomatopoeia."},{id:"5002-003",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Reading",q:"Students are learning to identify the main idea of a paragraph. Which instructional approach most effectively builds this skill over time?",options:["Provide worksheets with multiple-choice questions only","Have students memorize the definition of main idea","Model finding the main idea, gradually releasing responsibility as students practice with guided support","Assign students to underline every sentence in each paragraph"],answer:2,explain:"Gradual release of responsibility\u2014modeling, guided practice, independent application\u2014is research-based for building comprehension skills. Memorization alone does not develop skill, underlining every sentence prevents discrimination of importance, and worksheets without instruction do not teach the strategy."},{id:"5002-004",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Reading",q:"What is the primary function of a caption in an informational text?",options:["To summarize the entire chapter","To list the bibliography sources","To provide the table of contents","To explain or add detail to a photograph or illustration"],answer:3,explain:"Captions appear directly below images and provide brief explanations or additional information about what the image shows. They do not serve as tables of contents, bibliographies, or chapter summaries."},{id:"5002-005",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Reading",q:"In the sentence 'The ancient library, a treasure trove of scrolls, stood at the city center,' what is the function of the phrase 'a treasure trove of scrolls'?",options:["It is an appositive that renames and describes the library","It is a dependent clause that modifies the verb","It is a prepositional phrase indicating location","It is a compound predicate describing actions"],answer:0,explain:"An appositive is a noun phrase that renames or explains another noun right beside it. Here, 'a treasure trove of scrolls' renames 'the ancient library.' It is not a clause because it lacks a verb, nor is it merely indicating location."},{id:"5002-006",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Reading",q:"An author writes: 'The policy, though well-intentioned, produced outcomes opposite to those intended.' Which rhetorical strategy is the author employing?",options:["Appeal to emotion through vivid imagery","Situational irony highlighting the contrast between intention and outcome","Direct argument with supporting evidence","Simple chronological narration"],answer:1,explain:"The sentence highlights situational irony: a well-intentioned policy produces the opposite of its intended result. A paradox would require an apparently self-contradictory statement that reveals a truth; this sentence instead contrasts expectation with outcome."},{id:"5002-007",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Reading",q:"A fourth-grade student can decode words accurately but reads without expression and has difficulty recalling what was read. Which intervention would most directly address both issues?",options:["Drill sight words repeatedly until automatic","Assign more challenging independent reading texts","Use echo reading and guided oral reading with comprehension questions","Have the student read silently for longer periods each day"],answer:2,explain:"Echo reading (teacher reads, student repeats) builds fluency and expression, while paired comprehension questions address recall. The student already decodes well, so word drills target an unrelated need. Silent reading alone without strategy instruction does not build expression or retention."},{id:"5002-008",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Reading",q:"Which punctuation mark correctly joins two independent clauses without a conjunction?",options:["Comma","Apostrophe","Colon","Semicolon"],answer:3,explain:"A semicolon can connect two closely related independent clauses without a coordinating conjunction. A comma alone creates a comma splice. A colon introduces explanation or list. An apostrophe shows possession or contraction."},{id:"5002-009",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Reading",q:"A passage describes how indigenous communities managed forest fires through controlled burning. The author's purpose is primarily to:",options:["Inform readers about traditional ecological knowledge and its relevance","Entertain with stories of ancient forests","Persuade readers to move to rural areas","Argue that all modern fire prevention policies are wrong"],answer:0,explain:"The passage presents factual information about traditional practices and their significance, which is informative in purpose. It does not attack modern policies (too extreme), tell fictional stories, or advocate relocation. The balanced presentation indicates information-sharing about ecological knowledge."},{id:"5002-010",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Reading",q:"In the sentence 'Had the committee acted sooner, the project might have succeeded,' what is the implied meaning?",options:["The project succeeded despite committee hesitation","The committee did not act soon enough, and the project likely failed","The committee acted on time and the project succeeded","The committee was unsure about the project timeline"],answer:1,explain:"This is a third conditional expressing an unreal past condition. The inverted structure 'Had the committee acted' implies they did not act sooner, and 'might have succeeded' suggests the project probably did not succeed. It describes a hypothetical past contrary to what actually happened."},{id:"5002-011",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Reading",q:"Which strategy best helps emergent bilingual students access grade-level informational text?",options:["Provide only simplified texts at their reading level","Require students to read silently without assistance","Pre-teach key vocabulary with visuals and allow use of bilingual dictionaries during reading","Have students listen to the text without reading along"],answer:2,explain:"Scaffolding with pre-taught vocabulary, visual supports, and bilingual resources maintains access to grade-level content while building language skills. Simplified texts create achievement gaps, silent reading without support leaves struggles hidden, and listening-only skips decoding practice."},{id:"5002-012",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Reading",q:"What is the difference between fact and opinion in informational writing?",options:["Opinions appear in textbooks; facts appear in editorials","Facts are always longer than opinions","There is no meaningful difference between them","Facts can be verified; opinions express beliefs or judgments"],answer:3,explain:"A fact is a statement that can be proven true or false through evidence or verification. An opinion expresses a personal belief, feeling, or judgment that cannot be objectively proven. Length and placement in publications do not determine the distinction."},{id:"5002-013",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Reading",q:"Which transition word best shows contrast between two ideas?",options:["However","Therefore","Additionally","Consequently"],answer:0,explain:"'However' signals contrast or opposition between ideas. 'Therefore' and 'consequently' indicate cause and effect. 'Additionally' adds information rather than contrasting it."},{id:"5002-014",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Reading",q:"After a class discussion of a complex poem, a student says 'I just don't get it.' Which teacher response best models metacognitive strategy use?",options:[`"Don't worry about it; poems are supposed to be hard."`,`"Let's reread the confusing stanza together and ask ourselves what we expect to find."`,'"The poem is difficult; just memorize the main idea."','"Ask your parents for help with homework."'],answer:1,explain:"Modeling metacognition involves making thinking visible\u2014re-reading, setting purpose, monitoring comprehension. Telling students to memorize bypasses understanding. Sending them to parents externalizes responsibility. Dismissing the difficulty models resignation rather than strategy use."},{id:"5002-015",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Reading",q:"A teacher displays the word 'unhappiness' on the board and asks students to identify its parts. Which analysis is correct?",options:["un (root) + happy (affix) + ness (root)","unh (root) + a (prefix) + piness (suffix)","un- (prefix) + happy (root) + -ness (suffix)","una (prefix) + happy (root) + ness (suffix)"],answer:2,explain:"'Un-' is a bound morpheme meaning 'not' that attaches to the root 'happy.' '-ness' is a suffix that forms a noun from an adjective. The root 'happy' carries the core meaning. The other options misidentify bound morphemes as roots or split the root incorrectly."},{id:"5002-016",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Writing, Speaking, and Listening",q:"Which sentence uses the correct form of 'there,' 'their,' or 'they're'?",options:["There going to the park after their done with homework.","Theyre going to the park after there done with homework.","Their going to the park after theyre done with homework.","They're going to the park after they're done with homework."],answer:3,explain:"'They're' is a contraction of 'they are' and is correct in both positions. 'Their' shows possession. 'There' refers to place. The other options mix these homophones incorrectly."},{id:"5002-017",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Writing, Speaking, and Listening",q:"Which revision best corrects the run-on sentence 'The experiment failed the students tried again'?",options:["The experiment failed; the students tried again.","The experiment failed, the students tried again.","The experiment failed the students, tried again.","The experiment, failed the students tried again."],answer:0,explain:"A semicolon correctly joins two independent clauses that are closely related. A comma alone creates a comma splice. The other options place commas incorrectly or fail to fix the run-on structure."},{id:"5002-018",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Writing, Speaking, and Listening",q:"A sixth-grade student writes: 'The civil war was when many things happened.' Which feedback approach most effectively helps the student improve this thesis?",options:["Circle 'was when' and write 'fix this' in the margin","Ask the student to identify who fought in the war and what the central conflict was about","Rewrite the thesis for the student and have them copy it","Tell the student the thesis is acceptable as is"],answer:1,explain:"Guiding questions that prompt the student to specify content help them revise their own thinking. Vague feedback like 'fix this' is unhelpful. Rewriting for the student deprives them of practice. Accepting vague writing does not build skill."},{id:"5002-019",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Writing, Speaking, and Listening",q:"Which statement best distinguishes between form and function in grammar?",options:["Form refers to meaning; function refers to spelling","Form applies only to verbs; function applies only to nouns","Form is the physical structure of a word; function is its role in a sentence","Form and function are identical concepts in grammar"],answer:2,explain:"Form describes what a word looks like (its structure, ending, category), while function describes what it does in a sentence (its grammatical role). They are distinct but related concepts. Neither applies exclusively to one part of speech."},{id:"5002-020",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Writing, Speaking, and Listening",q:"What is the purpose of an outline before writing an essay?",options:["To copy vocabulary words into sentences","To ensure perfect grammar in every sentence","To replace the need for drafting","To organize ideas and plan the structure before writing"],answer:3,explain:"Outlines help writers organize thoughts, plan logical progression, and structure arguments before composing full sentences. They do not replace drafting, guarantee grammatical correctness, or serve as vocabulary exercises."},{id:"5002-021",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Writing, Speaking, and Listening",q:"During a peer review activity, students struggle to give constructive feedback. Which scaffold best supports effective peer review?",options:["Provide a feedback template with sentence stems like 'I noticed...' and 'One question I have is...'","Have students only compliment each other's work","Allow students to review without any guidelines","Tell students to find and circle all errors"],answer:0,explain:"Sentence stems give students concrete language for giving specific, actionable feedback. Simply finding errors is reductionist. No guidelines lead to unhelpful comments. Only compliments avoid critical analysis entirely."},{id:"5002-022",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Writing, Speaking, and Listening",q:"A student writes: 'Although the data seemed accurate, upon further review several discrepancies were found.' Which revision preserves the meaning while eliminating the dangling modifier?",options:["'Although the data seemed accurate, several discrepancies were found upon further review by the team.'","'Although the data seemed accurate, we found several discrepancies upon further review.'","'Upon further review, several discrepancies were found although the data seemed accurate.'","The original sentence is already correct"],answer:1,explain:"The original leaves no clear person performing 'upon further review.' Naming the reviewers\u2014'we found several discrepancies'\u2014gives the modifier a logical subject; the other revisions keep an awkward or unclear attachment."},{id:"5002-023",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Writing, Speaking, and Listening",q:"Which element is essential to a well-structured paragraph?",options:["At least ten sentences","Direct quotes from at least three sources","A topic sentence, supporting details, and a concluding sentence","Complex vocabulary throughout"],answer:2,explain:"A paragraph needs a clear topic (usually in a topic sentence), development through supporting details, and closure. Length, source count, and vocabulary complexity are not structural requirements."},{id:"5002-024",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Writing, Speaking, and Listening",q:"What is the primary difference between a run-on sentence and a comma splice?",options:["A comma splice contains three or more clauses","There is no difference between them","A run-on is always longer than a comma splice","A run-on has no punctuation; a comma splice uses only a comma"],answer:3,explain:"Both are types of fused sentences. A run-on joins independent clauses with no punctuation or conjunction. A comma splice joins them with only a comma. Length and clause count do not define the distinction."},{id:"5002-025",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Writing, Speaking, and Listening",q:"A multilingual learner submits an essay with frequent article errors (missing 'a,' 'an,' 'the'). Which instructional response is most appropriate?",options:["Focus feedback on one pattern at a time and provide targeted practice sentences","Tell the student articles do not matter for communication","Assign extra grammar worksheets on all English rules simultaneously","Correct every article error with red pen before reading the content"],answer:0,explain:"Focusing on one error pattern allows the student to master it before moving to another. Correcting every error overwhelms and shifts focus from meaning. Saying articles do not matter ignores a real feature of Standard English. Overloading with all rules simultaneously prevents mastery."},{id:"5002-026",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Writing, Speaking, and Listening",q:"Which sentence demonstrates correct subject-verb agreement with a collective noun?",options:["The team are winning its games.","The team is winning its games.","The team are winning their games.","The team is winning their games."],answer:1,explain:"When 'team' is treated as a single unit (singular), it takes the singular verb 'is' and singular possessive 'its.' Using 'are' with 'team' treats it as individual members, which changes the meaning. Mixing singular verb with plural possessive is inconsistent."},{id:"5002-027",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Writing, Speaking, and Listening",q:"What is the function of an introductory paragraph in an essay?",options:["To summarize the entire paper in one sentence","To repeat the conclusion verbatim","To hook the reader, provide background, and state the thesis","To list all sources used in the paper"],answer:2,explain:"An introduction engages the reader, provides necessary context, and presents the central argument (thesis). It does not summarize the paper, list sources, or repeat conclusions."},{id:"5002-028",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Writing, Speaking, and Listening",q:"Students are learning to write effective conclusions. Which example best models a strong concluding paragraph?",options:["A new topic to consider is whether schools should require students to recycle at home.","In conclusion, I discussed pollution, recycling, and conservation. That is all.","Recycling is important because it helps the environment and saves resources.","As this essay has shown, individual actions such as recycling can conserve resources, and widespread participation can create meaningful environmental change."],answer:3,explain:"A strong conclusion synthesizes the main idea and shows its significance without adding an unrelated topic. The correct paragraph links individual recycling to broader environmental change, while the distractors merely list points, restate a claim, or introduce a new issue."},{id:"5002-029",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Writing, Speaking, and Listening",q:"Which revision best improves the parallel structure in the sentence: 'The coach told the players to run fast, to dodge opponents, and they should shoot accurately'?",options:["'The coach told the players to run fast, to dodge opponents, and to shoot accurately.'","'The coach told the players running fast, dodging opponents, and shooting accurately.'","'The coach told the players: run fast, dodge opponents, shoot accurately.'","The original sentence already has correct parallel structure"],answer:0,explain:"Parallel structure requires matching grammatical forms. 'To run,' 'to dodge,' and 'to shoot' are three parallel infinitive phrases; the distractors mix forms or change the sentence into an awkward construction."},{id:"5002-030",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Writing, Speaking, and Listening",q:"What is the purpose of transitional words and phrases in writing?",options:["To replace the need for topic sentences","To show relationships between ideas and guide the reader","To make sentences longer","To add decorative language"],answer:1,explain:"Transitions signal logical relationships\u2014contrast, cause-effect, sequence, addition\u2014helping readers follow the argument. They are not filler, replacements for structure, or mere decoration."},{id:"5003-001",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Numbers and Operations",q:"What is the least common multiple of 6 and 8?",options:["48","16","24","12"],answer:2,explain:"The multiples of 6 are 6, 12, 18, 24... The multiples of 8 are 8, 16, 24... The smallest common multiple is 24. Twelve is only a multiple of 6, sixteen only of 8, and 48 is a common multiple but not the least."},{id:"5003-002",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Numbers and Operations",q:"A student explains that 0.5 is greater than 0.35 because 5 is greater than 35. Which representation best addresses this misconception?",options:["Convert both numbers to fractions without visual support","Show that 0.35 has more digits than 0.5","Tell the student to memorize that 0.5 is larger","Use base-ten blocks to model 0.5 as 5 tenths and 0.35 as 3 tenths and 5 hundredths"],answer:3,explain:"Base-ten blocks make the place value explicit: 0.5 fills 5 of 10 columns while 0.35 fills 3 full columns plus 5 small squares. The digit-count argument reinforces the misconception. Memorization does not build understanding. Fraction conversion without visualization does not address the root confusion."},{id:"5003-003",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Numbers and Operations",q:"Which assessment question best determines whether a student understands place value in decimals?",options:["'Model 2.47 using base-ten blocks and explain what each block represents.'","'Read the number 3.14 aloud.'","'Circle the digit 5 in the number 15.72.'","'What is 0.3 + 0.4?'"],answer:0,explain:"Having a student model and explain demonstrates conceptual understanding of place value. Simple computation tests procedure only. Reading the number tests naming, not value. Identifying a digit tests recognition, not understanding of magnitude."},{id:"5003-004",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Numbers and Operations",q:"Which number is equivalent to 3/4?",options:["3.4","0.75","0.43","0.34"],answer:1,explain:"Dividing 3 by 4 gives 0.75. The other decimals represent different place values and do not equal three fourths."},{id:"5003-005",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Numbers and Operations",q:"What is the greatest common factor of 12 and 18?",options:["9","3","6","36"],answer:2,explain:"The factors of 12 are 1, 2, 3, 4, 6, 12. The factors of 18 are 1, 2, 3, 6, 9, 18. The greatest common factor is 6. Three is a common factor but not the greatest. Thirty-six is a multiple, not a factor."},{id:"5003-006",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Numbers and Operations",q:"Why is 2/3 greater than 3/5 even though 3 > 2 and 5 > 3?",options:["Because 2 divided by 3 equals a whole number","Because fractions with smaller numbers are always larger","Because the numerators are always more important than denominators","Because 2/3 represents 40 parts out of 60 while 3/5 represents 36 parts out of 60, making 2/3 larger"],answer:3,explain:"Finding a common denominator (60) shows that 2/3 = 40/60 and 3/5 = 36/60. Since 40 > 36, 2/3 is larger. Comparing numerators or denominators alone ignores that the size of fractional parts depends on both. Fractions do not follow simple whole-number ordering rules."},{id:"5003-007",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Numbers and Operations",q:"A student adds 1/4 + 1/3 and gets 2/7. Which correction strategy addresses the misconception most directly?",options:["Use fraction circles to show that 1/4 + 1/3 covers more than 1/2, while 2/7 is less than 1/2","Ask the student to add the denominators first and see if it works","Have the student memorize the rule for adding fractions","Tell the student the answer is wrong and show the correct method"],answer:0,explain:"Visual models reveal the error: 1/4 plus 1/3 clearly exceeds half, while 2/7 is less than half. This creates cognitive conflict that motivates reconsideration. Simply correcting or memorizing does not address the additive misconception. Asking the student to verify with the incorrect method reinforces the error."},{id:"5003-008",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Numbers and Operations",q:"Which of the following is a prime number?",options:["21","23","25","15"],answer:1,explain:"A prime number has exactly two distinct factors: 1 and itself. Twenty-three is only divisible by 1 and 23. Fifteen is 3 \xD7 5, twenty-one is 3 \xD7 7, and twenty-five is 5 \xD7 5. None of these are prime."},{id:"5003-009",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Numbers and Operations",q:"What is 15% of 240?",options:["24","150","36","48"],answer:2,explain:"15% = 0.15. Multiplying 0.15 \xD7 240 = 36. Alternatively, 10% of 240 is 24, and 5% is half of that (12), so 24 + 12 = 36."},{id:"5003-010",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Numbers and Operations",q:"Which statement about the relationship between multiplication and division is ALWAYS true?",options:["Multiplication always produces a larger result than division","Multiplication and division always use the same numbers","You can divide by any number without restriction","Division is the inverse operation of multiplication"],answer:3,explain:"Division undoes multiplication: if a \xD7 b = c, then c \xF7 b = a (for nonzero b). This inverse relationship is fundamental. Multiplication does not always produce larger results (e.g., multiplying by a fraction less than 1). Division by zero is undefined. The operations do not always use the same numbers."},{id:"5003-011",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Algebraic Thinking",q:"Which expression is equivalent to 3 \xD7 (4 + 5)?",options:["3 \xD7 4 + 3 \xD7 5","3 \xD7 4 + 5","3 \xD7 4 \xD7 5","3 + 4 \xD7 5"],answer:0,explain:"The distributive property states that a \xD7 (b + c) = a \xD7 b + a \xD7 c. So 3 \xD7 (4 + 5) = 3 \xD7 4 + 3 \xD7 5 = 12 + 15 = 27. The other options misapply the operation order or distribution."},{id:"5003-012",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Algebraic Thinking",q:"A pattern begins: 2, 5, 8, 11, 14... Which rule describes the nth term?",options:["2n + 3","3n - 1","n \xD7 n - 1","n + 3"],answer:1,explain:"Each term increases by 3, suggesting 3n. For n=1, 3(1) = 3, but the first term is 2, so subtract 1: 3n - 1. Checking: 3(1)-1=2, 3(2)-1=5, 3(3)-1=8. The other rules produce incorrect values for the sequence."},{id:"5003-013",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Algebraic Thinking",q:"Students are learning to write equations from word problems. Which scaffold most effectively supports this transition?",options:["Skip word problems and focus on computation only","Have students memorize equation templates","Use bar models or tape diagrams to represent relationships before writing symbols","Provide equations with blanks to fill in"],answer:2,explain:"Visual models like bar models make the quantitative relationships concrete before abstracting to symbols. Memorization bypasses understanding. Fill-in-the-blank equations do not teach equation formation. Skipping word problems avoids the skill entirely."},{id:"5003-014",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Algebraic Thinking",q:"If x + 7 = 15, what is the value of x?",options:["7","15","22","8"],answer:3,explain:"Subtract 7 from both sides: x = 15 - 7 = 8. Check: 8 + 7 = 15. Seven is too small, fifteen is the sum not the missing addend, and twenty-two results from adding instead of subtracting."},{id:"5003-015",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Algebraic Thinking",q:"Which value of x makes the equation 4x = 20 true?",options:["5","16","4","24"],answer:0,explain:"Divide both sides by 4: x = 20 \xF7 4 = 5. Check: 4 \xD7 5 = 20. Four is the multiplier, not the solution. Sixteen and twenty-four come from incorrect operations."},{id:"5003-016",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Algebraic Thinking",q:"Which statement best explains why 0.5x and x/2 are equivalent expressions?",options:["They use the same letters","Dividing by 2 is the same as multiplying by one-half","They always produce different results","Zero makes them equal"],answer:1,explain:"Dividing by 2 and multiplying by 1/2 are inverse operations that yield the same result: x \xF7 2 = x \xD7 (1/2) = 0.5x. Same letters alone does not prove equivalence. Zero makes both expressions equal but does not explain general equivalence."},{id:"5003-017",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Algebraic Thinking",q:"A student writes '5 + n = 12' to represent 'five more than a number is twelve.' Which response best validates and extends this thinking?",options:["'Just change it to multiplication instead.'","'Five plus n is never equal to twelve.'","'Your equation is correct. Now write a second equation that shows how to solve for n.'","'That is wrong; it should be n + 5 = 12.'"],answer:2,explain:"The student's equation is algebraically correct\u2014addition is commutative. Validating the work builds confidence, and asking for a solution equation extends the reasoning. Insisting on a specific order imposes arbitrary convention. Suggesting multiplication changes the problem."},{id:"5003-018",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Algebraic Thinking",q:"Which number line shows the solution to x - 3 < 5?",options:["A closed circle at 8 with shading to the right","A closed circle at 2 with shading to the left","An open circle at 2 with shading to the right","An open circle at 8 with shading to the left"],answer:3,explain:"Solving: x - 3 < 5 \u2192 x < 8. An open circle at 8 (not included) with shading left shows all numbers less than 8. A closed circle would include 8. Shading right shows greater values. The boundary is 8, not 2."},{id:"5003-019",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Algebraic Thinking",q:"Two patterns are generated: Pattern A starts at 2 and adds 3 each time. Pattern B starts at 2 and adds 5 each time. Which statement is true about the terms?",options:["The terms in Pattern B increase faster, so they become larger than Pattern A after the first term","Pattern A will eventually surpass Pattern B","Every term in Pattern B is exactly 2 more than Pattern A","Pattern A and Pattern B have the same terms"],answer:0,explain:"Both start at 2, but Pattern B adds 5 each step while Pattern A adds 3. After the first term (both are 2), Pattern B terms grow faster: 7 vs 5, 12 vs 8, etc. The difference between corresponding terms increases by 2 each step. Pattern A never catches up."},{id:"5003-020",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Algebraic Thinking",q:"What is the value of the expression 2x + 3 when x = 4?",options:["20","11","14","9"],answer:1,explain:"Substitute x = 4: 2(4) + 3 = 8 + 3 = 11. Nine results from multiplying 2 \xD7 3 instead. Fourteen comes from 2 \xD7 4 \xD7 3. Twenty results from 2 \xD7 (4 + 3) without distributing correctly."},{id:"5003-021",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Geometry and Measurement, Data, Statistics, and Probability",q:"What is the area of a rectangle with length 8 cm and width 5 cm?",options:["26 cm\xB2","13 cm\xB2","40 cm\xB2","80 cm\xB2"],answer:2,explain:"Area = length \xD7 width = 8 \xD7 5 = 40 cm\xB2. Thirteen is the sum (perimeter-related error). Twenty-six is the perimeter. Eighty results from doubling both dimensions."},{id:"5003-022",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Geometry and Measurement, Data, Statistics, and Probability",q:"Which statement about triangles is ALWAYS true?",options:["Triangles must have at least two equal angles","All sides must be different lengths","All triangles have at least one right angle","The sum of interior angles is always 180 degrees"],answer:3,explain:"The triangle angle sum theorem states that interior angles always total 180\xB0 regardless of triangle type. Not all triangles are right triangles. Equilateral triangles have three equal sides. Isosceles triangles have two equal angles, but scalene triangles have none."},{id:"5003-023",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Geometry and Measurement, Data, Statistics, and Probability",q:"Students confuse perimeter and area. Which activity best helps them distinguish the two concepts?",options:["Use grid paper to count units for area and unit cubes to trace around for perimeter","Have students memorize the formulas for each","Assign worksheets with both calculations side by side","Tell students perimeter is outside and area is inside"],answer:0,explain:"Concrete manipulation\u2014counting squares for area and laying cubes along edges for perimeter\u2014creates distinct sensory experiences for each concept. Memorization without experience does not prevent confusion. Worksheets without context reinforce procedural over conceptual understanding. Metaphors alone are insufficient."},{id:"5003-024",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Geometry and Measurement, Data, Statistics, and Probability",q:"What is the mean of the data set: 4, 7, 7, 9, 13?",options:["40","8","9","7"],answer:1,explain:"Mean = sum \xF7 count = (4 + 7 + 7 + 9 + 13) \xF7 5 = 40 \xF7 5 = 8. Seven is the median (middle value). Nine is incorrect arithmetic. Forty is the sum, not the mean."},{id:"5003-025",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Geometry and Measurement, Data, Statistics, and Probability",q:"Which angle measure represents a right angle?",options:["45\xB0","360\xB0","90\xB0","180\xB0"],answer:2,explain:"A right angle measures exactly 90\xB0. Forty-five degrees is acute. One hundred eighty degrees is a straight angle. Three hundred sixty degrees is a full rotation."},{id:"5003-026",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Geometry and Measurement, Data, Statistics, and Probability",q:"A rectangle has length 12 cm and width 5 cm. If both dimensions are doubled, what happens to the area?",options:["The area doubles","The area triples","The area stays the same","The area quadruples"],answer:3,explain:"Original area = 12 \xD7 5 = 60 cm\xB2. New dimensions: 24 cm \xD7 10 cm = 240 cm\xB2. Since 240 = 4 \xD7 60, the area quadruples. Doubling each dimension multiplies area by 2 \xD7 2 = 4. The area does not simply double."},{id:"5003-027",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Geometry and Measurement, Data, Statistics, and Probability",q:"A student measures a table's area as 24 square feet but its perimeter as 20 feet. The teacher wants the student to understand why area and perimeter are different. Which question is most effective?",options:["'Can you show me how you measured the outside edge versus the space inside?'","'Look at the answer key to see which is correct.'","'Did you use the right formula?'","'Perimeter is always smaller than area.'"],answer:0,explain:"Asking the student to demonstrate the different measurements creates conceptual distinction through action. Questioning the formula assumes error without understanding. Stating a rule is abstract. Consulting an answer key bypasses reasoning."},{id:"5003-028",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Geometry and Measurement, Data, Statistics, and Probability",q:"Which shape has exactly four sides of equal length and four right angles?",options:["Rhombus","Square","Trapezoid","Rectangle"],answer:1,explain:"A square has four equal sides and four right angles. A rectangle has four right angles but not necessarily equal sides. A rhombus has four equal sides but not necessarily right angles. A trapezoid has only one pair of parallel sides."},{id:"5003-029",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Geometry and Measurement, Data, Statistics, and Probability",q:"What is the probability of rolling an even number on a standard six-sided die?",options:["1/3","2/3","1/2","1/6"],answer:2,explain:"Even numbers on a die are 2, 4, and 6\u2014three outcomes out of six. Probability = 3/6 = 1/2. One-sixth is a single outcome. One-third would be two outcomes. Two-thirds would be four outcomes."},{id:"5003-030",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Geometry and Measurement, Data, Statistics, and Probability",q:"Which statement about the median of a data set is TRUE?",options:["The median is calculated by adding all values and dividing by the count","The median is always one of the values in the data set","The median is affected by extreme values more than the mean","The median divides the data into two equal halves"],answer:3,explain:"The median is the middle value that splits the data so half are above and half below. It is not always a data value (it can be average of two middle values). The median is LESS affected by extremes than the mean. Adding and dividing calculates the mean, not median."},{id:"5004-001",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"United States History, Government, and Citizenship",q:"How many amendments are in the Bill of Rights?",options:["10","27","50","5"],answer:0,explain:"The Bill of Rights comprises the first ten amendments to the Constitution, ratified in 1791. Twenty-seven is the total number of amendments. Five and fifty are not amendment counts."},{id:"5004-002",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"United States History, Government, and Citizenship",q:"Which branch of the federal government has the power to declare war?",options:["All three branches jointly","Legislative","Judicial","Executive"],answer:1,explain:"Article I, Section 8 of the Constitution grants Congress the power to declare war. The executive commands the military but does not formally declare war. The judiciary interprets laws. No single branch declares war independently."},{id:"5004-003",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"United States History, Government, and Citizenship",q:"The Tenth Amendment states that powers not delegated to the federal government are reserved to the states or the people. This principle is best described as:",options:["Judicial review","Popular sovereignty","Federalism","Separation of powers"],answer:2,explain:"Federalism divides power between national and state governments. The Tenth Amendment explicitly reserves non-delegated powers to states or people. Separation of powers divides government among branches. Judicial review allows courts to invalidate laws. Popular sovereignty means power comes from the people."},{id:"5004-004",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"United States History, Government, and Citizenship",q:"Which instructional strategy best helps students understand the purpose of checks and balances?",options:["Assign reading of the Constitution without discussion","Have students memorize each branch's powers","Show a video about the presidential election only","Use role-play where students act as different branches blocking each other's actions"],answer:3,explain:"Role-play creates experiential understanding: students feel how each branch can check another. Memorization without context is abstract. Reading alone does not illuminate function. Videos limited to elections miss the systemic interaction."},{id:"5004-005",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"United States History, Government, and Citizenship",q:"Who was the first President of the United States?",options:["George Washington","Benjamin Franklin","Thomas Jefferson","John Adams"],answer:0,explain:"George Washington served as the first president from 1789 to 1797. Jefferson was the third president. Adams was the second. Franklin was a statesman but never president."},{id:"5004-006",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"United States History, Government, and Citizenship",q:"The Civil War fundamentally changed the relationship between states and the federal government because:",options:["It reduced federal power significantly","It confirmed the supremacy of the federal government over the states","It established that states could nullify federal laws","It gave states the right to secede"],answer:1,explain:"The Union victory established that the federal government is supreme and states cannot secede. Nullification and secession were arguments of the Confederacy, defeated in war. Federal power increased, not decreased, through Reconstruction amendments."},{id:"5004-007",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"United States History, Government, and Citizenship",q:"Students struggle to understand why the Constitution includes theelastic clause. Which explanation is most accurate?",options:["It was designed to make the Constitution longer","It lets the President change the Constitution","It gives Congress implied powers to carry out its enumerated powers","It allows Congress to make any law it wants"],answer:2,explain:"The elastic (necessary and proper) clause permits Congress to pass laws necessary to execute its enumerated powers. It does not grant unlimited power. It does not give the President constitutional authority. Its purpose was functional, not decorative."},{id:"5004-008",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"United States History, Government, and Citizenship",q:"What is the minimum age requirement to become President of the United States?",options:["30 years","25 years","40 years","35 years"],answer:3,explain:"Article II, Section 1 requires the president to be at least 35 years old, a natural-born citizen, and a resident for 14 years. Twenty-five is the age for the House. Thirty is for the Senate. Forty is not a constitutional requirement."},{id:"5004-009",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"United States History, Government, and Citizenship",q:"Which amendment granted women the right to vote?",options:["19th Amendment","26th Amendment","15th Amendment","24th Amendment"],answer:0,explain:"The 19th Amendment (1920) prohibited voting discrimination based on sex. The 15th (1870) addressed race. The 24th (1964) eliminated poll taxes. The 26th (1971) lowered the voting age to 18."},{id:"5004-010",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"United States History, Government, and Citizenship",q:"Marbury v. Madison (1803) established which important principle?",options:["Congress controls the judicial branch","The Supreme Court has the power of judicial review","The President can veto any law","States can override federal law"],answer:1,explain:"Chief Justice John Marshall's decision established that courts can declare laws unconstitutional. This judicial review power is not explicitly in the Constitution but was inferred from the structure. It does not concern presidential veto power, state supremacy, or congressional control of courts."},{id:"5004-011",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"United States History, Government, and Citizenship",q:"A student asks why the Founding Fathers created a republic instead of a direct democracy. Which response is most historically accurate?",options:["Direct democracy was illegal at the time","They preferred monarchy but compromised","They feared direct democracy would lead to mob rule and wanted safeguards","They did not know how to run a direct democracy"],answer:2,explain:"The Founders, influenced by Montesquieu and historical examples, feared pure democracy could become tyrannical majority rule. A republic with representatives and checks was designed to temper passions. They understood direct democracy; they chose to avoid it. There was no legal prohibition, and they rejected monarchy."},{id:"5004-012",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"United States History, Government, and Citizenship",q:"How many justices serve on the Supreme Court?",options:["13","11","7","9"],answer:3,explain:"There are nine Supreme Court justices: one Chief Justice and eight Associate Justices. The number is set by Congress, not the Constitution, and has been nine since 1869. Seven, eleven, and thirteen are incorrect."},{id:"5004-013",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"Geography, Anthropology, and Sociology",q:"How many inhabited continents are commonly recognized in the seven-continent model?",options:["Seven","Six","Five","Eight"],answer:0,explain:"The seven-continent model identifies Africa, Antarctica, Asia, Europe, North America, South America, and Australia. Other cultural models group continents differently, so the question specifies the model and asks about the commonly recognized seven."},{id:"5004-014",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"Geography, Anthropology, and Sociology",q:"Which geographic feature most strongly influenced where early civilizations developed?",options:["Desert locations","River valleys with fertile soil","Mountain ranges","High-altitude plateaus"],answer:1,explain:"Early civilizations (Mesopotamia, Egypt, Indus Valley, China) all developed near rivers that provided water, fertile silt, and transportation. Mountains, deserts, and high plateaus generally posed challenges to large-scale agriculture."},{id:"5004-015",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"Geography, Anthropology, and Sociology",q:"The concept of 'cultural diffusion' best describes which phenomenon?",options:["The biological evolution of human beings","A society intentionally isolating itself","The spread of cultural beliefs and practices from one group to another","A culture losing all its traditions"],answer:2,explain:"Cultural diffusion is the transmission of cultural elements\u2014ideas, styles, religions, technologies\u2014between societies. It does not mean cultural loss, isolation, or biological evolution. Trade routes, migration, and conquest are common diffusion mechanisms."},{id:"5004-016",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"Geography, Anthropology, and Sociology",q:"Which activity best helps students understand the concept of 'human-environment interaction'?",options:["Drawing maps without labels","Listing climate zones by name","Memorizing capital cities","Studying how a local community adapted to or modified its environment"],answer:3,explain:"Examining a local example makes the abstract concept concrete: how people depend on, adapt to, and modify their environment. Capital cities test rote knowledge. Unlabeled maps lack content. Listing climates names categories but does not explore interaction."},{id:"5004-017",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"Geography, Anthropology, and Sociology",q:"What is the imaginary line at 0\xB0 latitude?",options:["Equator","International Date Line","Tropic of Cancer","Prime Meridian"],answer:0,explain:"The Equator is at 0\xB0 latitude and divides the Earth into Northern and Southern hemispheres. The Prime Meridian is at 0\xB0 longitude. The Tropic of Cancer is at approximately 23.5\xB0N. The Date Line is near 180\xB0 longitude."},{id:"5004-018",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"Geography, Anthropology, and Sociology",q:"Which ocean is the largest?",options:["Atlantic Ocean","Pacific Ocean","Indian Ocean","Arctic Ocean"],answer:1,explain:"The Pacific Ocean is the largest, covering more area than all land masses combined. The Atlantic is second. The Indian is third. The Arctic is the smallest."},{id:"5004-019",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"Geography, Anthropology, and Sociology",q:"A sociologist compares how unemployment, housing policy, and policing patterns affect crime rates across many cities. Which level of analysis is being used?",options:["Biological determinism","Micro-level face-to-face interaction","Macro-level structural analysis","Individual psychology"],answer:2,explain:"The study examines large-scale institutions and structural conditions across cities, which is macro-level analysis. Micro-level analysis would focus on direct interactions among individuals."},{id:"5004-020",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"Geography, Anthropology, and Sociology",q:"Students are learning about cultural traditions. Which approach best avoids cultural stereotyping?",options:["Avoiding discussion of cultural differences entirely","Teaching that all members of a culture behave identically","Presenting only famous examples from each culture","Showing cultural traditions as dynamic and variable within groups"],answer:3,explain:"Presenting cultures as diverse and changing counters the harmful assumption of uniformity. Teaching identical behavior reinforces stereotypes. Famous examples may misrepresent ordinary life. Avoiding differences prevents honest understanding."},{id:"5004-021",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"Geography, Anthropology, and Sociology",q:"Which country has the largest population in the world?",options:["India","Indonesia","United States","China"],answer:0,explain:"India surpassed China as the world's most populous country in 2023. The United States is third. Indonesia is fourth. Population figures change, but India currently holds the top position."},{id:"5004-022",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"Geography, Anthropology, and Sociology",q:"The term 'globalization' in social studies best refers to:",options:["The unification of all languages into one","Increasing interconnectedness among countries economically, culturally, and politically","The spread of American culture worldwide","The elimination of all national borders"],answer:1,explain:"Globalization describes growing interdependence through trade, communication, migration, and cultural exchange. It is not limited to American culture (though that is a component). Languages have not unified. National borders persist, though their significance may shift."},{id:"5004-023",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"World History and Economics",q:"Which ancient civilization built the pyramids at Giza?",options:["Mesopotamian","Roman","Egyptian","Greek"],answer:2,explain:"The Egyptian civilization built the Giza pyramids around 2550 BCE as tombs for pharaohs. Romans built aqueducts and coliseums. Greeks built the Parthenon. Mesopotamians built ziggurats."},{id:"5004-024",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"World History and Economics",q:"What is the basic economic question that every society must answer?",options:["How to maximize profit for businesses","How to distribute wealth equally","How to eliminate all scarcity","What to produce, how to produce it, and for whom to produce"],answer:3,explain:"All societies face scarcity and must decide what goods and services to produce, how to produce them, and who receives them. Equality, eliminating scarcity, and maximizing profit are goals or outcomes, not the fundamental questions every society confronts."},{id:"5004-025",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"World History and Economics",q:"The Industrial Revolution's most significant impact on society was:",options:["The shift from agrarian, rural societies to urban, industrial ones","The invention of the printing press","The discovery of the Americas","The development of democratic governments"],answer:0,explain:"Industrialization transformed human settlement patterns, moving populations from farms to cities and creating factory-based economies. The printing press predates industrialization. Discovering the Americas preceded it. Democratic governments developed through political, not purely industrial, change."},{id:"5004-026",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"World History and Economics",q:"Which strategy best helps students understand the concept of 'supply and demand'?",options:["Memorize the definitions of supply and demand","Use a classroom lemonade stand simulation where price changes with customer numbers","Watch a documentary about stock markets","Read a textbook chapter on economics"],answer:1,explain:"Hands-on simulation makes abstract economic principles concrete: students experience how limited supply and high demand raise prices. Definitions alone are abstract. Textbook reading without application is passive. Stock market documentaries are too complex for introductory understanding."},{id:"5004-027",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"World History and Economics",q:"Which event marked the beginning of World War I?",options:["The invasion of Poland","The bombing of Pearl Harbor","The assassination of Archduke Franz Ferdinand","The fall of the Berlin Wall"],answer:2,explain:"The assassination of Archduke Franz Ferdinand of Austria in 1914 triggered the chain of alliances that led to World War I. The invasion of Poland started World War II. Pearl Harbor brought the U.S. into World War II. The Berlin Wall fell in 1989, ending the Cold War."},{id:"5004-028",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"World History and Economics",q:"What type of economy is characterized by government ownership of major industries?",options:["Mixed economy","Traditional economy","Market economy","Command economy"],answer:3,explain:"A command (planned) economy features government control over production, distribution, and pricing. Market economies rely on private ownership and supply-demand. Traditional economies follow customs. Mixed economies combine elements."},{id:"5004-029",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"World History and Economics",q:"The Enlightenment's greatest contribution to modern democracy was the idea that:",options:["Government legitimacy comes from the consent of the governed","Kings receive power from God","Religious authority should guide politics","Only landowners should vote"],answer:0,explain:"Enlightenment thinkers like Locke and Rousseau argued that governments derive power from the people, not divine right. This principle underlies modern democratic theory. Divine right justified monarchy. Religious guidance and property restrictions contradict Enlightenment ideals."},{id:"5004-030",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"World History and Economics",q:"A student confuses the Renaissance with the Reformation. Which comparison most clearly distinguishes them?",options:["'They are the same event with different names.'","'The Renaissance was a cultural revival of classical learning; the Reformation was a religious movement that split Western Christianity.'","'The Renaissance was art; the Reformation was religion.'","'Both happened in the 1800s.'"],answer:1,explain:"The Renaissance centered on revived classical learning, art, and humanism, whereas the Reformation challenged practices and authority within Western Christianity. The distractors either oversimplify the movements or assign them incorrect subjects."},{id:"5004-031",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"Geography, Anthropology, and Sociology",q:"Which ocean borders the east coast of Africa?",options:["Atlantic Ocean","Arctic Ocean","Indian Ocean","Pacific Ocean"],answer:2,explain:"The Indian Ocean lies east of Africa. The Atlantic is west. The Pacific is far to the east, beyond Asia. The Arctic is north of Europe."},{id:"5004-032",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"World History and Economics",q:"The Columbian Exchange is best described as:",options:["The exploration of the Pacific Ocean","A trade route between Europe and Asia","A treaty between Native Americans and Europeans","The transfer of plants, animals, diseases, and people between the Americas and the Old World after 1492"],answer:3,explain:"The Columbian Exchange refers to the widespread transfer of organisms (crops, livestock), diseases (smallpox), and people (including enslaved Africans) between the Eastern and Western Hemispheres after Columbus's voyages. It was not a trade route, treaty, or Pacific exploration."},{id:"5005-001",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Earth Science",q:"What are the three main types of rocks?",options:["Sedimentary, metamorphic, and igneous","Limestone, shale, and conglomerate","Crystalline, amorphous, and composite","Granite, basalt, and sandstone"],answer:0,explain:"The rock cycle classifies rocks into three types: igneous (formed from cooled magma), sedimentary (formed from compacted sediments), and metamorphic (formed by heat and pressure). The other options list specific rock types or incorrect categories."},{id:"5005-002",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Earth Science",q:"Which process is primarily responsible for creating fossil fuels?",options:["Volcanic eruption","Accumulation and burial of organic matter over millions of years","Erosion of mountain ranges","Evaporation of ocean water"],answer:1,explain:"Fossil fuels (coal, oil, natural gas) form from buried organic material subjected to heat and pressure over geologic time. Volcanic activity, erosion, and evaporation are different geologic processes unrelated to fossil fuel formation."},{id:"5005-003",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Earth Science",q:"Students believe that seasons are caused by Earth's distance from the Sun. Which demonstration best corrects this misconception?",options:["Draw a diagram showing a circular orbit","Have students measure temperature at noon only","Show that Earth's axis tilt causes different angles of sunlight on hemispheres","Tell students the belief is wrong"],answer:2,explain:"Demonstrating axial tilt with a globe and light source shows how the tilted axis causes direct vs. angled sunlight, creating seasons. Correcting without evidence reinforces the misconception. Measuring at noon only ignores seasonal variation. A circular orbit diagram does not address the actual cause."},{id:"5005-004",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Earth Science",q:"What causes earthquakes?",options:["Solar radiation","Wind patterns","Ocean tides","Movement of tectonic plates along faults"],answer:3,explain:"Earthquakes result from stress release along faults as tectonic plates move. Wind, tides, and solar radiation do not generate seismic energy. Plate boundaries are the primary locations of earthquake activity."},{id:"5005-005",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Earth Science",q:"Which layer of the Earth is the hottest?",options:["Inner core","Crust","Mantle","Outer core"],answer:0,explain:"The inner core is the hottest layer, at approximately 5,400\xB0C (similar to the Sun's surface). The outer core is hot but cooler than the inner core. The mantle and crust are progressively cooler."},{id:"5005-006",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Earth Science",q:"How does the water cycle move water through the environment?",options:["Rain falls only in mountains","Water evaporates, condenses into clouds, precipitates, and flows back to bodies of water","Water disappears and reappears randomly","Water freezes in oceans and melts on land"],answer:1,explain:"The water cycle involves evaporation (liquid to gas), condensation (gas to liquid in clouds), precipitation (rain/snow), and collection (flow to oceans, lakes, groundwater). The other options describe incomplete or incorrect processes."},{id:"5005-007",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Earth Science",q:"A rock layer contains fossils of tropical plants but is now in an arid desert. What does this most likely indicate?",options:["The fossils are fake","The plants were transported by humans","The climate of that region was different in the past","Deserts are always cold"],answer:2,explain:"Fossil evidence shows that Earth's climates have changed over geologic time. Tropical plant fossils in a desert indicate the area once had a warm, wet climate. This is evidence of climate change, not human transport, forgery, or current desert conditions."},{id:"5005-008",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Earth Science",q:"Which hands-on activity best helps students understand soil composition?",options:["Reading about soil types","Memorizing soil vocabulary","Watching a video about farming","Performing a soil separation lab using layers of soil in a jar of water"],answer:3,explain:"A soil jar lab lets students observe sediment layers settling by size (sand, silt, clay, organic matter), making composition visible and measurable. Reading and videos are passive. Memorization does not build observation skills."},{id:"5005-009",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Earth Science",q:"What is the Mohs hardness scale used for?",options:["Rating the scratch resistance of minerals","Determining rock age","Classifying fossil types","Measuring earthquake magnitude"],answer:0,explain:"The Mohs scale ranks minerals 1\u201310 by hardness, indicating which can scratch another. Talc is 1 (softest); diamond is 10 (hardest). It does not measure earthquakes (Richter/Moment magnitude), determine age (radiometric dating), or classify fossils."},{id:"5005-010",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Earth Science",q:"Which statement about the rock cycle is true?",options:["Metamorphic rocks cannot become sedimentary","Any rock type can transform into any other rock type given the right conditions","Rocks can only change from igneous to sedimentary","The rock cycle only occurs underground"],answer:1,explain:"The rock cycle shows that igneous, sedimentary, and metamorphic rocks can transform into one another through processes like melting, weathering, compaction, and metamorphism. The other options impose artificial restrictions not supported by geologic evidence."},{id:"5005-011",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Life Science",q:"What is the basic unit of structure and function in living organisms?",options:["Atom","Tissue","Cell","Organ"],answer:2,explain:"The cell is the smallest unit that can perform all life functions. Atoms are smaller but not alive. Tissues and organs are made of cells but are higher levels of organization."},{id:"5005-012",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Life Science",q:"Which organelle is responsible for producing energy in the cell?",options:["Vacuole","Nucleus","Ribosome","Mitochondria"],answer:3,explain:"Mitochondria are the 'powerhouses' that produce ATP through cellular respiration. The nucleus stores DNA. Ribosomes make proteins. Vacuoles store materials."},{id:"5005-013",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Life Science",q:"A student observes that a plant grows toward a window. This is an example of:",options:["Phototropism","Photosynthesis","Respiration","Transpiration"],answer:0,explain:"Phototropism is growth toward light. Photosynthesis uses light to make food. Respiration releases energy. Transpiration is water loss. The directional growth response is specifically tropism."},{id:"5005-014",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Life Science",q:"Which model best represents the relationship between producers, consumers, and decomposers in an ecosystem?",options:["A diagram of a single plant","A food web showing energy transfer among organisms and decomposers connected to remains and wastes from multiple trophic levels","A weather chart","A list of animals arranged alphabetically"],answer:1,explain:"A food web shows feeding relationships, while links from organisms and wastes to decomposers show nutrient recycling across trophic levels. A simple chain placing decomposers only at the end misrepresents their role."},{id:"5005-015",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Life Science",q:"What process do plants use to make their own food?",options:["Digestion","Fermentation","Photosynthesis","Respiration"],answer:2,explain:"Photosynthesis converts sunlight, water, and carbon dioxide into glucose and oxygen. Respiration releases energy from food. Fermentation is anaerobic breakdown. Digestion breaks down food in animals."},{id:"5005-016",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Life Science",q:"Why are decomposers essential to ecosystems?",options:["They create new species","They eat predators","They produce oxygen","They recycle nutrients from dead organisms back into the soil"],answer:3,explain:"Decomposers break down dead matter, returning nutrients to soil for producers to use. They do not primarily produce oxygen, eat predators, or create species. Nutrient cycling is their key ecological role."},{id:"5005-017",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Life Science",q:"Students think that all bacteria are harmful. Which teaching approach best corrects this misconception?",options:["Show examples of beneficial bacteria in yogurt, human gut, and nitrogen fixation","Skip teaching about bacteria entirely","Tell students bacteria are mostly harmless","Have students read about disease-causing bacteria only"],answer:0,explain:"Providing concrete examples of beneficial bacteria counters the 'all bacteria are bad' misconception with evidence. Simply telling students is less effective than showing real examples. Teaching only harmful bacteria reinforces the misconception. Avoiding the topic loses learning."},{id:"5005-018",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Life Science",q:"Which structure is found in plant cells but NOT in animal cells?",options:["Mitochondria","Cell wall","Cell membrane","Nucleus"],answer:1,explain:"Plant cells have rigid cell walls made of cellulose; animal cells do not. Both have nuclei, cell membranes, and mitochondria. The cell wall provides structural support unique to plants."},{id:"5005-019",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Life Science",q:"What is the function of the root system in a plant?",options:["Photosynthesis","Producing seeds","Absorbing water and minerals from soil","Releasing oxygen"],answer:2,explain:"Roots anchor the plant and absorb water and dissolved minerals from soil. Photosynthesis occurs in leaves. Oxygen is released by leaves. Seeds are produced in flowers/fruits."},{id:"5005-020",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Life Science",q:"A population of beetles has both green and brown individuals. Birds eat more green beetles on brown soil. This is an example of:",options:["Habitat destruction","Genetic engineering","Artificial selection","Natural selection"],answer:3,explain:"Natural selection favors traits that improve survival in a given environment. Brown beetles are better camouflaged on brown soil, so they survive and reproduce more. Artificial selection involves human choice. Genetic engineering involves direct DNA manipulation. Habitat destruction is unrelated."},{id:"5005-021",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Physical Science",q:"What is the formula for speed?",options:["Speed = distance \xF7 time","Speed = force \xD7 distance","Speed = mass \xD7 velocity","Speed = energy \xF7 time"],answer:0,explain:"Speed measures how fast something moves: distance traveled divided by time taken. Mass \xD7 velocity is momentum. Force \xD7 distance is work. Energy \xF7 time is power."},{id:"5005-022",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Physical Science",q:"When ice melts into water, what happens to the energy and arrangement of molecules?",options:["Energy decreases; molecules lock into a rigid lattice","Energy is absorbed; the open ice lattice breaks down and molecules move more freely","Energy is destroyed; molecules stop moving","Energy stays the same; molecules form a more ordered crystal"],answer:1,explain:"Melting absorbs energy and disrupts the ordered hydrogen-bonded lattice of ice, allowing molecules to move more freely. Because ordinary ice has an open lattice, liquid water is denser near its melting point; it is inaccurate to say the molecules simply move farther apart."},{id:"5005-023",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Physical Science",q:"Students believe that heavier objects fall faster than lighter objects. Which demonstration best challenges this misconception?",options:["Drop a heavy book and a light paper simultaneously; the book hits first","Tell students Galileo proved they fall at the same rate","Drop a crumpled paper and a book together\u2014they land at the same time","Show a feather falling slowly in air"],answer:2,explain:"Crumpling paper eliminates air resistance effect, showing that mass does not affect fall speed. The book-paper demonstration reinforces the misconception. A feather highlights air resistance, not the principle. Telling is less effective than experiencing."},{id:"5005-024",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Physical Science",q:"What is the chemical formula for water?",options:["CO\u2082","O\u2082","NaCl","H\u2082O"],answer:3,explain:"Water is H\u2082O: two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom. CO\u2082 is carbon dioxide. O\u2082 is oxygen gas. NaCl is table salt."},{id:"5005-025",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Physical Science",q:"Which force pulls objects toward Earth's center?",options:["Gravity","Magnetism","Tension","Friction"],answer:0,explain:"Gravity is the attractive force between masses. Earth's gravity pulls objects toward its center. Magnetism affects magnetic materials. Friction opposes motion. Tension is force in a rope or cable."},{id:"5005-026",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Physical Science",q:"A ball rolls down a hill and slows to a stop. What energy transformation occurred?",options:["Nuclear energy converted to sound","Kinetic energy converted to thermal energy through friction","Chemical energy converted to potential energy","Potential energy converted to kinetic energy only"],answer:1,explain:"As the ball rolls, friction between the ball and ground converts kinetic energy into thermal energy (heat). The ball's motion energy decreases as heat increases. Potential-to-kinetic describes the downhill motion, not the stopping."},{id:"5005-027",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Physical Science",q:"Which assessment best measures a student's understanding of conservation of energy?",options:["Fill in the blanks: 'Energy can ___ but not be ___ or ___'","Multiple-choice question: 'What is the law of conservation of energy?'","Have students design and explain an experiment showing energy transforms but is not created or destroyed","List three forms of energy"],answer:2,explain:"Designing and explaining an experiment requires applying the concept, demonstrating deep understanding. Definition recall tests memory. Fill-in-the-blank is recognition. Listing forms does not show understanding of conservation."},{id:"5005-028",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Physical Science",q:"What type of energy is stored in a battery?",options:["Thermal energy","Kinetic energy","Nuclear energy","Chemical energy"],answer:3,explain:"Batteries store chemical energy that converts to electrical energy when the circuit is closed. Kinetic energy is motion energy. Thermal energy is heat. Nuclear energy comes from atomic nuclei."},{id:"5005-029",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Physical Science",q:"What happens to most materials when they are heated?",options:["They expand","They contract","They disappear","They freeze"],answer:0,explain:"Most materials expand when heated because particles move faster and spread apart. Contraction occurs when cooling. Freezing is a phase change to solid. Heating does not make materials disappear."},{id:"5005-030",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Physical Science",q:"A student mixes baking soda and vinegar in a sealed bag. The bag inflates. Which explanation is correct?",options:["The bag was defective and had a hole","A chemical reaction produced carbon dioxide gas","Heat from the mixture expanded the air inside","The bag stretched from the liquid"],answer:1,explain:"Baking soda (sodium bicarbonate) and vinegar (acetic acid) react to produce carbon dioxide gas, which inflates the sealed bag. The liquid does not stretch the bag. Heat is minimal. A hole would deflate, not inflate."},{id:"5002-031",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Reading",q:"What is the main idea of a passage?",options:["The title only","The first sentence","The most important point the author wants readers to understand","A detailed supporting fact"],answer:2,explain:"The main idea is the central, most important point\u2014not necessarily the first sentence, a detail, or the title alone. It encompasses what the passage is primarily about."},{id:"5002-032",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Reading",q:"In a persuasive essay, which element is essential to establish credibility?",options:["Making exaggerated claims","Ignoring opposing viewpoints","Using emotional language exclusively","Appealing to ethos through reliable evidence and author expertise"],answer:3,explain:"Ethos (credibility) comes from demonstrating expertise, using reliable sources, and showing fairness. Pure emotion without logic or credibility is weak persuasion. Exaggeration undermines trust."},{id:"5002-033",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Reading",q:"Which approach best helps English learners access grade-level text?",options:["Provide visual supports, pre-teach key vocabulary, and use graphic organizers","Have them read silently without help","Skip reading instruction for English learners","Use only simplified texts"],answer:0,explain:"Scaffolding with visuals, vocabulary preview, and organizers maintains access to grade-level content while building language. Simplified texts widen achievement gaps. Silent reading without support leaves gaps invisible."},{id:"5002-034",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Reading",q:"What is the function of a topic sentence?",options:["To end a paragraph","To state the main point of a paragraph","To list sources","To describe the setting"],answer:1,explain:"A topic sentence states the paragraph's main idea, usually in the first or near-first position. It guides what details should follow."},{id:"5002-035",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Reading",q:"Which type of text organization presents events in the order they happened?",options:["Problem and solution","Compare and contrast","Chronological order","Cause and effect"],answer:2,explain:"Chronological organization sequences events by time. Cause-and-effect shows why things happen. Compare-contrast examines similarities and differences."},{id:"5002-036",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Reading",q:"An author writes: 'The factory's smokestacks, like gray sentinels, dominated the skyline.' What is the effect of this figurative language?",options:["It makes the factories seem friendly","It describes the color blue","It compares factories to soldiers literally","It personifies the smokestacks as watchful, imposing figures"],answer:3,explain:"The simile compares smokestacks to sentinels, giving them human-like vigilance and imposing presence. This creates an atmospheric, somewhat ominous tone."},{id:"5002-037",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Reading",q:"Students are learning to summarize. Which guidance is most effective?",options:["Identify the most important ideas and leave out details and examples","Write everything in fewer words","Copy the first sentence of each paragraph","Make it as short as possible regardless of content"],answer:0,explain:"Summarizing requires identifying key ideas and omitting minor details. Reducing everything word-for-word preserves too much. Copying topic sentences misses development. Brevity without selectivity loses meaning."},{id:"5002-038",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Reading",q:"What is the difference between fiction and nonfiction?",options:["Fiction is longer; nonfiction is shorter","Fiction is made up; nonfiction is based on facts","Fiction has pictures; nonfiction does not","There is no difference"],answer:1,explain:"Fiction involves imagined stories and characters. Nonfiction presents factual information about the real world. Length, illustrations, and difficulty do not define the distinction."},{id:"5002-039",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Reading",q:"A reader encounters an unfamiliar word 'photosynthesis' in a science text. Which strategy best helps determine its meaning?",options:["Memorize the dictionary definition without context","Ask someone to tell them the answer","Use context clues from surrounding sentences and break the word into parts","Skip the word and continue reading"],answer:2,explain:"Context clues and morphological analysis (photo = light, synthesis = putting together) build independent word-learning skills. Skipping avoids learning. Rote memorization without context is fragile. Asking for answers does not build strategy."},{id:"5002-040",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Reading",q:"Which discussion protocol best develops students' ability to cite textual evidence?",options:["Group work with no accountability","Open discussion with no structure","Teacher-led lecture about the text","Socratic seminar where students must quote or reference specific passages"],answer:3,explain:"Socratic seminars require students to ground claims in text, building evidence-based reasoning. Unstructured discussion may drift. Lectures model but do not practice. Unaccountable group work allows avoidance."},{id:"5002-041",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Reading",q:"What is a metaphor?",options:["A direct comparison without using 'like' or 'as'","Repetition of sounds","An exaggeration","A question asked for effect"],answer:0,explain:"A metaphor directly equates two unlike things (e.g., 'time is money'). Hyperbole is exaggeration. Alliteration is sound repetition. Rhetorical questions ask without expecting answers."},{id:"5002-042",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Reading",q:"Why might an author use irony in a story?",options:["To confuse readers intentionally","To highlight the gap between expectation and reality","To avoid stating themes directly","To make the story longer"],answer:1,explain:"Irony creates contrast between what is expected and what actually occurs, often revealing deeper truths. It is a deliberate literary device, not confusion, padding, or avoidance."},{id:"5002-043",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Reading",q:"A student consistently misreads 'thought' as 'through.' Which intervention is most appropriate?",options:["Tell the student to slow down","Have the student copy the word twenty times","Teach the digraph 'ough' and its multiple pronunciations through explicit phonics instruction","Remove the word from future readings"],answer:2,explain:"The 'ough' digraph has multiple pronunciations in English (thought, through, though, tough). Explicit instruction on this pattern builds decoding flexibility. Copying is drill without understanding. Telling to slow down does not address the root issue."},{id:"5002-044",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Reading",q:"What is the purpose of a conclusion in an informational text?",options:["To repeat the introduction word for word","To introduce new information","To list unrelated facts","To summarize key points and provide closure"],answer:3,explain:"Conclusions reinforce main ideas and help readers remember key takeaways. They should not introduce new information, list irrelevant facts, or simply copy the introduction."},{id:"5002-045",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Reading",q:"Which punctuation mark indicates possession?",options:["Apostrophe","Comma","Period","Semicolon"],answer:0,explain:"An apostrophe shows possession (e.g., Sarah's book, the dogs' bones). Commas separate clauses. Periods end sentences. Semicolons join related clauses."},{id:"5002-046",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Reading",q:"A passage argues that school uniforms reduce distractions. Which evidence would best support this claim?",options:["A teacher's opinion that uniforms are nice","Research data showing decreased disciplinary incidents after uniform adoption","A photo of students in uniforms","A statement that uniforms cost money"],answer:1,explain:"Empirical data on disciplinary incidents provides evidence for a causal claim. Opinions, cost statements, and photos do not support the specific argument about distractions."},{id:"5002-047",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Reading",q:"Which assessment best measures inferential comprehension?",options:["Asking students to spell vocabulary words","Asking students to identify the author","Asking students to explain what a character feels based on actions described","Asking students to recall a fact stated directly in the text"],answer:2,explain:"Inference requires reading between the lines\u2014using clues to understand unstated meanings. Direct recall tests memory. Spelling and author identification test different skills."},{id:"5002-048",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Reading",q:"What is the role of dialogue in a narrative?",options:["To list facts about the setting","To replace narration entirely","To confuse the reader","To reveal character and advance the plot"],answer:3,explain:"Dialogue shows personality, relationships, and conflict while moving the story forward. It supplements rather than replaces narration. It does not list setting facts or aim to confuse."},{id:"5002-049",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Reading",q:"Which signal word indicates cause and effect?",options:["Because","For example","However","Similarly"],answer:0,explain:"'Because' signals causation\u2014explaining why something happens. 'However' shows contrast. 'Similarly' shows comparison. 'For example' introduces illustration."},{id:"5002-050",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Reading",q:"A poem uses enjambment repeatedly. What effect does this create?",options:["Equal length stanzas","A flowing, uninterrupted momentum that carries meaning across line breaks","A rhyming pattern at line ends","Clear pauses at the end of every line"],answer:1,explain:"Enjambment runs a sentence past the line break without pause, creating forward motion and encouraging readers to push through. It contrasts with end-stopped lines. It does not create rhyme or equal stanzas."},{id:"5002-051",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Reading",q:"Students need to compare two characters from different stories. Which organizer is most effective?",options:["A KWL chart","A word search","A Venn diagram with character traits in each section","A timeline of events"],answer:2,explain:"A Venn diagram visually shows similarities (overlap) and differences (unique sections), making comparison concrete. KWL charts track prior knowledge and questions. Timelines show sequence. Word searches are games."},{id:"5002-052",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Reading",q:"What is the denotation of a word?",options:["The rhyme scheme","The author's biography","The emotional association","The literal dictionary definition"],answer:3,explain:"Denotation is the literal, objective meaning found in dictionaries. Connotation is the emotional or cultural association. Denotation is not rhyme or biography."},{id:"5002-053",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Reading",q:"Why is point of view important in a narrative?",options:["It shapes what information the reader receives and how they interpret events","It determines the book's price","It changes the genre","It affects the font size"],answer:0,explain:"Point of view controls the narrator's access to thoughts and information, influencing reader sympathy, understanding, and interpretation. It is a fundamental storytelling choice, not related to physical attributes or genre classification."},{id:"5002-054",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Reading",q:"Which strategy best supports students who struggle with text cohesion?",options:["Assign longer texts immediately","Teach them to identify transition words and track referents (who/what 'they' refers to)","Have them read faster","Skip cohesive devices"],answer:1,explain:"Cohesion depends on transitions and clear reference. Teaching students to track these explicitly builds comprehension of how ideas connect. Reading faster worsens the problem. Skipping devices ignores the skill. Longer texts overwhelm struggling readers."},{id:"5002-055",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Reading",q:"What is the purpose of a diagram in an informational text?",options:["To replace the text entirely","To decorate the page","To visually represent information that is hard to convey in words alone","To waste space"],answer:2,explain:"Diagrams clarify complex relationships, processes, or structures visually. They complement text, not replace it. They serve function, not decoration or waste."},{id:"5002-056",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Writing, Speaking, and Listening",q:"What is the purpose of a thesis statement?",options:["To list all sources","To entertain the reader","To describe the format","To state the main argument of an essay"],answer:3,explain:"A thesis statement presents the central claim or argument that the essay will support. It is not for entertainment, source listing, or format description."},{id:"5002-057",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Writing, Speaking, and Listening",q:"Which sentence is written in the passive voice?",options:["The papers were graded by the teacher.","The teacher graded the papers.","The teacher is grading papers.","The teacher will grade papers."],answer:0,explain:"'The papers were graded by the teacher' is passive because the subject receives the action. Each distractor makes the teacher the actor and is therefore active voice."},{id:"5002-058",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Writing, Speaking, and Listening",q:"A student's writing lacks transitions between paragraphs. Which feedback is most useful?",options:["'Add more transitions' written in red","Highlight pairs of paragraphs and ask the student to identify the logical relationship and choose an appropriate transition","Ignore transitions and focus only on content","Rewrite the essay for the student"],answer:1,explain:"Guiding the student to analyze relationships and choose transitions builds the skill. Vague feedback, rewriting for them, or ignoring the issue does not teach the strategy."},{id:"5002-059",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Writing, Speaking, and Listening",q:"Which revision best improves the sentence: 'The research was done by us and the results were good'?",options:["'We did the research and the results were good.'","The original is already correct","'Our research yielded positive results.'","'The research that we did produced good results.'"],answer:2,explain:"'Our research yielded positive results' is concise, formal, and active. 'We did the research and the results were good' is grammatical but less precise, while the other alternatives remain awkward or vague."},{id:"5002-060",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Writing, Speaking, and Listening",q:"What is a compound sentence?",options:["A fragment","A sentence with one independent clause","A sentence with a dependent clause","Two independent clauses joined by a coordinating conjunction"],answer:3,explain:"A compound sentence joins two complete thoughts with a conjunction like 'and,' 'but,' or 'so.' A simple sentence has one clause. A complex sentence has an independent and dependent clause."},{id:"5002-061",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Writing, Speaking, and Listening",q:"Which modeling technique best shows students how to write a strong topic sentence?",options:["Think aloud while drafting a topic sentence, explaining the reasoning process","Show several examples and have students copy them","Give students a fill-in-the-blank template only","Assign a topic sentence worksheet"],answer:0,explain:"Think-aloud modeling makes the cognitive process visible. Copying does not teach reasoning. Templates are helpful but insufficient alone. Worksheets practice without modeling."},{id:"5002-062",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Writing, Speaking, and Listening",q:"Which sentence demonstrates correct parallel structure?",options:["She likes hiking, swim, and bike.","She likes hiking, swimming, and biking.","She likes hiking, to swim, and biking.","She likes to hike, swimming, and to bike."],answer:1,explain:"All items in the list use the gerund form (-ing), creating parallel structure. The other options mix infinitives and gerunds, breaking parallelism."},{id:"5002-063",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Writing, Speaking, and Listening",q:"What is the difference between a clause and a phrase?",options:["A clause cannot stand alone","A phrase is longer than a clause","A clause has a subject and verb; a phrase does not","They are the same thing"],answer:2,explain:"A clause contains both a subject and a verb. A phrase is a group of words without both. Some clauses are independent (can stand alone); some are dependent."},{id:"5002-064",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Writing, Speaking, and Listening",q:"Which ending is appropriate for a formal email to a teacher?",options:["Peace!","XOXO","Later gator!","Best wishes,"],answer:3,explain:"'Best wishes' is professional and appropriate for formal academic communication. The others are casual or inappropriate for the context."},{id:"5002-065",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Writing, Speaking, and Listening",q:"A student writes a persuasive piece that presents only one side of an issue. How should the teacher respond?",options:["Ask the student to consider the opposing view and address it fairly","Rewrite the opposing view for the student","Tell the student the essay is incomplete and cannot be graded","Praise the writing and ignore the missing counterargument"],answer:0,explain:"Addressing counterarguments strengthens persuasion and teaches intellectual honesty. Ignoring the issue misses a teaching moment. Rejecting the essay entirely is punitive. Rewriting deprives the student of the learning opportunity."},{id:"5002-066",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Writing, Speaking, and Listening",q:"What is the correct use of a semicolon?",options:["To separate items in a list","To join two closely related independent clauses","To replace a period","To begin a sentence"],answer:1,explain:"Semicolons connect independent clauses that are closely related in thought. Commas separate list items. Semicolons cannot begin sentences. They complement rather than replace periods."},{id:"5002-067",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Writing, Speaking, and Listening",q:"Which word is an adjective?",options:["Quickly","Run","Beautiful","Happyful"],answer:2,explain:"'Beautiful' describes a noun (a beautiful day). 'Quickly' is an adverb. 'Run' is a verb. 'Happyful' is not a real word."},{id:"5002-068",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Writing, Speaking, and Listening",q:"Which revision clearly indicates that Mike made the mistake in 'Tom told Mike that he made a mistake'?",options:["Tom told Mike, 'You made a mistake.'","Tom told Mike that Tom made a mistake.","Tom told Mike that he had made a mistake.","Tom said that a mistake had been made."],answer:0,explain:"Directly quoting Tom as saying 'You made a mistake' identifies Mike as the person who erred. Naming Tom instead changes the meaning, and the pronoun 'he' remains ambiguous."},{id:"5002-069",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Writing, Speaking, and Listening",q:"Which peer review structure maximizes constructive feedback?",options:["Students use a rubric with specific criteria to give feedback on content, organization, and conventions","Students only compliment each other","Students read each other's papers silently","Students swap papers and circle errors"],answer:0,explain:"Rubric-based peer review focuses feedback on specific, actionable criteria. Error-circling is reductionist. Silent reading provides no feedback. Compliments alone avoid critical analysis."},{id:"5002-070",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Writing, Speaking, and Listening",q:"What is an abstract noun?",options:["A noun that can be touched","A noun that names an idea, quality, or state","A noun that is always capitalized","A noun that has no plural form"],answer:1,explain:"Abstract nouns name ideas (freedom), qualities (bravery), or states (happiness) that cannot be perceived by the five senses. Concrete nouns name tangible things."},{id:"5002-071",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Writing, Speaking, and Listening",q:"Which punctuation ends a declarative sentence?",options:["Exclamation point","Comma","Period","Question mark"],answer:2,explain:"Declarative sentences (statements) end with periods. Question marks end questions. Exclamation points end strong commands or exclamations. Commas separate elements within sentences."},{id:"5002-072",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Writing, Speaking, and Listening",q:"Which sentence demonstrates correct use of the subjunctive mood?",options:["If I was rich, I would travel.","If I am rich, I traveled.","If I be rich, I will travel.","If I were rich, I would travel."],answer:3,explain:"'If I were rich, I would travel' correctly uses subjunctive were for a hypothetical condition. The alternatives either use the indicative was or pair incompatible verb forms."},{id:"5002-073",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Writing, Speaking, and Listening",q:"Students need to learn to vary sentence openings. Which activity is most effective?",options:["Rewriting identical sentences with different openings (prepositional phrase, participle, adverbial clause)","Memorizing sentence types","Writing only simple sentences","Counting words in each sentence"],answer:0,explain:"Practicing varied openings builds syntactic flexibility. Memorization without application does not improve writing. Word counting and simple-sentence-only practices do not develop variety."},{id:"5002-074",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Writing, Speaking, and Listening",q:"What is an antecedent?",options:["A type of verb","The noun a pronoun refers to","A word that comes after another","A punctuation mark"],answer:1,explain:"An antecedent is the noun that a pronoun replaces or refers to (e.g., in 'Maria lost her book,' 'Maria' is the antecedent of 'her')."},{id:"5002-075",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Writing, Speaking, and Listening",q:"Which phrase is an example of a dangling modifier?",options:["After finishing the race, she collapsed.","She opened the door carefully.","Running quickly, the finish line came into view.","The dog wagged its tail happily."],answer:2,explain:"In 'Running quickly, the finish line came into view,' the opening phrase should modify a runner, but the grammatical subject is 'the finish line.' The other sentences attach their modifiers to a logical subject."},{id:"5002-076",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Writing, Speaking, and Listening",q:"A multilingual writer uses correct grammar but writes in a style that seems 'formal' to native speakers. How should a teacher respond?",options:["Praise it without any feedback","Correct every sentence to sound more casual","Reject the paper for being too formal","Recognize that formal register may reflect the writer's linguistic background and focus feedback on audience awareness rather than eliminating the style"],answer:3,explain:"Formality varies across languages and cultures. Feedback should help students adjust register for audience without devaluing their linguistic identity. Demanding casualness or rejecting formal style is inappropriate."},{id:"5002-077",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Writing, Speaking, and Listening",q:"What is the function of an interrupter?",options:["To add extra information within a sentence, usually set off by commas","To start a new paragraph","To replace the subject","To end a sentence"],answer:0,explain:"Interrupters (e.g., 'however,' 'that is,' 'in my opinion') insert additional comment within a sentence and are typically enclosed in commas, dashes, or parentheses."},{id:"5002-078",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Writing, Speaking, and Listening",q:"Which is a proper use of capitalization?",options:["i love mathematics","I love mathematics","I Love Mathematics","I love Mathematics"],answer:1,explain:"The pronoun 'I' is always capitalized. Subject names are not capitalized unless they are proper nouns. 'Mathematics' as a school subject is not a proper noun and is not capitalized."},{id:"5002-079",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Writing, Speaking, and Listening",q:"Which revision best fixes the faulty parallelism: 'The class enjoyed reading, to write, and painting'?",options:["The class enjoyed read, write, and paint.","The class enjoyed to read, to write, and to paint.","The class enjoyed reading, writing, and painting.","The class enjoyed reading, writing, and to paint."],answer:2,explain:"'Reading, writing, and painting' uses three gerunds and is fully parallel. The infinitive version is also parallel in isolation, so the best answer is the gerund sequence because it follows naturally after 'enjoyed'; the mixed-form distractors are not parallel."},{id:"5002-080",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Writing, Speaking, and Listening",q:"Which strategy best helps students edit for consistency in tense?",options:["Tell them to check tenses","Ignore tense consistency","Use spell check only","Have them read aloud and mark each verb, then check whether tense shifts are intentional"],answer:3,explain:"Reading aloud and isolating verbs makes tense shifts visible and deliberate. Telling students to 'check' is vague. Spell check does not catch tense errors. Ignoring the issue leaves problems unaddressed."},{id:"5002-081",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Writing, Speaking, and Listening",q:"What is an interjection?",options:["A word expressing sudden emotion, often followed by an exclamation point","A preposition","A type of noun","A linking verb"],answer:0,explain:"Interjections (e.g., 'Wow!', 'Ouch!') express emotion and are often set off by exclamation points. They are not nouns, verbs, or prepositions."},{id:"5002-082",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Writing, Speaking, and Listening",q:"Why is active voice generally preferred in academic writing?",options:["It is always grammatically correct","It is clearer and more direct about who performs the action","It uses fewer words always","Passive voice is never acceptable"],answer:1,explain:"Active voice identifies the actor explicitly, making writing clearer. Passive voice is sometimes appropriate (when the actor is unknown or unimportant). Active voice is not always shorter, nor is passive never acceptable."},{id:"5002-083",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Writing, Speaking, and Listening",q:"A student's writing is verbose with unnecessary words. Which intervention is most effective?",options:["Tell the student to write shorter","Underline every word and make the student delete half","Model conciseness by rewriting a paragraph together, discussing what can be removed","Ignore wordiness"],answer:2,explain:"Collaborative revision teaches the skill of identifying and removing redundancy. Random deletion is arbitrary. Telling students to be shorter does not teach how. Ignoring wordiness leaves the habit unaddressed."},{id:"5002-084",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Writing, Speaking, and Listening",q:"What is a colophon?",options:["A type of paragraph","A character in a novel","A method of citation","A brief statement about the publication of a book or document"],answer:3,explain:"A colophon is a statement at the end of a book about its production (printer, date, place). It is not a paragraph type, character, or citation method."},{id:"5002-085",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Writing, Speaking, and Listening",q:"Which pair of words are homophones?",options:["Their and there","Happy and joyful","Fast and quick","Big and large"],answer:0,explain:"Homophones sound the same but have different spellings and meanings. 'Their' and 'there' are homophones. The other pairs are synonyms, not homophones."},{id:"5003-031",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Numbers and Operations",q:"What is the product of 7 and 8?",options:["48","56","64","15"],answer:1,explain:"Product means multiplication: 7 \xD7 8 = 56. Fifteen is the sum. Forty-eight and sixty-four are products of other number pairs."},{id:"5003-032",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Numbers and Operations",q:"Why does dividing by a fraction less than 1 produce a larger result?",options:["Fractions make numbers smaller","Division always increases numbers","Dividing by a fraction is equivalent to multiplying by its reciprocal, which is greater than 1","The operation is incorrect"],answer:2,explain:"Dividing by 1/2 equals multiplying by 2. In general, dividing by a/b equals multiplying by b/a. When the divisor is less than 1, its reciprocal is greater than 1, producing a larger result."},{id:"5003-033",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Numbers and Operations",q:"Students think that 0.12 is larger than 0.5 because 12 > 5. Which representation best helps them see the error?",options:["Tell them they are wrong","Have them convert to fractions without explanation","Ask them to count the digits","Show a number line with both numbers plotted"],answer:3,explain:"A number line visually shows 0.5 is halfway while 0.12 is near zero, making the comparison concrete. Telling is ineffective. Fraction conversion without support may not address the misconception. Counting digits reinforces the error."},{id:"5003-034",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Numbers and Operations",q:"Which is the least positive number divisible by both 3 and 4?",options:["12","24","18","20"],answer:0,explain:"A number divisible by both 3 and 4 must be a multiple of their least common multiple, 12. Although 24 is also divisible by both, 12 is the least positive such number."},{id:"5003-035",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Numbers and Operations",q:"What is the value of the digit 5 in 3,527?",options:["5","500","5,000","50"],answer:1,explain:"The digit 5 is in the hundreds place, so its value is 500. Place value determines digit value based on position."},{id:"5003-036",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Numbers and Operations",q:"Which statement about integers is true?",options:["All integers are positive","Integers include fractions","Integers include negative numbers, zero, and positive whole numbers","Integers are only even numbers"],answer:2,explain:"Integers are {..., -3, -2, -1, 0, 1, 2, 3, ...}\u2014negative, zero, and positive whole numbers. They exclude fractions and decimals. Not all integers are positive or even."},{id:"5003-037",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Numbers and Operations",q:"Which tool best helps students understand the relationship between addition and subtraction?",options:["Memory games only","Drill worksheets","Flashcards with facts","A fact family triangle showing how three numbers relate through both operations"],answer:3,explain:"Fact families show that 3 + 4 = 7, 4 + 3 = 7, 7 - 3 = 4, and 7 - 4 = 3, revealing the inverse relationship. Flashcards and drills practice memory but do not explain relationships."},{id:"5003-038",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Numbers and Operations",q:"What is 1/2 expressed as a decimal?",options:["0.5","1.2","0.12","0.2"],answer:0,explain:"One-half equals 0.5. Dividing 1 by 2 gives 0.5. The other options misplace the decimal or confuse the fraction."},{id:"5003-039",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Numbers and Operations",q:"A bag contains 3 red marbles and 5 blue marbles. What is the probability of selecting a red marble?",options:["3/5","3/8","8/3","5/8"],answer:1,explain:"Probability = favorable outcomes \xF7 total outcomes = 3 \xF7 8 = 3/8. Three is the number of red marbles; eight is the total (3 + 5)."},{id:"5003-040",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Numbers and Operations",q:"Why is 0.999... (repeating) equal to 1?",options:["It depends on the calculator","It is false; they are different","The difference between 0.999... and 1 is zero; they are the same number expressed differently","It is an approximation"],answer:2,explain:"The infinite decimal 0.999... equals 1 by definition of limits in real numbers. The difference is 0. This is a theorem, not an approximation or calculator artifact."},{id:"5004-033",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"United States History, Government, and Citizenship",q:"What are the three branches of the U.S. federal government?",options:["Federal, State, Local","Army, Navy, Air Force","President, Congress, Supreme Court only","Executive, Legislative, Judicial"],answer:3,explain:"The Constitution separates federal power among the legislative branch, which makes laws; the executive branch, which enforces laws; and the judicial branch, which interprets laws. The distractors omit a branch or confuse branches with particular institutions."},{id:"5004-034",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"United States History, Government, and Citizenship",q:"How many years does a U.S. Senator serve?",options:["6 years","8 years","2 years","4 years"],answer:0,explain:"Senators serve six-year terms, with one-third of the Senate up for election every two years. House members serve two years. The President serves four years."},{id:"5004-035",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"United States History, Government, and Citizenship",q:"The Necessary and Proper Clause is also known as the:",options:["Commerce Clause","Elastic Clause","Due Process Clause","Supremacy Clause"],answer:1,explain:"Article I, Section 8 gives Congress the power to make laws 'necessary and proper' for executing its enumerated powers. This clause is flexible enough to stretch (elastic) to cover implied powers."},{id:"5004-036",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"United States History, Government, and Citizenship",q:"Which activity helps students understand the amendment process?",options:["Memorizing all amendments","Reading about amendments only","Simulating the ratification process by having students propose, debate, and vote on a class amendment","Listing amendments in order"],answer:2,explain:"Simulating the process gives students experiential understanding of proposal and ratification requirements. Memorization and listing do not convey procedure. Reading alone is passive."},{id:"5004-037",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"United States History, Government, and Citizenship",q:"What is the purpose of the Preamble to the Constitution?",options:["To list the amendments","To establish the electoral college","To define treason","To state the goals and purposes of the government"],answer:3,explain:"The Preamble ('We the People...') states the Constitution's purposes: form a more perfect union, establish justice, ensure domestic tranquility, provide for defense, promote welfare, secure liberty."},{id:"5004-038",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"United States History, Government, and Citizenship",q:"Which right is protected by the First Amendment?",options:["Freedom of speech","Protection from unreasonable search","Right to bear arms","Right to a fair trial"],answer:0,explain:"The First Amendment protects speech, religion, press, assembly, and petition. Bearing arms is the Second Amendment. Fair trial is the Sixth. Protection from search is the Fourth."},{id:"5004-039",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"United States History, Government, and Citizenship",q:"Students confuse the Bill of Rights with the original Constitution. Which comparison is most helpful?",options:["The Bill of Rights came first","The Bill of Rights was added later to protect individual liberties not explicitly stated in the original Constitution","They are the same document","The Constitution replaced the Bill of Rights"],answer:1,explain:"The Bill of Rights (first ten amendments) was added in 1791 to address Anti-Federalist concerns about individual rights. It was not part of the original 1787 Constitution."},{id:"5004-040",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"United States History, Government, and Citizenship",q:"What happens if the Supreme Court declares a law unconstitutional?",options:["The President overrides the decision","The law is temporarily suspended","The law is void and cannot be enforced","Congress must rewrite the law immediately"],answer:2,explain:"Judicial review allows courts to invalidate laws that conflict with the Constitution. The law becomes unenforceable. Congress may rewrite it to address the Court's concerns."},{id:"5004-041",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"United States History, Government, and Citizenship",q:"How many votes in the Senate are needed to convict in an impeachment trial?",options:["Three-fourths","Simple majority","Unanimous","Two-thirds"],answer:3,explain:"Constitution Article I requires a two-thirds vote of Senators present to convict and remove a president or other federal officer."},{id:"5004-042",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"United States History, Government, and Citizenship",q:"The concept of 'checks and balances' differs from 'separation of powers' because:",options:["Separation divides branches; checks and balances let each branch limit the others","Separation of powers is not in the Constitution","They are the same thing","Checks and balances apply only to the judiciary"],answer:0,explain:"Separation of powers assigns each branch distinct functions. Checks and balances allow each branch to constrain the others (e.g., veto, judicial review, override). They work together but are distinct concepts."},{id:"5004-043",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"United States History, Government, and Citizenship",q:"Which primary source is most effective for teaching students about the Constitutional Convention?",options:["A fictional story about the Founders","Excerpts from Madison's notes on the debates","A movie about the Constitution","A modern textbook summary"],answer:1,explain:"Madison's notes are a contemporary primary source showing actual debates and compromises. Textbooks are secondary. Fiction and movies are interpretive, not historical evidence."},{id:"5004-044",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"United States History, Government, and Citizenship",q:"Who writes federal laws?",options:["State governors","The President","Congress","The Supreme Court"],answer:2,explain:"Congress (House and Senate) creates federal legislation. The President signs or vetoes it. The Court interprets it. State governors make state laws."},{id:"5004-045",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"United States History, Government, and Citizenship",q:"Why did the framers include the Electoral College?",options:["To give more power to small states only","To let the President choose electors","Because they disliked democracy","As a compromise between election by Congress and direct popular vote"],answer:3,explain:"The Electoral College was a compromise: it gave states a role (based on congressional representation) while avoiding pure direct election or congressional selection. It balanced large and small state interests."},{id:"5004-046",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"United States History, Government, and Citizenship",q:"Students think the Constitution is outdated and irrelevant. Which response best addresses this?",options:["Show how Supreme Court cases apply constitutional principles to modern issues","Assign more reading without discussion","Tell them it is still the law","Agree that it is outdated"],answer:0,explain:"Connecting constitutional principles to current events demonstrates ongoing relevance. Asserting it is still law without explanation does not persuade. More reading without connection reinforces disengagement. Agreeing is educationally inappropriate."},{id:"5004-047",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"United States History, Government, and Citizenship",q:"What is the role of the Vice President in the Senate?",options:["To write Senate rules","To preside and cast tie-breaking votes","To vote on all legislation","To appoint judges"],answer:1,explain:"The Vice President presides over the Senate and casts votes only to break ties. They do not vote on all legislation, write rules, or appoint judges."},{id:"5004-048",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"United States History, Government, and Citizenship",q:"How many senators does each state have?",options:["One","Varies by state size","Two","Based on population"],answer:2,explain:"Each state has exactly two senators regardless of population, as established by the Great Compromise. Representatives are apportioned by population."},{id:"5005-031",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Earth Science",q:"What causes day and night on Earth?",options:["The Moon's orbit","The Sun moving around Earth","Clouds blocking sunlight","Earth's rotation on its axis"],answer:3,explain:"Earth rotates once approximately every 24 hours, causing different parts to face toward or away from the Sun. The Moon affects tides, not day/night. The Sun does not orbit Earth."},{id:"5005-032",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Earth Science",q:"Which rock type is formed from cooled magma or lava?",options:["Igneous","Limestone","Sedimentary","Metamorphic"],answer:0,explain:"Igneous rocks form from solidified molten rock. Sedimentary rocks form from compacted sediments. Metamorphic rocks form from heat and pressure. Limestone is a type of sedimentary rock."},{id:"5005-033",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Earth Science",q:"A rock layer is found upside down in a mountain. What is the most likely explanation?",options:["The layers were deposited upside down","Tectonic forces folded or faulted the layers after deposition","Water current arranged it that way","The rock was made that way"],answer:1,explain:"Tectonic activity can fold, fault, and invert rock layers after they form. Sedimentary layers deposit horizontally; inversion requires later geologic force."},{id:"5005-034",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Earth Science",q:"Which model best represents the layers of the Earth?",options:["A photograph of soil","A list of rock names","A cutaway diagram showing crust, mantle, outer core, and inner core with relative thickness","A globe"],answer:2,explain:"A cutaway diagram shows relative sizes and boundaries of Earth's internal layers. Soil photographs, lists, and globes do not reveal internal structure."},{id:"5005-035",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Earth Science",q:"What is the Moho?",options:["A weather pattern","A type of volcano","A fossil site","The boundary between Earth's crust and mantle"],answer:3,explain:"The Mohorovi\u010Di\u0107 discontinuity (Moho) marks the boundary between crust and mantle, identified by a change in seismic wave speed."},{id:"5005-036",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Earth Science",q:"How do continental and oceanic crust differ?",options:["Continental crust is thicker and less dense","Oceanic crust is thicker and less dense","Continental crust is thinner and denser","They are identical"],answer:0,explain:"Continental crust is thicker (30-50 km) but less dense, allowing it to 'float' higher. Oceanic crust is thinner (5-10 km) and denser, forming ocean basins."},{id:"5005-037",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Earth Science",q:"Students think volcanoes only occur at plate boundaries. How should a teacher address this?",options:["Confirm their belief","Show examples of hotspot volcanoes like Hawaii that occur away from boundaries","Skip the topic","Tell them they are wrong"],answer:1,explain:"Hotspot volcanism (e.g., Hawaii) occurs within plates, not just at boundaries. Providing counterexamples corrects the misconception. Confirming it reinforces error. Simply saying 'wrong' does not teach."},{id:"5005-038",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Earth Science",q:"What type of plate boundary creates new crust?",options:["Convergent","Strike-slip","Divergent","Transform"],answer:2,explain:"Divergent boundaries pull plates apart, allowing magma to rise and create new crust (e.g., mid-ocean ridges). Convergent boundaries destroy crust. Transform boundaries slide past each other."},{id:"5005-039",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Earth Science",q:"What is the Ring of Fire?",options:["A Native American legend","A fire safety program","A type of lava flow","A zone of frequent earthquakes and volcanoes around the Pacific Ocean"],answer:3,explain:"The Ring of Fire is a horseshoe-shaped zone of seismic and volcanic activity around the Pacific basin, caused by plate subduction."},{id:"5005-040",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Earth Science",q:"Why do earthquake waves help us know about Earth's interior?",options:["Different wave types travel at different speeds through different materials, revealing internal structure","They vibrate the surface only","They stop at the crust","They are visible from satellites"],answer:0,explain:"Seismic waves change speed and direction (refract) when passing through materials of different density. Shadow zones and wave behavior reveal the liquid outer core and solid inner core."},{id:"5005-041",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Earth Science",q:"Which experiment best demonstrates how sedimentary layers form?",options:["Melting ice cubes","Layering sand, gravel, and soil in a clear container and observing the strata","Boiling water","Mixing paint colors"],answer:1,explain:"Layering materials in a container creates visible strata, demonstrating how sediments accumulate in layers over time. Other experiments do not demonstrate sedimentation."},{id:"5005-042",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Earth Science",q:"What is the process called when rocks break down into smaller pieces?",options:["Erosion","Compaction","Weathering","Deposition"],answer:2,explain:"Weathering breaks rocks into smaller pieces in place. Erosion transports the pieces. Deposition lays them down. Compaction presses them together."},{id:"5005-043",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Earth Science",q:"How does the water cycle connect the atmosphere, land, and oceans?",options:["Water stays in one place","Only rain connects them","Oceans are separate from the cycle","Evaporation, condensation, precipitation, and runoff move water continuously among reservoirs"],answer:3,explain:"The water cycle is a continuous loop: oceans evaporate, water vapor condenses into clouds, precipitation falls on land, runoff returns to oceans. All reservoirs are connected."},{id:"5005-044",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Earth Science",q:"Students think that erosion and weathering are the same. Which distinction is most important to teach?",options:["Weathering breaks rocks down; erosion transports the pieces","They are the same","Erosion happens faster","Weathering only happens in deserts"],answer:0,explain:"Weathering is breakdown in place; erosion is movement. This distinction is fundamental to understanding landscape formation. They are related but distinct processes."},{id:"5005-045",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Life Science",q:"What is DNA's primary function?",options:["Building cell membranes","Storing genetic information","Energy storage","Digesting food"],answer:1,explain:"DNA carries genetic instructions for development, function, and reproduction. It is not primarily for energy, membranes, or digestion."},{id:"5005-046",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Life Science",q:"Why are mutations important to evolution?",options:["They always harm organisms","They prevent reproduction","They introduce genetic variation that natural selection acts upon","They have no effect"],answer:2,explain:"Mutations create new alleles, providing the raw material for evolution. Most are neutral; some are harmful; rare ones are beneficial. Variation is essential for natural selection."},{id:"5005-047",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Life Science",q:"Students think that evolution means individuals change during their lifetime. How should a teacher correct this?",options:["Say it is wrong without explanation","Agree with them","Skip evolution entirely","Distinguish between individual adaptation and population-level evolution over generations"],answer:3,explain:"Evolution occurs in populations over generations, not in individuals. An individual's traits are largely fixed. Teaching the distinction clarifies the misconception."},{id:"5005-048",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Life Science",q:"What is the role of RNA in protein synthesis?",options:["Carries genetic instructions from DNA to ribosomes to make proteins","Stores genetic information permanently","Builds cell walls","Provides energy"],answer:0,explain:"Messenger RNA (mRNA) carries DNA's code to ribosomes, where transfer RNA (tRNA) brings amino acids to build proteins. DNA stores information; RNA translates it."},{id:"5005-049",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Life Science",q:"Which organelle contains chlorophyll?",options:["Mitochondria","Chloroplast","Ribosome","Nucleus"],answer:1,explain:"Chloroplasts contain chlorophyll, the green pigment that captures light for photosynthesis. Mitochondria produce energy. The nucleus stores DNA. Ribosomes make proteins."},{id:"5005-050",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Life Science",q:"Why do sexually reproducing organisms have more genetic variation than asexual ones?",options:["Sexual reproduction is faster","Asexual reproduction does not exist","Combining genetic material from two parents creates new gene combinations","Sexual reproduction produces clones"],answer:2,explain:"Meiosis and fertilization shuffle genes, creating unique combinations. Asexual reproduction produces genetically identical offspring. Variation comes from recombination, not speed."},{id:"5005-051",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Life Science",q:"Which investigation best demonstrates osmosis?",options:["Observe identical potato strips before treatment only","Place equal potato strips in distilled water and salt solutions, then measure changes in mass","Count cells in a dry microscope slide","Compare ropes of different thickness"],answer:1,explain:"Using equal potato strips, controlled solution volumes, and before-and-after mass measurements provides observable evidence of water movement across cell membranes. A comparison without treatment or measurement cannot demonstrate osmosis."},{id:"5005-052",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Life Science",q:"What is the basic unit of heredity?",options:["Gene","Cell","Organ","Tissue"],answer:0,explain:"A gene is a segment of DNA that codes for a specific trait. Cells are larger units. Organs and tissues are higher levels of organization."},{id:"5005-053",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Life Science",q:"If a homozygous dominant (BB) parent crosses with a homozygous recessive (bb) parent, what percentage of offspring are expected to show the dominant trait?",options:["0%","100%","25%","50%"],answer:1,explain:"All offspring are Bb (heterozygous). Since B is dominant, all express the dominant trait. This is a basic Mendelian cross."},{id:"5005-054",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Life Science",q:"Students think that organisms evolve because they 'need' to. How should a teacher respond?",options:["Say it is too complex to explain","Agree\u2014it is common sense","Explain that evolution results from random variation and non-random selection, not purposeful need","Tell them to stop asking"],answer:2,explain:"Evolution is not goal-directed. Random mutations provide variation; environmental pressures select advantageous traits. Teaching teleology reinforces misconceptions."},{id:"5005-055",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Life Science",q:"What is homeostasis?",options:["Reproduction","Growth","Movement","Maintaining stable internal conditions"],answer:3,explain:"Homeostasis is the maintenance of stable internal conditions (temperature, pH, water balance) despite external changes. It is a key characteristic of life."},{id:"5005-056",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Life Science",q:"Which kingdom is characterized mainly by multicellular organisms that carry out photosynthesis?",options:["Plantae","Animalia","Fungi","Archaea"],answer:0,explain:"Members of Plantae are predominantly multicellular photosynthetic organisms. Some protists are also photosynthetic, so asking only which kingdom \u201Cincludes\u201D autotrophs would not have a unique answer."},{id:"5005-057",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Life Science",q:"Why do predators and prey often evolve together?",options:["They compete for the same food","Coevolution: adaptations in one drive counter-adaptations in the other","They are the same species","Predators protect prey"],answer:1,explain:"Coevolution occurs when species exert selective pressure on each other. Prey evolve better escape mechanisms; predators evolve better hunting strategies. This 'arms race' drives mutual adaptation."},{id:"5005-058",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Life Science",q:"Which assessment best measures understanding of ecological relationships?",options:["Memorizing trophic levels","Labeling a diagram without explanation","Having students create and explain a food web for a local ecosystem","Multiple choice on definitions"],answer:2,explain:"Creating and explaining a food web demonstrates applied understanding of relationships. Definitions and labeling test recognition, not comprehension. Application reveals deeper understanding."},{id:"5005-059",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Physical Science",q:"What is the SI unit of force?",options:["Joule","Pascal","Watt","Newton"],answer:3,explain:"Force is measured in Newtons (N). Joules measure energy. Watts measure power. Pascals measure pressure."},{id:"5005-060",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Physical Science",q:"According to Newton's second law, what happens to acceleration if mass doubles while force stays the same?",options:["Acceleration halves","Acceleration quadruples","Acceleration doubles","Acceleration stays the same"],answer:0,explain:"F = ma, so a = F/m. If mass doubles, acceleration is halved (inversely proportional). Acceleration decreases, not increases."},{id:"5005-061",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Physical Science",q:"Students think that heavier objects fall faster. Which investigation most effectively challenges this?",options:["Show a picture of falling objects","Drop two objects of different mass and observe they hit simultaneously (in vacuum or minimal air resistance)","Have them calculate weight","Tell them the law"],answer:1,explain:"Empirical evidence\u2014dropping objects of different mass\u2014directly contradicts the misconception. Explanation without evidence is less persuasive. Pictures and calculations do not demonstrate the principle."},{id:"5005-062",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Physical Science",q:"What is the relationship between frequency and wavelength of a wave?",options:["They are always equal","They are unrelated","They are inversely proportional (higher frequency = shorter wavelength)","They are directly proportional"],answer:2,explain:"Frequency and wavelength are inversely related: c = f\u03BB. Higher frequency means shorter wavelength (and vice versa) for waves traveling at the same speed."},{id:"5005-063",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Physical Science",q:"What type of energy does a moving object have?",options:["Chemical energy","Potential energy","Nuclear energy","Kinetic energy"],answer:3,explain:"Kinetic energy is energy of motion. Potential energy is stored energy. Chemical energy is stored in bonds. Nuclear energy is stored in atomic nuclei."},{id:"5005-064",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Physical Science",q:"Why does a mirror produce a reflection?",options:["Light bounces off the smooth surface at the same angle it arrived","The mirror creates light","It absorbs light","Light passes through the mirror"],answer:0,explain:"Reflection follows the law: angle of incidence equals angle of reflection. Smooth surfaces (mirrors) produce clear reflections. Absorption, creation, and transmission do not produce images."},{id:"5005-065",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Physical Science",q:"Which demonstration best shows that air has mass?",options:["Blowing on your hand","Weighing an inflated balloon, deflating it, and weighing again","Feeling wind","Watching wind blow leaves"],answer:1,explain:"Weighing before and after deflation shows the mass of the escaped air. Blowing, watching leaves, and feeling wind demonstrate air movement but not mass directly."},{id:"5005-066",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Physical Science",q:"What is the chemical symbol for gold?",options:["Ag","Gd","Au","Go"],answer:2,explain:"Au comes from 'aurum,' the Latin name for gold. Go is not a symbol. Gd is gadolinium. Ag is silver (argentum)."},{id:"5005-067",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Physical Science",q:"What state of matter has definite shape and volume?",options:["Gas","Liquid","Plasma","Solid"],answer:3,explain:"Solids have fixed shape and volume because particles are tightly packed in fixed positions. Liquids have definite volume but take container shape. Gases have neither."},{id:"5005-068",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Physical Science",q:"Why do metals conduct electricity well?",options:["They have free-moving electrons in their atomic structure","They are shiny","They are heavy","They have tightly bound electrons"],answer:0,explain:"Metals have a 'sea' of delocalized electrons that move freely, carrying electric current. Tight binding prevents conduction. Weight and shine are unrelated properties."},{id:"5005-069",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Physical Science",q:"Which approach best teaches the concept of density?",options:["Show a video only","Have students predict and test whether objects sink or float in water, then relate to mass/volume","Memorize the formula","Give a definition and move on"],answer:1,explain:"Sink/float predictions create intuitive understanding before formal definition. Memorization without experience is shallow. Videos and definitions alone lack engagement."},{id:"5005-070",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Physical Science",q:"What is the speed of light approximately?",options:["300 km/s","30,000 km/s","300,000 km/s","3,000 km/s"],answer:2,explain:"Light travels at approximately 300,000 kilometers per second (3 \xD7 10\u2078 m/s) in a vacuum."},{id:"5005-071",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Physical Science",q:"What happens to the pressure of a gas when its volume decreases at constant temperature?",options:["Pressure decreases","Pressure stays the same","Pressure becomes zero","Pressure increases"],answer:3,explain:"Boyle's Law: P\u2081V\u2081 = P\u2082V\u2082. Decreasing volume increases pressure (inverse relationship). Particles collide more frequently with container walls."},{id:"5005-072",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Physical Science",q:"Which scenario best demonstrates conservation of momentum?",options:["Two colliding carts where total momentum before equals total momentum after","A Falling object","A ball rolling to a stop","A stationary rock"],answer:0,explain:"In a closed system, total momentum is conserved during collisions. A rolling ball stopping involves friction (external force). Falling and stationary objects do not demonstrate collision dynamics."},{id:"5004-049",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"Geography, Anthropology, and Sociology",q:"Which geographic tool is best suited for analyzing layers of population, roads, and land use together?",options:["A compass used only to find direction","A geographic information system (GIS)","A political speech","A chronological timeline"],answer:1,explain:"GIS stores and overlays spatial data layers, allowing users to analyze relationships among population, transportation, and land use. A compass provides direction but cannot combine data layers."},{id:"5004-050",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"Geography, Anthropology, and Sociology",q:"What is 'place' as a geographic concept?",options:["Only coordinates on a map","The distance between two points","The physical and human characteristics that give a location unique identity","Any point on Earth"],answer:2,explain:"Place combines location with human and physical characteristics that make it distinctive. Coordinates alone are 'site.' Location is position. Distance is separation."},{id:"5004-051",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"Geography, Anthropology, and Sociology",q:"Students confuse 'location' with 'place.' Which distinction is clearest?",options:["They are synonyms","Location is physical; place is mental","Place is older than location","Location is where; place is what makes it unique"],answer:3,explain:"Location answers 'where' (coordinates, address). Place answers 'what is it like there' (characteristics, meaning). The distinction is fundamental to geographic thinking."},{id:"5004-052",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"Geography, Anthropology, and Sociology",q:"Which type of map projection distorts area the least?",options:["Equal-area (projeci\xF3n equivalente)","Conic","Mercator","Cylindrical"],answer:0,explain:"Equal-area projections preserve relative size, though they distort shape. Mercator preserves angles but severely distorts area near poles. Conic and cylindrical are families, not distortion types."},{id:"5004-053",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"Geography, Anthropology, and Sociology",q:"What is the prime meridian?",options:["The equator","0\xB0 longitude, passing through Greenwich, England","The International Date Line","The Tropic of Cancer"],answer:1,explain:"The Prime Meridian is 0\xB0 longitude, the reference line for measuring east-west position. The equator is 0\xB0 latitude."},{id:"5004-054",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"Geography, Anthropology, and Sociology",q:"What is the difference between density and dispersion?",options:["They are the same","Density is about quality; dispersion is about quantity","Density is the number of items per unit area; dispersion is the pattern of spread","Dispersion applies only to animals"],answer:2,explain:"Density measures concentration (people per km\xB2). Dispersion describes arrangement (clustered, scattered, linear). Both are fundamental geographic concepts."},{id:"5004-055",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"Geography, Anthropology, and Sociology",q:"Which activity best teaches students to think geographically?",options:["Memorizing state capitals","Listing country names","Copying maps","Asking 'why there?'\u2014examining why phenomena occur where they do"],answer:3,explain:"Geographic thinking centers on asking why things are located where they are, considering spatial patterns, processes, and relationships. Rote memorization does not develop spatial reasoning."},{id:"5004-056",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"Geography, Anthropology, and Sociology",q:"What is globalization's effect on local cultures?",options:["It can homogenize cultures but also strengthens cultural resistance and adaptation","It always destroys local culture","It only affects economics","It has no effect"],answer:0,explain:"Globalization creates both convergence (homogenization) and divergence (cultural preservation/adaptation). The effect is complex, not uniformly destructive or neutral."},{id:"5004-057",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"Geography, Anthropology, and Sociology",q:"Which climate zone is found near the equator?",options:["Polar","Tropical","Temperate","Arid"],answer:1,explain:"Tropical climates occur near the equator (0\xB0\u201323.5\xB0 latitude), characterized by warm temperatures year-round. Polar is near poles. Temperate is mid-latitude. Arid can occur in various zones."},{id:"5004-058",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"World History and Economics",q:"Which civilization developed cuneiform writing?",options:["Romans","Egyptians","Sumerians","Greeks"],answer:2,explain:"Sumerians in Mesopotamia developed cuneiform, one of the earliest writing systems, around 3200 BCE. Egyptians developed hieroglyphs. Greeks and Romans used alphabetic systems."},{id:"5004-059",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"World History and Economics",q:"What was the primary economic impact of the Silk Road?",options:["It only transported silk","It had no significant impact","It isolated civilizations","It connected East and West through trade, spreading goods and ideas"],answer:3,explain:"The Silk Road was a network of trade routes connecting China to the Mediterranean, facilitating exchange of goods, religions, technologies, and diseases across Eurasia."},{id:"5004-060",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"World History and Economics",q:"Which method best helps students understand cause and effect in historical events?",options:["Analyzing primary sources to identify multiple causes and consequences","Reading summaries only","Memorizing dates","Listing events chronologically"],answer:0,explain:"Primary sources reveal complexity of causation. Dates and chronological lists are factual but do not explain why. Summaries simplify causation."},{id:"5004-061",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"World History and Economics",q:"What was the main outcome of the Magna Carta (1215)?",options:["It abolished slavery","It limited the king's power and established legal rights","It created the United Nations","It established democracy"],answer:1,explain:"The Magna Carta established that the king was not above the law and guaranteed certain legal rights. It did not create democracy, the UN, or abolish slavery."},{id:"5004-062",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"World History and Economics",q:"Which ancient wonder is located at Giza in Egypt and still stands today?",options:["Temple of Artemis at Ephesus","Hanging Gardens of Babylon","Great Pyramid of Giza","Colossus of Rhodes"],answer:2,explain:"The Great Pyramid of Giza is in Egypt and is the only one of the traditional Seven Wonders of the Ancient World still substantially standing. The other listed wonders were located outside Egypt."},{id:"5004-063",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"World History and Economics",q:"How did the Industrial Revolution change family structure?",options:["Children stopped working","Families became larger and stayed on farms","There was no change","Work moved from homes to factories, changing family roles and dynamics"],answer:3,explain:"Industrialization separated workplace from home, shifting labor patterns. Family roles changed as wages became central. Child labor initially increased before reform."},{id:"5003-041",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Numbers and Operations",q:"What is 25% of 200?",options:["50","25","100","75"],answer:0,explain:"25% = 0.25. 0.25 \xD7 200 = 50. Or: 25% is one-fourth, and 200 \xF7 4 = 50."},{id:"5003-042",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Numbers and Operations",q:"Why is the sum of two odd numbers always even?",options:["Because all numbers are even","Because odd numbers can be written as 2n+1, and (2n+1)+(2m+1) = 2(n+m+1), which is even","Because odd numbers cancel out","Because odd numbers are prime"],answer:1,explain:"Any odd number is 2n+1. Adding two odds: (2n+1)+(2m+1) = 2n+2m+2 = 2(n+m+1). This is 2 times an integer, hence even."},{id:"5003-043",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Numbers and Operations",q:"A student adds fractions by adding numerators and denominators separately (1/3 + 1/4 = 2/7). Which intervention addresses this error?",options:["Give the student harder problems","Have the student memorize more fractions","Use visual models to show that 1/3 + 1/4 is more than 1/2, while 2/7 is less","Tell the student the rule"],answer:2,explain:"Visual models create cognitive conflict: 1/3 + 1/4 clearly exceeds half, but 2/7 does not. This reveals the error's absurdity better than rules alone."},{id:"5003-044",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Numbers and Operations",q:"What is the LCM of 4 and 6?",options:["24","10","6","12"],answer:3,explain:"Multiples of 4: 4, 8, 12... Multiples of 6: 6, 12... The least common multiple is 12."},{id:"5003-045",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Numbers and Operations",q:"Which number is not divisible by 9?",options:["36","28","18","27"],answer:1,explain:"A number is divisible by 9 when its digits sum to a multiple of 9. The digit sum of 28 is 10, so 28 is not divisible by 9; 18, 27, and 36 are."},{id:"5003-046",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Numbers and Operations",q:"For any two positive integers a and b, which relationship is true?",options:["There is no relationship between GCD and LCM","GCD(a,b) \xD7 LCM(a,b) = a \xD7 b","GCD is always larger than LCM","GCD(a,b) = LCM(a,b)"],answer:1,explain:"For positive integers, the product of the greatest common divisor and least common multiple equals the product of the two integers. For 4 and 6, 2 \xD7 12 = 4 \xD7 6 = 24."},{id:"5003-047",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Numbers and Operations",q:"Which activity best helps students understand equivalent fractions?",options:["Converting to decimals only","Drawing random shapes","Using fraction strips or circles to show that 1/2 = 2/4 = 3/6","Memorizing fraction tables"],answer:2,explain:"Concrete manipulatives show visually that different numbers of parts can represent the same amount. Memorization without experience does not build conceptual understanding."},{id:"5003-048",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Numbers and Operations",q:"What is the reciprocal of 3/4?",options:["0","3/4","-3/4","4/3"],answer:3,explain:"The reciprocal flips the fraction: 3/4 \u2192 4/3. Multiplying a number by its reciprocal equals 1."},{id:"5003-049",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Numbers and Operations",q:"Which number is both a perfect square and a perfect cube?",options:["64","16","8","81"],answer:0,explain:"64 = 8\xB2 = 4\xB3. It is both a perfect square (8\xD78) and a perfect cube (4\xD74\xD74). Eight is a cube but not a square. Sixteen is a square but not a cube. Eighty-one is a square but not a cube."},{id:"5003-050",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Numbers and Operations",q:"If a/b = c/d, which statement must be true?",options:["a + b = c + d","ad = bc","a = c and b = d","a/c = b/d is false"],answer:1,explain:"Cross-multiplication: if a/b = c/d, then ad = bc. This is the fundamental property of proportions."},{id:"5003-051",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Algebraic Thinking",q:"Solve: 5x - 3 = 12",options:["x = 2","x = 15","x = 3","x = 9"],answer:2,explain:"Add 3 to both sides: 5x = 15. Divide by 5: x = 3. Check: 5(3) - 3 = 15 - 3 = 12."},{id:"5003-052",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Algebraic Thinking",q:"Which expression is equivalent to (x + 3)(x - 3)?",options:["x\xB2 + 9","x\xB2 + 6x - 9","x\xB2 - 6x + 9","x\xB2 - 9"],answer:3,explain:"This is a difference of squares: (a+b)(a-b) = a\xB2 - b\xB2. So (x+3)(x-3) = x\xB2 - 9."},{id:"5003-053",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Algebraic Thinking",q:"What is the slope of the line y = 3x + 2?",options:["3","x","y","2"],answer:0,explain:"In slope-intercept form y = mx + b, the coefficient of x is the slope. Here m = 3."},{id:"5003-054",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Algebraic Thinking",q:"Which graph represents an inverse variation?",options:["A parabola","A hyperbola in quadrants I and III","A horizontal line","A straight line through the origin"],answer:1,explain:"Inverse variation (y = k/x) produces a hyperbola. Direct variation (y = kx) is a line through the origin. Parabolas represent quadratic relationships."},{id:"5003-055",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Algebraic Thinking",q:"Students write '2x = 10, so x = 5' but cannot explain why. What is the best follow-up?",options:["Move on to the next problem","Give them more equations to solve","Ask the student to check: does 2 times 5 equal 10? Then ask what operation undoes multiplication","Tell them the answer is correct"],answer:2,explain:"Checking reinforces understanding: 2 \xD7 5 = 10 confirms the solution. Asking about inverse operations builds conceptual knowledge of division as the opposite of multiplication."},{id:"5003-056",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Geometry and Measurement, Data, Statistics, and Probability",q:"What is the perimeter of a square with side length 7 cm?",options:["49 cm","14 cm","21 cm","28 cm"],answer:3,explain:"Perimeter = 4 \xD7 side = 4 \xD7 7 = 28 cm. Forty-nine is the area, not the perimeter."},{id:"5003-057",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Geometry and Measurement, Data, Statistics, and Probability",q:"Two triangles are similar. If the ratio of their corresponding sides is 2:3, what is the ratio of their areas?",options:["4:9","1:1","2:3","8:27"],answer:0,explain:"Area ratio = (side ratio)\xB2 = (2/3)\xB2 = 4/9. Perimeter ratio equals side ratio; volume ratio is the cube."},{id:"5003-058",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Geometry and Measurement, Data, Statistics, and Probability",q:"Students measure the lengths of four ropes as 1.2 m, 95 cm, 1.05 m, and 110 cm. Which step best supports an accurate comparison?",options:["Compare the written digits without considering units","Convert all measurements to the same unit before ordering them","Round every measurement to the nearest meter","Count the strands in each rope"],answer:1,explain:"Measurements must use a common unit before they can be compared reliably. Converting all lengths to centimeters or meters preserves their values; comparing raw digits or over-rounding can reverse the order."},{id:"5003-059",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Geometry and Measurement, Data, Statistics, and Probability",q:"What is the mode of the data set: 3, 5, 5, 7, 9, 9, 9, 11?",options:["7","11","9","5"],answer:2,explain:"The mode is the most frequently occurring value. Nine appears three times, more than any other value."},{id:"5003-060",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Geometry and Measurement, Data, Statistics, and Probability",q:"What is the area of a triangle with base 10 cm and height 6 cm?",options:["60 cm\xB2","120 cm\xB2","16 cm\xB2","30 cm\xB2"],answer:3,explain:"Area = \xBD \xD7 base \xD7 height = \xBD \xD7 10 \xD7 6 = 30 cm\xB2."},{id:"5004-064",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"United States History, Government, and Citizenship",q:"What is the filibuster?",options:["A Senate tactic used to prolong debate or delay or prevent a vote","A constitutional amendment","A presidential veto","A procedure for ending debate immediately"],answer:0,explain:"A filibuster is an effort in the Senate to prolong debate or delay or prevent a vote. For most legislation, invoking cloture generally requires three-fifths of the full Senate, usually 60 votes."},{id:"5004-065",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"United States History, Government, and Citizenship",q:"Why was the 14th Amendment significant?",options:["It established the income tax","It guaranteed equal protection under the law and defined citizenship","It granted women the vote","It abolished slavery"],answer:1,explain:"The 14th Amendment (1868) granted citizenship to all born in the U.S. and guaranteed equal protection. The 19th granted women's suffrage. The 13th abolished slavery. The 16th established income tax."},{id:"5004-066",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"United States History, Government, and Citizenship",q:"Which approach best evaluates the reliability of a historical source?",options:["Accept it whenever it was created during the event","Examine the author, purpose, context, and corroboration with other evidence","Prefer the source with the most dramatic language","Use only modern summaries"],answer:1,explain:"A source is not automatically reliable merely because it is primary. Historians evaluate who created it, for what purpose, in what context, and whether other evidence corroborates its claims."},{id:"5004-067",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"United States History, Government, and Citizenship",q:"What does the Constitution's Preamble begin with?",options:["The Constitution establishes","When in the Course of Human Events","In God We Trust","We the People"],answer:3,explain:"The Preamble begins 'We the People of the United States...'"},{id:"5004-068",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"United States History, Government, and Citizenship",q:"How does federalism affect citizens' rights?",options:["Some rights are protected federally while states can grant additional protections","Federalism eliminates all state power","States can override federal rights","Rights are the same in every state"],answer:0,explain:"Federalism creates layered rights: the Constitution sets minimum protections, but states can provide additional rights (e.g., privacy, education). States cannot diminish federal rights."},{id:"5004-069",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"United States History, Government, and Citizenship",q:"Which approach best teaches students about the Constitution's adaptability?",options:["Ignoring the Constitution","Examining amendments and Supreme Court cases that interpret the Constitution in new contexts","Reading the original text only","Memorizing the articles"],answer:1,explain:"Amendments and landmark cases show how the Constitution evolves. Reading only the original text misses its living nature. Memorization without application is insufficient."},{id:"5005-073",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Earth Science",q:"What causes tides on Earth?",options:["Earth's rotation only","Wind","The Moon's gravitational pull","Ocean currents"],answer:2,explain:"Tides are primarily caused by the Moon's gravitational attraction on Earth's oceans. The Sun also contributes but is less influential due to distance."},{id:"5005-074",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Earth Science",q:"How do continents move?",options:["They float on water","They are pushed by ocean currents","They spin with Earth","Convection currents in the mantle drive plate motion"],answer:3,explain:"Mantle convection currents transfer heat from Earth's interior, causing plates to move. Continents are carried on these plates. They do not float on water or ride ocean currents."},{id:"5005-075",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Earth Science",q:"Students think all eclipses occur because the Moon blocks the Sun. Which explanation best corrects this?",options:["Use a Sun-Earth-Moon model to show that the Moon blocks sunlight during a solar eclipse, while Earth\u2019s shadow falls on the Moon during a lunar eclipse","State that the Moon never blocks sunlight","Explain that eclipses are caused by clouds","Treat solar and lunar eclipses as the same alignment"],answer:0,explain:"Solar and lunar eclipses involve different alignments. During a solar eclipse the Moon lies between Earth and the Sun; during a lunar eclipse Earth lies between the Sun and Moon."},{id:"5005-076",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Earth Science",q:"What is the rock cycle?",options:["Rocks that rotate","The continuous transformation of rocks between igneous, sedimentary, and metamorphic types","A type of fossil","Rock music"],answer:1,explain:"The rock cycle describes how rocks transform through geological processes: melting, cooling, weathering, compaction, and metamorphism."},{id:"5005-077",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Earth Science",q:"What is the most abundant gas in Earth's atmosphere?",options:["Carbon dioxide","Oxygen","Nitrogen","Argon"],answer:2,explain:"Nitrogen makes up approximately 78% of the atmosphere. Oxygen is about 21%. Carbon dioxide is about 0.04%."},{id:"5005-078",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Earth Science",q:"Why do we have seasons?",options:["The Sun changes temperature","Earth is closer to the Sun in summer","Clouds block sunlight","Earth's axis is tilted, causing different hemispheres to receive more direct sunlight at different times"],answer:3,explain:"Earth's 23.5\xB0 axial tilt causes one hemisphere to lean toward the Sun (summer) while the other leans away. Distance variation is minimal and not the cause."},{id:"5005-079",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Earth Science",q:"Which investigation best helps students understand soil erosion?",options:["Pouring water over soil slopes with and without vegetation and observing runoff","Reading about erosion","Watching a video","Memorizing definitions"],answer:0,explain:"Hands-on erosion experiments with and without plant cover demonstrate how roots stabilize soil. Reading, videos, and memorization do not provide experiential evidence."},{id:"5005-080",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Earth Science",q:"What is the Mohs hardness of diamond?",options:["7","10","1","5"],answer:1,explain:"Diamond is the hardest natural mineral on the Mohs scale, rated 10. Talc is 1 (softest). Quartz is 7."},{id:"5005-081",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Life Science",q:"What is the function of the cell membrane?",options:["Protein synthesis","Energy production","Controlling what enters and exits the cell","Cell division"],answer:2,explain:"The cell membrane is selectively permeable, regulating the passage of substances in and out of the cell."},{id:"5005-082",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Life Science",q:"Why do cells divide?",options:["To create energy","Because they get bored","To make the organism bigger only","For growth, repair, and reproduction"],answer:3,explain:"Cell division (mitosis) enables growth, tissue repair, and asexual reproduction. Cells do not divide for entertainment or energy production."},{id:"5005-083",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Life Science",q:"Students think that viruses are living organisms. Which evidence best helps them revise this?",options:["Show that viruses cannot reproduce without a host cell and lack cellular structure","Ignore the misconception","Compare viruses to bacteria without explanation","Tell them viruses are not alive"],answer:0,explain:"Viruses lack cellular structure and cannot reproduce independently\u2014they require a host. These are key criteria for life that viruses do not meet."},{id:"5005-084",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Life Science",q:"What is the process by which plants convert sunlight into food?",options:["Fermentation","Photosynthesis","Transpiration","Respiration"],answer:1,explain:"Photosynthesis uses sunlight, water, and CO\u2082 to produce glucose and oxygen: 6CO\u2082 + 6H\u2082O \u2192 C\u2086H\u2081\u2082O\u2086 + 6O\u2082."},{id:"5005-085",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Life Science",q:"Which organism is a producer?",options:["Fungus","Human","Grass","Lion"],answer:2,explain:"Producers (autotrophs) make their own food via photosynthesis. Grass is a producer. Lions and humans are consumers. Fungi are decomposers."},{id:"5005-086",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Life Science",q:"What is the significance of meiosis?",options:["It produces energy","It repairs damaged DNA","It produces identical cells","It creates genetic diversity through crossing over and independent assortment"],answer:3,explain:"Meiosis halves chromosome number and shuffles genetic material, creating unique gametes. This diversity is essential for evolution and adaptation."},{id:"5005-087",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Life Science",q:"Which lab best demonstrates natural selection?",options:["Simulating predation with colored beads on colored paper and counting survivors","Measuring plant height","Observing beans germinate","Counting cell divisions"],answer:0,explain:"Bead simulations show how camouflage affects survival\u2014predators select more visible beads. This models selection pressure empirically."},{id:"5005-088",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Life Science",q:"What is the basic unit of classification?",options:["Kingdom","Species","Genus","Phylum"],answer:1,explain:"Species is the most specific level of classification. The hierarchy is: domain, kingdom, phylum, class, order, family, genus, species."},{id:"5005-089",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Physical Science",q:"What is the unit of electrical resistance?",options:["Watt","Ampere","Ohm","Volt"],answer:2,explain:"Resistance is measured in Ohms (\u03A9). Voltage is volts. Current is amperes. Power is watts."},{id:"5005-090",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Physical Science",q:"What happens to the frequency of a wave when its wavelength increases (assuming constant speed)?",options:["Frequency stays the same","Frequency becomes zero","Frequency increases","Frequency decreases"],answer:3,explain:"Since speed = frequency \xD7 wavelength, if wavelength increases and speed is constant, frequency must decrease (inverse relationship)."},{id:"5005-091",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Physical Science",q:"Students think that heat and temperature are the same. Which distinction is most important?",options:["Heat is energy transfer; temperature measures average kinetic energy","They are identical","Temperature is always higher","Heat only occurs in solids"],answer:0,explain:"Heat is thermal energy in transit. Temperature measures the average kinetic energy of particles. A cup of coffee and a bathtub can have the same temperature but different heat content."},{id:"5005-092",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Physical Science",q:"What is the chemical formula for table salt?",options:["CO\u2082","NaCl","H\u2082O","O\u2082"],answer:1,explain:"Table salt is sodium chloride (NaCl). H\u2082O is water. CO\u2082 is carbon dioxide. O\u2082 is oxygen gas."},{id:"5005-093",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Physical Science",q:"What type of energy is stored in food?",options:["Kinetic energy","Sound energy","Chemical energy","Nuclear energy"],answer:2,explain:"Food stores chemical energy in molecular bonds, released during digestion and cellular respiration."},{id:"5005-094",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Physical Science",q:"Why does a rocket accelerate upward when it ejects gas downward?",options:["The rocket is lighter than air","Gravity pulls the rocket up","The gas pushes against the ground","Newton's third law: action-reaction pairs\u2014the gas pushes down, the rocket pushes up"],answer:3,explain:"Newton's third law: for every action, there is an equal and opposite reaction. The rocket expels gas downward; the gas pushes the rocket upward. This works in vacuum too."},{id:"5005-095",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Physical Science",q:"Which model best represents the atomic structure of an element?",options:["A nucleus surrounded by electron clouds","A solid sphere","A maze","A circle with dots on the edge"],answer:0,explain:"Modern atomic theory describes a dense nucleus with electrons in probability clouds around it. The solid sphere is the outdated Dalton model."},{id:"5005-096",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Physical Science",q:"What is the boiling point of water at sea level?",options:["0\xB0C","100\xB0C","32\xB0C","212\xB0C"],answer:1,explain:"Water boils at 100\xB0C (212\xB0F) at sea level. It freezes at 0\xB0C (32\xB0F). Boiling point decreases at higher altitudes."},{id:"5004-070",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"Geography, Anthropology, and Sociology",q:"What is the largest ocean?",options:["Atlantic","Indian","Pacific","Arctic"],answer:2,explain:"The Pacific Ocean is the largest, covering more area than all land masses combined."},{id:"5004-071",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"Geography, Anthropology, and Sociology",q:"What is the difference between migration and diffusion?",options:["Diffusion is physical movement","Migration only happens in animals","They are the same","Migration involves movement of people; diffusion involves spread of ideas or traits"],answer:3,explain:"Migration = physical movement of people. Diffusion = spread of cultural traits, ideas, or innovations without movement of people."},{id:"5004-072",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"Geography, Anthropology, and Sociology",q:"Which activity best teaches students about human-environment interaction?",options:["Investigating how a local community's activities affect the environment and vice versa","Listing natural resources","Memorizing climate zones","Drawing maps"],answer:0,explain:"Local investigation makes the abstract concept concrete: students see real cause-effect relationships between people and their environment."},{id:"5004-073",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"Geography, Anthropology, and Sociology",q:"What is urbanization?",options:["Building factories","The growth of cities and increasing proportion of people living in urban areas","Planting trees in cities","Moving to rural areas"],answer:1,explain:"Urbanization is the process by which a growing percentage of a population lives in cities and towns."},{id:"5004-074",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"Geography, Anthropology, and Sociology",q:"Which continent has the most countries?",options:["South America","Europe","Africa","Asia"],answer:2,explain:"Africa has 54 recognized countries, the most of any continent."},{id:"5004-075",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"Geography, Anthropology, and Sociology",q:"What is a 'culture hearth'?",options:["A cooking area","A place where fires are started","A type of museum","A region where major cultural traits originate and spread"],answer:3,explain:"Culture hearths are regions where civilizations developed and from which cultural innovations spread (e.g., Mesopotamia, Indus Valley, China)."},{id:"5004-076",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"Geography, Anthropology, and Sociology",q:"Students think that all cultures progress through the same stages. Which response counters this?",options:["Show that cultures develop unique paths based on environment, history, and choice","Ignore the idea","Tell them it is wrong","Agree with them"],answer:0,explain:"Cultural development is not uniform\u2014environment, historical contact, and innovation create diverse trajectories. Showing variety counters linear stage theories."},{id:"5004-077",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"Geography, Anthropology, and Sociology",q:"What is the Equator?",options:["The North Pole","0\xB0 latitude","0\xB0 longitude","The International Date Line"],answer:1,explain:"The Equator is 0\xB0 latitude, dividing Earth into Northern and Southern hemispheres."},{id:"5004-078",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"World History and Economics",q:"Which empire was known for its code of laws under Hammurabi?",options:["Egyptian","Persian","Babylonian","Greek"],answer:2,explain:"Hammurabi's Code (c. 1750 BCE) was one of the earliest written legal codes, from the Babylonian Empire."},{id:"5004-079",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"World History and Economics",q:"What was the primary economic system of medieval Europe?",options:["Capitalism","Communism","Socialism","Feudalism"],answer:3,explain:"Feudalism was a hierarchical system based on land ownership and obligations between lords and vassals. Capitalism, socialism, and communism developed later."},{id:"5004-080",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"World History and Economics",q:"Which approach best helps students understand the impact of the Columbian Exchange?",options:["Analyzing how New World crops (potatoes, corn) transformed Old World populations and diets","Listing explorers' names","Memorizing traded items","Reading about ships only"],answer:0,explain:"Examining how potatoes enabled population growth in Europe makes the Exchange's impact concrete and measurable."},{id:"5004-081",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"World History and Economics",q:"What caused the fall of the Roman Empire?",options:["A single event","Multiple factors including invasion, economic trouble, and political instability","An alien invasion","The invention of gunpowder"],answer:1,explain:"Rome's fall resulted from barbarian invasions, economic decline, political corruption, and overexpansion. No single cause explains it."},{id:"5004-082",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"World History and Economics",q:"Which ancient civilization built Machu Picchu?",options:["Aztec","Olmec","Inca","Maya"],answer:2,explain:"Machu Picchu was built by the Inca civilization in the 15th century in present-day Peru."},{id:"5004-083",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"World History and Economics",q:"What is the significance of the Magna Carta?",options:["It established democracy","It created the United Nations","It abolished monarchy","It limited royal power and established that everyone is subject to the law"],answer:3,explain:"The Magna Carta (1215) established the principle that no one, not even the king, is above the law\u2014a foundation for constitutional governance."},{id:"5004-084",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"World History and Economics",q:"Students think that all civilizations follow the same developmental path. Which evidence challenges this?",options:["Compare case studies showing how geography, resources, institutions, and contact shaped different development paths","Present a single timeline as the universal sequence","Ask students to memorize the names of early civilizations","Avoid comparing civilizations"],answer:0,explain:"Comparative case studies show that societies developed differently in response to distinct environments, resources, institutions, and contacts. A single universal timeline would reinforce rather than challenge the misconception."},{id:"5004-085",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"World History and Economics",q:"What is the main religion of India?",options:["Islam","Hinduism","Christianity","Buddhism"],answer:1,explain:"Hinduism is the predominant religion in India, with about 80% of the population identifying as Hindu."},{id:"5005-097",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Earth Science",q:"What is the Earth's crust made of?",options:["Gas","Molten lava","Solid rock","Liquid metal"],answer:2,explain:"The crust is Earth's solid outer layer, composed of rock. The mantle is partially molten. The core is metallic."},{id:"5005-098",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Earth Science",q:"How do fossils form?",options:["Fossils grow in the ground","Fossils are made by worms","Animals turn to stone immediately","Organisms are buried by sediment, and minerals replace organic material over time"],answer:3,explain:"Fossilization requires rapid burial, protection from scavengers, and mineral replacement (permineralization) over geologic time."},{id:"5005-099",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Earth Science",q:"Students think that fossils prove species never change. How should a teacher respond?",options:["Show fossil sequences demonstrating evolution (e.g., horse, whale ancestry)","Tell them they are wrong","Agree with them","Skip fossils"],answer:0,explain:"Fossil sequences show gradual change over time\u2014evidence for evolution. The horse lineage and whale transition are classic examples."},{id:"5005-100",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Earth Science",q:"What type of earthquake wave travels fastest?",options:["S-wave (secondary wave)","P-wave (primary wave)","Surface wave","Love wave"],answer:1,explain:"P-waves (primary) are compressional waves that travel fastest and arrive first. S-waves are slower. Surface waves are slowest but cause most damage."},{id:"5005-101",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Earth Science",q:"What is the process by which rain, snow, or sleet falls to Earth?",options:["Condensation","Evaporation","Precipitation","Transpiration"],answer:2,explain:"Precipitation is water falling from clouds as rain, snow, sleet, or hail. Evaporation is liquid to gas. Condensation is gas to liquid."},{id:"5005-102",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Earth Science",q:"Why are sedimentary rocks important to scientists?",options:["They are rare","They contain no information","They are used for building only","They preserve fossils and record Earth's history"],answer:3,explain:"Sedimentary layers (strata) contain fossils and record environmental conditions at the time of deposition, providing a timeline of Earth's history."},{id:"5005-103",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Earth Science",q:"Which demonstration best shows how mountains form?",options:["Pushing two pieces of foam board together to simulate collision","Melting ice","Blowing air","Pouring water on sand"],answer:0,explain:"Pushing foam boards simulates compressional forces that fold and thrust rock upward, forming mountains at convergent boundaries."},{id:"5005-104",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Earth Science",q:"What is the theory of plate tectonics?",options:["Continents don't move","Earth's lithosphere is divided into plates that move over the asthenosphere","The Earth is flat","Mountains never change"],answer:1,explain:"Plate tectonics explains that Earth's outer shell (lithosphere) is broken into plates that float on the semi-fluid asthenosphere below."},{id:"5002-086",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Reading",q:"What is the rhyme scheme of a poem with lines ending in cat, hat, dog, log?",options:["ABAB","AAAA","AABB","ABBA"],answer:2,explain:"Cat and hat rhyme (A), dog and log rhyme (B), giving AABB pattern."},{id:"5002-087",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Reading",q:"What is the difference between connotation and denotation?",options:["They are the same","Connotation is literal; denotation is emotional","Neither has anything to do with meaning","Denotation is literal meaning; connotation is emotional association"],answer:3,explain:"Denotation is the dictionary definition. Connotation is the feeling or idea a word suggests beyond its literal meaning."},{id:"5002-088",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Reading",q:"A passage contrasts the narrator's childhood memories with present reality. What is the effect of this structure?",options:["It highlights change and loss over time","It confuses the reader","It has no literary purpose","It makes the passage longer"],answer:0,explain:"Juxtaposing past and present emphasizes how things have changed, creating themes of nostalgia, loss, or growth."},{id:"5002-089",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Reading",q:"Which strategy best builds students' reading stamina?",options:["Assigning the same text daily","Gradually increasing reading duration with varied texts","Reading only short passages","Avoiding difficult texts"],answer:1,explain:"Stamina builds gradually through extended, varied reading. Short passages don't challenge endurance. Repetition and avoidance limit growth."},{id:"5002-090",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Reading",q:"What is an epigraph?",options:["A chapter heading","A type of paragraph","A quote at the beginning of a work that sets the theme","A character's thoughts"],answer:2,explain:"An epigraph is a brief quotation at the start of a work, often from another author, that frames or hints at the theme."},{id:"5002-091",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Reading",q:"Why might an author use an unreliable narrator?",options:["To confuse readers","It is required by the genre","Because they cannot write well","To create dramatic irony and invite readers to question the story"],answer:3,explain:"Unreliable narrators engage readers actively, creating dramatic irony where readers discern truths the narrator cannot see."},{id:"5002-092",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Reading",q:"Students can decode but cannot summarize. Which approach helps?",options:["Teach the difference between important and minor details using graphic organizers","Read faster","More decoding practice","Skip summarizing"],answer:0,explain:"Summarization requires discriminating key ideas from details. Graphic organizers make this hierarchy visible."},{id:"5002-093",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Reading",q:"What is the function of foreshadowing?",options:["To introduce characters randomly","To hint at future events and create anticipation","To summarize the plot","To describe settings"],answer:1,explain:"Foreshadowing gives clues about what will happen later, building suspense and coherence."},{id:"5002-094",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Reading",q:"Which sentence contains clear onomatopoeia?",options:["The wind moved through the trees.","The bell was large.","The bell went ding-dong.","The cold felt bitter."],answer:2,explain:"'Ding-dong' imitates the sound of a bell and is therefore onomatopoeia. The other choices describe actions or qualities without reproducing a sound."},{id:"5002-095",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Reading",q:"A student reads a historical account that presents only one side of a conflict. What skill should the teacher develop?",options:["Creative writing","Memorization","Speed reading","Source criticism and looking for multiple perspectives"],answer:3,explain:"Evaluating sources for bias and seeking multiple perspectives is essential historical thinking."},{id:"5002-096",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Reading",q:"Which technique helps students visualize text?",options:["Asking students to draw or describe mental images from the text","Reading without attention","Reading quickly","Skipping descriptions"],answer:0,explain:"Visualization connects language to mental imagery, deepening comprehension and engagement."},{id:"5002-097",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Reading",q:"What is a palindrome?",options:["A punctuation mark","A word or phrase that reads the same backward and forward","A type of poem","A character type"],answer:1,explain:"Palindromes like 'madam' or 'racecar' read identically forwards and backwards."},{id:"5002-098",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Reading",q:"What is the effect of a non-linear narrative?",options:["It makes reading easier","It has no effect","It creates suspense and requires readers to piece together events","It is always confusing"],answer:2,explain:"Non-linear narratives (flashbacks, fragmented timelines) engage readers in reconstruction, creating mystery and thematic depth."},{id:"5002-099",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Reading",q:"Students struggle with abstract concepts in literature. Which support is most effective?",options:["Skip abstract texts","Define terms only","More abstract texts","Connect abstract ideas to concrete personal experiences"],answer:3,explain:"Grounding abstract concepts in students' lived experience makes them accessible and meaningful."},{id:"5002-100",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Reading",q:"What is a motif in literature?",options:["A recurring element that has symbolic significance","A character name","A plot twist","A single symbol"],answer:0,explain:"Motifs are recurring images, ideas, or elements that reinforce themes throughout a work."},{id:"5002-101",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Writing, Speaking, and Listening",q:"What is an adverb?",options:["A connecting word","A describing word that modifies verbs, adjectives, or other adverbs","A naming word","An action word"],answer:1,explain:"Adverbs modify verbs (run quickly), adjectives (very tall), or other adverbs (quite slowly)."},{id:"5002-102",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Writing, Speaking, and Listening",q:"Which sentence uses an idiom to mean that someone identified the exact issue?",options:["He kicked the bucket yesterday.","She hit the nail on the head with her argument.","The carpenter struck a nail with a hammer.","It is raining heavily outside."],answer:1,explain:"'Hit the nail on the head' is an idiom meaning to identify or describe something exactly. The literal carpenter sentence is not idiomatic, and the other choices express different meanings."},{id:"5002-103",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Writing, Speaking, and Listening",q:"A student's writing lacks voice and sounds mechanical. Which feedback is most helpful?",options:["'Write more personally'","Rewrite it for them","Tell them to use bigger words","Have the student read their work aloud and identify where it sounds like 'they' rather than a real person"],answer:3,explain:"Reading aloud helps students hear their own voice and identify where it sounds artificial. Vague advice or rewriting does not teach the skill."},{id:"5002-104",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Writing, Speaking, and Listening",q:"What is a run-on sentence?",options:["Two independent clauses joined incorrectly without proper punctuation","A very long sentence","A fragment","A sentence with many adjectives"],answer:0,explain:"Run-ons fuse independent clauses without proper conjunction or punctuation (comma splice or fused sentence)."},{id:"5002-105",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Writing, Speaking, and Listening",q:"What does a comma splice fix require?",options:["Removing the comma","Adding a coordinating conjunction after the comma, using a semicolon, or making two sentences","Adding an adjective","Adding a period only"],answer:1,explain:"Fixes include: comma + FANBOYS, semicolon, or period to separate the clauses properly."},{id:"5002-106",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Writing, Speaking, and Listening",q:"Which revision improves clarity without changing meaning: 'The utilization of the apparatus was performed by the student'?",options:["'Utilizing apparatus was student-performed.'","'The apparatus was utilized by the student in a performed manner.'","'The student utilized the apparatus.'","Original is best"],answer:2,explain:"Active voice with simple verbs improves clarity. The original uses nominalization ('utilization') and passive voice unnecessarily."},{id:"5002-107",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Writing, Speaking, and Listening",q:"Which strategy best teaches students to write effective introductions?",options:["Copying from examples","Writing intros last","Memorizing intro formulas","Analyzing strong introductions and identifying hooks, background, and thesis"],answer:3,explain:"Close reading of model introductions builds awareness of structure and technique better than rote formulas."},{id:"5002-108",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Writing, Speaking, and Listening",q:"What is a dangling participle?",options:["A participle phrase that doesn't logically modify the subject","A punctuation error","A type of noun","A correct sentence"],answer:0,explain:"Example: 'Walking to the store, the rain started.' The rain didn't walk\u2014the modifier dangles."},{id:"5002-109",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Writing, Speaking, and Listening",q:"Which sentence demonstrates correct use of the subjunctive?",options:["If I be you, I would go","If I were you, I would go","If I was you, I would go","If I am you, I will go"],answer:1,explain:"The subjunctive 'were' expresses a hypothetical contrary to fact. 'Was' is colloquial but incorrect in formal writing."},{id:"5002-110",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Writing, Speaking, and Listening",q:"A student writes: 'Me and him went to the store.' How should a teacher respond?",options:["'That is wrong, fix it'","Circle it in red","Model the correct form in conversation without shaming: 'Oh, you and he went?'","Ignore it"],answer:2,explain:"Recasting models correct usage naturally without drawing negative attention. Shaming or ignoring misses teaching moments."},{id:"5002-111",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Writing, Speaking, and Listening",q:"What is the purpose of an appendix?",options:["To summarize the paper","To list references","To state the thesis","To provide supplementary material too detailed for the main text"],answer:3,explain:"Appendices contain extra data, charts, or documents that support but are too detailed for the main body."},{id:"5002-112",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Writing, Speaking, and Listening",q:"Which is a subordinating conjunction?",options:["Because","But","Or","And"],answer:0,explain:"'Because' introduces a dependent clause. And, but, or are coordinating conjunctions (FANBOYS)."},{id:"5002-113",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Writing, Speaking, and Listening",q:"Why is revision different from editing?",options:["They are the same","Revision addresses content and organization; editing addresses mechanics","Revision is optional","Editing comes first"],answer:1,explain:"Revision rethinks and restructures content. Editing fixes surface errors. Good writing requires both, in that order."},{id:"5002-114",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Writing, Speaking, and Listening",q:"Students use vague language like 'stuff' and 'things.' Which intervention helps?",options:["Give them lists of fancy words","Tell them to be specific","Have them replace vague words with precise alternatives in context","Ignore vague language"],answer:2,explain:"Contextual replacement builds precision. Lists are abstract. Ignoring the issue leaves habits unchallenged."},{id:"5002-115",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Writing, Speaking, and Listening",q:"What is a transition word?",options:["A sentence starter only","A type of noun","A punctuation mark","A word that shows relationship between ideas"],answer:3,explain:"Transitions (however, therefore, furthermore) signal logical relationships between sentences and paragraphs."},{id:"5004-086",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"United States History, Government, and Citizenship",q:"What are the first three words of the Constitution?",options:["We the People","In God We Trust","When in the Course","The Senate and House"],answer:0,explain:"'We the People of the United States' opens the Preamble."},{id:"5004-087",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"United States History, Government, and Citizenship",q:"What is the purpose of impeachment?",options:["To elect a new president","To charge federal officials with misconduct","To pass laws","To interpret the Constitution"],answer:1,explain:"Impeachment is the House's power to charge officials; the Senate conducts the trial. It removes officials for 'Treason, Bribery, or other high Crimes and Misdemeanors.'"},{id:"5004-088",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"United States History, Government, and Citizenship",q:"Why was the Bill of Rights added to the Constitution?",options:["To create a monarchy","To increase presidential power","To address Anti-Federalist concerns about individual liberties","It was not added"],answer:2,explain:"Anti-Federalists demanded explicit protections for individual rights. The first ten amendments were ratified in 1791 to secure Constitution support."},{id:"5004-089",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"United States History, Government, and Citizenship",q:"Which activity best teaches students about civic participation?",options:["Reading about voting only","Drawing flags","Memorizing government structure","Having students identify a local issue and research how to influence policy"],answer:3,explain:"Authentic civic engagement connects curriculum to students' lives, making participation meaningful rather than abstract."},{id:"5004-090",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"United States History, Government, and Citizenship",q:"How long does a Supreme Court justice serve?",options:["Life tenure (during good behavior)","10 years","20 years","4 years"],answer:0,explain:"Article III grants federal judges life tenure during 'good Behaviour,' insulating them from political pressure."},{id:"5004-091",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"United States History, Government, and Citizenship",q:"What is the significance of Marbury v. Madison?",options:["It abolished slavery","It established judicial review","It granted women's suffrage","It created the Federal Reserve"],answer:1,explain:"Marbury v. Madison (1803) established the Supreme Court's power to declare laws unconstitutional\u2014judicial review."},{id:"5004-092",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"United States History, Government, and Citizenship",q:"Students think the Constitution is set in stone and cannot change. How should a teacher respond?",options:["Skip constitutional change","Agree with them","Show how amendments and court interpretations have adapted the Constitution","Tell them it is wrong"],answer:2,explain:"Amendments (27 total) and landmark cases show the Constitution is a living document that evolves."},{id:"5004-093",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"United States History, Government, and Citizenship",q:"What is the role of the House of Representatives?",options:["To interpret laws","Only to advise the President","To appoint judges","To initiate revenue bills and represent constituents by population"],answer:3,explain:"The House has exclusive power to initiate revenue bills and represents people proportionally. The Senate advises on treaties and appointments."},{id:"5004-094",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"United States History, Government, and Citizenship",q:"What is a veto?",options:["The President's power to reject legislation","A type of law","A tax","A court decision"],answer:0,explain:"The presidential veto allows the executive to reject bills passed by Congress. Congress can override with a two-thirds vote."},{id:"5004-095",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"United States History, Government, and Citizenship",q:"How does the Electoral College work?",options:["Congress directly chooses the President in every election","Each state receives electors equal to its total representation in Congress; D.C. receives three, and electors cast presidential votes","The Vice President assigns electors after the election","The national popular-vote winner automatically receives every electoral vote"],answer:1,explain:"A state\u2019s electors equal its number of representatives plus two senators, and the Twenty-third Amendment gives D.C. three electors. This allocation reflects population through House seats but is not purely proportional because every state also has two Senate-based electors."},{id:"5005-105",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Life Science",q:"What is the function of the nucleus?",options:["Protein synthesis","Cell movement","Contains DNA and controls cell activities","Energy production"],answer:2,explain:"The nucleus houses DNA and regulates gene expression, directing cell functions."},{id:"5005-106",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Life Science",q:"Why are wetlands important ecosystems?",options:["They have no ecological value","They are only beautiful","They produce oil","They filter pollutants, prevent floods, and provide habitat"],answer:3,explain:"Wetlands act as natural filters, reduce flooding, support biodiversity, and store carbon."},{id:"5005-107",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Life Science",q:"Students think that organisms evolve to meet their needs. Which explanation corrects this?",options:["Explain that random variations exist; environments select advantageous traits, organisms don't choose to change","Skip evolution","Agree with them","Say it is wrong"],answer:0,explain:"Evolution is not goal-directed. Random mutations arise; selection filters them. Organisms don't 'try' to adapt."},{id:"5005-108",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Life Science",q:"What is the role of decomposers?",options:["To eat other animals","To break down dead organic matter and recycle nutrients","To produce oxygen","To make food from sunlight"],answer:1,explain:"Decomposers (bacteria, fungi) recycle nutrients by breaking down dead matter, returning essentials to the ecosystem."},{id:"5005-109",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Life Science",q:"What is a habitat?",options:["A genetic trait","An animal's diet","The natural environment where an organism lives","A type of cell"],answer:2,explain:"A habitat provides the physical environment and resources an organism needs to survive."},{id:"5005-110",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Life Science",q:"Why do islands have unique species?",options:["Island species are stronger","Island animals cannot swim","Islands have more resources","Isolation limits gene flow, allowing independent evolution"],answer:3,explain:"Geographic isolation prevents interbreeding with mainland populations, leading to unique adaptations (adaptive radiation)."},{id:"5005-111",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Life Science",q:"Which demonstration best shows carrying capacity?",options:["Simulating a population growing until resources limit further growth","Listing species","Counting animals","Drawing animals"],answer:0,explain:"Simulation shows populations growing exponentially then leveling off when resources become limiting."},{id:"5005-112",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Life Science",q:"What is symbiosis?",options:["A weather pattern","Close, long-term interaction between different species","Competition between species","A type of rock"],answer:1,explain:"Symbiosis includes mutualism (both benefit), commensalism (one benefits, one unaffected), and parasitism (one benefits, one harmed)."},{id:"5005-113",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Life Science",q:"What is the difference between aerobic and anaerobic respiration?",options:["Aerobic occurs only in plants","Anaerobic produces more energy","Aerobic requires oxygen; anaerobic does not","They are the same"],answer:2,explain:"Aerobic respiration uses oxygen to produce ~36 ATP. Anaerobic (fermentation) produces only 2 ATP without oxygen."},{id:"5005-114",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Life Science",q:"Students confuse mitosis and meiosis. Which comparison helps most?",options:["Memorize definitions","Ignore the difference","Draw random cells","Use a table comparing: mitosis = 2 identical cells; meiosis = 4 genetically unique gametes"],answer:3,explain:"Side-by-side comparison highlights key differences: purpose, product, and genetic outcome."},{id:"5005-115",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Physical Science",q:"What is the unit of power?",options:["Watt","Joule","Newton","Pascal"],answer:0,explain:"Power is measured in Watts (W). One watt equals one joule per second."},{id:"5005-116",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Physical Science",q:"Why does a balloon stick to a wall after rubbing on hair?",options:["The balloon is magnetic","Static electricity creates an attractive force","Gravity pulls the balloon","The wall is sticky"],answer:1,explain:"Rubbing transfers electrons, creating static charge. The charged balloon induces opposite charge on the wall, causing attraction."},{id:"5005-117",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Physical Science",q:"Students think that energy can be created. Which principle corrects this?",options:["Tell them it's wrong","Skip energy topics","Demonstrate energy conservation: potential energy converts to kinetic but total energy stays constant","Let them believe it"],answer:2,explain:"Experiments with pendulums or roller coasters show energy transformation but conservation\u2014energy isn't created or destroyed."},{id:"5005-118",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Physical Science",q:"What is an element?",options:["A compound","A mixture","A solution","A substance made of only one type of atom"],answer:3,explain:"Elements are pure substances consisting of one type of atom (e.g., gold, oxygen). Compounds contain multiple elements chemically bonded."},{id:"5005-119",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Physical Science",q:"What is the chemical symbol for oxygen?",options:["O","On","Og","Ox"],answer:0,explain:"Oxygen's symbol is O. Other symbols: Ox (not real), On (not real), Og (oganesson)."},{id:"5005-120",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Physical Science",q:"How does pressure change with depth in water?",options:["Pressure stays constant","Pressure increases with depth due to water weight above","Pressure decreases with depth","Pressure becomes zero"],answer:1,explain:"Pressure = \u03C1gh\u2014pressure increases linearly with depth because more water weighs above."},{id:"5005-121",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Physical Science",q:"Which experiment best demonstrates conductivity?",options:["Measuring temperature","Mixing colors","Building a circuit with a bulb and testing different materials","Weighing objects"],answer:2,explain:"Testing materials in a circuit shows which conduct electricity (bulb lights) and which insulate (bulb stays dark)."},{id:"5005-122",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Physical Science",q:"What is the pH of pure water?",options:["0","14","1","7"],answer:3,explain:"Pure water is neutral at pH 7. Below 7 is acidic; above 7 is basic."},{id:"5005-123",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Physical Science",q:"What is an atom's nucleus made of?",options:["Protons and neutrons","Only protons","Electrons only","Only neutrons"],answer:0,explain:"The nucleus contains protons (positive charge) and neutrons (neutral). Electrons orbit outside."},{id:"5005-124",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Physical Science",q:"Why do mirrors reflect light but windows transmit it?",options:["Windows absorb all light","Mirrors have a metallic coating that reflects; glass transmits because it lacks this coating","Mirrors are thicker","Mirrors are magical"],answer:1,explain:"Mirrors have a reflective metal backing (silver/aluminum). Glass transmits because its molecular structure allows light photons to pass through."},{id:"5004-096",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"World History and Economics",q:"Which ancient Greek philosopher is known as the 'father of history'?",options:["Aristotle","Plato","Herodotus","Socrates"],answer:2,explain:"Herodotus (c. 484\u2013425 BCE) wrote 'The Histories' about the Greco-Persian Wars and is called the father of history."},{id:"5004-097",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"World History and Economics",q:"What was the main economic effect of the Agricultural Revolution?",options:["People became nomadic","Trade decreased","Cities disappeared","Surplus food enabled population growth and specialization"],answer:3,explain:"Farming surplus supported larger populations and allowed some people to specialize in non-food-production roles (artisans, rulers, priests)."},{id:"5004-098",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"World History and Economics",q:"Which approach best teaches students about global interdependence?",options:["Tracing the journey of a product from raw materials to final sale across continents","Reading about trade only","Listing resources by country","Memorizing country names"],answer:0,explain:"Product lifecycle tracing shows how economies are connected through supply chains."},{id:"5004-099",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"World History and Economics",q:"What is mercantilism?",options:["A religious movement","An economic system where colonies exist to benefit the mother country","A type of democracy","Free trade without restrictions"],answer:1,explain:"Mercantilism held that wealth was finite and colonies existed to enrich the ruling country through exports and restricted trade."},{id:"5004-100",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"World History and Economics",q:"Which civilization developed democracy first?",options:["Roman","Egyptian","Greek (Athens)","Persian"],answer:2,explain:"Athens developed direct democracy around 500 BCE, where citizens voted directly on legislation."},{id:"5004-101",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"World History and Economics",q:"What caused the Black Death to spread rapidly?",options:["Volcanic eruptions","War only","Alien visitation","Trade routes carrying infected fleas on rats"],answer:3,explain:"The Silk Road and maritime trade transported infected rats and fleas across Eurasia, causing pandemic mortality."},{id:"5004-102",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"World History and Economics",q:"Students think history is just dates and names. How should a teacher respond?",options:["Show how historical thinking involves causation, change over time, and evidence evaluation","Skip critical thinking","Tell them to memorize more","Agree with them"],answer:0,explain:"History is analytical\u2014examining causes, consequences, and sourcing\u2014not merely chronological recall."},{id:"5004-103",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"World History and Economics",q:"Which innovation supplied mechanical power to many early factories and later transformed rail and steamship transportation?",options:["The printing press","The improved steam engine","The wheel","The magnetic compass"],answer:1,explain:"Improved steam engines powered machinery independently of flowing water and later drove locomotives and steamships. The question specifies the role rather than claiming one invention was the only key innovation of industrialization."},{id:"5004-104",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"World History and Economics",q:"Which empire built the Colosseum?",options:["Persian","Greek","Roman","Ottoman"],answer:2,explain:"The Roman Colosseum (Flavian Amphitheatre) was built around 70-80 CE for gladiatorial contests."},{id:"5004-105",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"World History and Economics",q:"How did the Renaissance change European thought?",options:["It rejected science entirely","It returned focus to religious dogma","It eliminated art","It emphasized humanism, individualism, and secular learning"],answer:3,explain:"The Renaissance (14th-17th centuries) revived classical learning and emphasized human potential, questioning medieval scholasticism."},{id:"5002-116",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Reading",q:"What is the purpose of a table of contents?",options:["To list chapters and sections with page numbers","To define difficult words","To list references","To entertain readers"],answer:0,explain:"Tables of contents organize a book's structure, helping readers locate specific sections quickly."},{id:"5002-117",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Reading",q:"Why might an author use first-person narration?",options:["To create distance from the story","To provide intimate access to a character's thoughts and feelings","Because it is required","To confuse readers"],answer:1,explain:"First-person narration ('I') creates closeness and subjective perspective, letting readers experience events through one character's eyes."},{id:"5002-118",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Reading",q:"Students read but cannot recall what they read. Which strategy helps?",options:["Read faster","Read silently without stopping","Teach active reading: pause to summarize, ask questions, connect to prior knowledge","Skip difficult parts"],answer:2,explain:"Active reading strategies (summarizing, questioning, connecting) improve encoding and retention."},{id:"5002-119",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Reading",q:"What is a literary theme?",options:["A plot twist","The setting","A character's name","A central idea or message about life"],answer:3,explain:"Themes are universal ideas explored in literature (e.g., courage, friendship, justice), expressed through characters and plot."},{id:"5002-120",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Reading",q:"Which is an example of a reliable source?",options:["A peer-reviewed journal article","A social media post","A personal blog","An anonymous website"],answer:0,explain:"Peer-reviewed journals undergo expert evaluation, ensuring accuracy and credibility."},{id:"5002-121",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Reading",q:"What is the effect of using sensory details in writing?",options:["Confuses readers","Creates vivid imagery and engages readers' imagination","Has no effect","Makes text longer"],answer:1,explain:"Sensory details (sight, sound, smell, touch, taste) create immersive experiences that help readers visualize and feel the text."},{id:"5002-122",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Reading",q:"Which approach best develops critical reading skills?",options:["Memorizing text","Accepting text at face value","Teaching students to question author bias, evidence quality, and omitted perspectives","Reading quickly for plot"],answer:2,explain:"Critical reading involves evaluating sources for bias, evidence, and perspective\u2014essential for informed citizenship."},{id:"5002-123",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Reading",q:"What is an allegory?",options:["A type of poem","A biography","A joke","A story with a hidden moral or political meaning"],answer:3,explain:"Allegories use characters and events to represent abstract ideas (e.g., Animal Farm as political satire)."},{id:"5002-124",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Reading",q:"What is the glossary used for?",options:["Defining difficult words in a text","Listing characters","Summarizing chapters","Describing settings"],answer:0,explain:"Glossaries provide definitions of specialized or unfamiliar terms used in a text."},{id:"5002-125",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Reading",q:"Why is context important for understanding word meaning?",options:["Context only helps with poetry","Words have multiple meanings; surrounding text clarifies which meaning is intended","Context doesn't matter","Dictionaries are unnecessary"],answer:1,explain:"Context clues (surrounding words, sentences, paragraphs) help readers determine meaning when words are ambiguous or unfamiliar."},{id:"5003-061",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Numbers and Operations",q:"What is 3/4 expressed as a decimal?",options:["0.25","0.5","0.75","1.25"],answer:2,explain:"Absolute value measures distance from zero, so it is never negative. Therefore |-7| = 7; -7 is the original number, not its absolute value."},{id:"5003-062",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Numbers and Operations",q:"Why can you not divide by zero?",options:["It equals infinity","It equals one","Zero is too small","It is undefined because no number multiplied by zero equals a non-zero number"],answer:3,explain:"Division asks: 'What number times the divisor equals the dividend?' No number \xD7 0 = non-zero, so division by zero is undefined."},{id:"5003-063",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Numbers and Operations",q:"A student says 0.3 is the same as 0.30. Is this correct?",options:["Yes, they are equivalent\u2014trailing zeros after the decimal don't change value","No, they are different","Only in money","Depends on the context"],answer:0,explain:"0.3 = 3/10 = 30/100 = 0.30. Trailing zeros in decimals are placeholders that don't affect value."},{id:"5003-064",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Numbers and Operations",q:"Which number is irrational?",options:["0.5","\u221A2","3","0.333..."],answer:1,explain:"Irrational numbers cannot be expressed as fractions. \u221A2 \u2248 1.414... has non-repeating, non-terminating decimals. The others are rational."},{id:"5003-065",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Numbers and Operations",q:"What is the absolute value of -7?",options:["0","1/7","7","-7"],answer:2,explain:"Absolute value is distance from zero, always non-negative. |-7| = 7."},{id:"5003-066",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Numbers and Operations",q:"If a/b = c/d, then ad = bc. This property is called:",options:["Distributive property","Commutative property","Associative property","Cross-multiplication"],answer:3,explain:"Cross-multiplication solves proportions by multiplying diagonals: a/b = c/d \u2192 ad = bc."},{id:"5003-067",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Numbers and Operations",q:"Students think that multiplying always makes numbers bigger. How to correct?",options:["Show multiplication by fractions less than 1 (e.g., 1/2 \xD7 10 = 5) produces smaller results","Give more practice problems","Tell them they're wrong","Ignore the misconception"],answer:0,explain:"Counterexamples with proper fractions show multiplication doesn't always increase magnitude."},{id:"5003-068",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Numbers and Operations",q:"What is the square root of 144?",options:["11","12","10","14"],answer:1,explain:"12 \xD7 12 = 144, so \u221A144 = 12."},{id:"5003-069",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Numbers and Operations",q:"Which number is not a factor of 24?",options:["8","5","6","12"],answer:1,explain:"A factor divides 24 with no remainder: 8, 6, and 12 are factors, but 24 \xF7 5 is not a whole number. Therefore 5 is the only correct choice."},{id:"5003-070",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Numbers and Operations",q:"Why is the sum of two even numbers always even?",options:["Because odd numbers cancel out","Because all numbers are even","It is not always even","Because even numbers are 2n, and 2n + 2m = 2(n+m), which is divisible by 2"],answer:3,explain:"Even numbers have form 2n. Sum: 2n + 2m = 2(n+m), which is 2 times an integer\u2014by definition even."},{id:"5004-106",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"Geography, Anthropology, and Sociology",q:"What is the largest desert in the world?",options:["Antarctic Desert","Gobi","Kalahari","Sahara"],answer:0,explain:"Antarctica is the world's largest desert (by definition, deserts receive little precipitation). The Sahara is the largest hot desert."},{id:"5004-107",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"Geography, Anthropology, and Sociology",q:"What is the difference between population density and population distribution?",options:["They are the same","Density is people per unit area; distribution is how people are spaced across an area","Density applies only to cities","Distribution is always higher"],answer:1,explain:"Density = number/area. Distribution = pattern of spread (clustered, scattered, linear)."},{id:"5004-108",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"Geography, Anthropology, and Sociology",q:"Which activity best teaches cultural relativism?",options:["Judging other cultures by your standards","Ignoring cultural differences","Analyzing cultural practices within their own historical and environmental context","Comparing cultures to find 'superior' ones"],answer:2,explain:"Cultural relativism understands practices within their own context rather than judging by external standards."},{id:"5004-109",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"Geography, Anthropology, and Sociology",q:"What causes a monsoon?",options:["Earthquakes","Volcanic eruptions","Solar flares","Seasonal wind reversals bringing heavy rainfall"],answer:3,explain:"Monsoons are seasonal wind patterns that reverse direction, bringing wet seasons to South and Southeast Asia."},{id:"5004-110",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"Geography, Anthropology, and Sociology",q:"Which river carries the greatest average volume of water to the ocean?",options:["Nile","Yangtze","Mississippi","Amazon"],answer:3,explain:"The Amazon River has by far the greatest average discharge of any river. This avoids the disputed question of whether the Nile or Amazon is longest under different measurement methods."},{id:"5004-111",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"Geography, Anthropology, and Sociology",q:"What is urban sprawl?",options:["Building skyscrapers","Uncontrolled expansion of urban areas into surrounding rural land","Cleaning up cities","Population decline"],answer:1,explain:"Urban sprawl is low-density, car-dependent development spreading beyond city boundaries, consuming farmland and natural habitats."},{id:"5004-112",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"Geography, Anthropology, and Sociology",q:"Students think that 'third world' means 'less developed' in a negative way. How should a teacher address this?",options:["Agree with the term","Use the term without explanation","Explain the term's Cold War origins and that it is outdated and misleading","Ignore the term"],answer:2,explain:"'Third World' originated during the Cold War to describe non-aligned nations and is now considered outdated and imprecise."},{id:"5004-113",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"Geography, Anthropology, and Sociology",q:"What is a time zone?",options:["A seasonal change","An hour of daylight","A type of clock","A region following a uniform standard time, approximately 15\xB0 of longitude wide"],answer:3,explain:"Time zones divide Earth into 24 regions, each roughly 15\xB0 wide, with uniform local time."},{id:"5004-114",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"Geography, Anthropology, and Sociology",q:"Which continent is the smallest by land area?",options:["Australia","South America","Antarctica","Europe"],answer:0,explain:"Australia is the smallest continent at approximately 7.7 million km\xB2."},{id:"5004-115",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"Geography, Anthropology, and Sociology",q:"What is the 'demographic transition model'?",options:["A type of map","A model showing how populations change from high birth/death rates to low birth/death rates as they develop","A migration pattern","A population census"],answer:1,explain:"The demographic transition describes stages: high birth/death \u2192 declining death \u2192 declining birth \u2192 low birth/death, as societies industrialize."},{id:"5005-125",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Earth Science",q:"What is the outer layer of the Earth called?",options:["Asthenosphere","Mantle","Crust","Core"],answer:2,explain:"The crust is Earth's thin outer solid shell. The mantle is below it; the core is at the center."},{id:"5005-126",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Earth Science",q:"Why do continents fit together like puzzle pieces?",options:["It is a coincidence","God made them that way","Water eroded them","They were once connected in a supercontinent"],answer:3,explain:"Continental drift theory (Wegener) proposed that all continents were once joined in Pangaea and have since drifted apart."},{id:"5005-127",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Earth Science",q:"Students think that erosion and deposition are the same. How to distinguish?",options:["Show that erosion moves material; deposition drops it in a new location","Use complex terminology","Say they are the same","Ignore the confusion"],answer:0,explain:"Erosion = transport. Deposition = settling. They are sequential parts of the same process but distinct steps."},{id:"5005-128",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Earth Science",q:"Which cloud type is most directly associated with thunderstorms, lightning, and heavy precipitation?",options:["Cirrus","Cumulus humilis","Nimbostratus","Cumulonimbus"],answer:3,explain:"Cumulonimbus clouds grow vertically and are directly associated with thunderstorms, lightning, strong winds, and heavy precipitation. Cirrus clouds may occur during fair weather or precede changing weather, so they are not a reliable fair-weather indicator."},{id:"5005-129",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Earth Science",q:"What is the Coriolis effect?",options:["Ocean currents move water","Gravity pulls objects down","Earth's rotation deflects moving objects to the right in the Northern Hemisphere","Wind blows from high to low pressure"],answer:2,explain:"Earth's rotation causes deflection: right in the Northern Hemisphere, left in the Southern. This affects winds and ocean currents."},{id:"5005-130",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Earth Science",q:"How does the water cycle relate to weather?",options:["Weather only affects oceans","The water cycle only happens in summer","They are unrelated","Water cycle drives precipitation, humidity, and cloud formation\u2014key weather elements"],answer:3,explain:"The water cycle (evaporation, condensation, precipitation) is the engine of weather systems, distributing heat and moisture."},{id:"5005-131",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Earth Science",q:"Which classroom model best represents glacier flow while acknowledging the model\u2019s limitations?",options:["Observe a thick, slow-moving viscous material flowing down a slope and compare it with time-lapse glacier data","Push a rigid tray of ice cubes across a desk","Melt an ice cube rapidly with hot water","Freeze a cup of still water"],answer:0,explain:"A viscous material can model slow deformation and flow, while time-lapse data connect the model to real glacier movement. Rigid ice cubes sliding as a block do not represent internal deformation of glacier ice."},{id:"5005-132",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Earth Science",q:"What is the Rock Cycle's main principle?",options:["Rocks dissolve in water","Rocks continuously transform between types through geologic processes","Rocks never change","Only igneous rocks exist"],answer:1,explain:"The rock cycle shows how igneous, sedimentary, and metamorphic rocks transform into one another through melting, cooling, weathering, and metamorphism."},{id:"5005-133",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Earth Science",q:"What causes wind?",options:["Earth's rotation only","Volcanic activity","Air moving from high to low pressure areas","Ocean currents"],answer:2,explain:"Wind is air moving from high-pressure to low-pressure areas, driven by uneven solar heating."},{id:"5005-134",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Earth Science",q:"Why are fossils found in mountain rock layers?",options:["Animals lived on mountains","Mountains were always above sea level","Fossils float upward","Mountains formed from ocean floor uplift; ancient marine organisms were preserved in sediment"],answer:3,explain:"Tectonic uplift raises ancient seafloor sediments (containing marine fossils) to form mountains."},{id:"5004-116",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"World History and Economics",q:"Which civilization built the Great Wall?",options:["Chinese","Indian","Japanese","Korean"],answer:0,explain:"The Great Wall of China was built over centuries by various Chinese dynasties, primarily for defense against northern invaders."},{id:"5004-117",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"World History and Economics",q:"What was the main economic motivation for European exploration?",options:["Religious conversion only","Access to Asian spices and wealth through new trade routes","Escape from overcrowding","Scientific curiosity"],answer:1,explain:"European powers sought direct access to Asian spice trades, bypassing Ottoman-controlled land routes."},{id:"5004-118",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"World History and Economics",q:"Students think that ancient civilizations were 'primitive.' How should a teacher respond?",options:["Ignore the comment","Tell them they are wrong without evidence","Show the sophistication of ancient engineering, governance, and mathematics","Agree with them"],answer:2,explain:"Demonstrating achievements (pyramids, Roman roads, Mayan calendars) counters misconceptions about ancient 'primitiveness.'"},{id:"5004-119",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"World History and Economics",q:"What is capitalism?",options:["Government ownership of all property","Barter system","Communal living","Private ownership of production with market-driven economics"],answer:3,explain:"Capitalism features private ownership, profit motive, and market competition as the primary economic organizing principle."},{id:"5004-120",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"United States History, Government, and Citizenship",q:"Which war was fought between North and South in the United States?",options:["Civil War","Revolutionary War","War of 1812","World War I"],answer:0,explain:"The American Civil War (1861-1865) was fought between the Union (North) and Confederacy (South)."},{id:"5004-121",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"World History and Economics",q:"What was the significance of the Magna Carta?",options:["It abolished feudalism","It limited royal power and established rule of law","It created the US Constitution","It established democracy"],answer:1,explain:"The Magna Carta (1215) established that even the king is subject to the law\u2014a foundational principle for constitutional governance."},{id:"5004-122",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"World History and Economics",q:"Which strategy best helps students understand cause and effect in history?",options:["Memorizing dates","Listing events chronologically","Analyzing primary sources to trace multiple causes and consequences","Reading summaries"],answer:2,explain:"Primary source analysis reveals complexity of causation better than rote memorization or chronological listing."},{id:"5004-123",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"World History and Economics",q:"What is the Cold War?",options:["A warm conflict","A trade agreement","A religious war","A geopolitical tension between US and USSR without direct military conflict"],answer:3,explain:"The Cold War (1947-1991) was ideological and political tension between the US-led West and Soviet-led East, fought through proxy wars and arms races."},{id:"5004-124",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"World History and Economics",q:"Who was the first Emperor of Rome?",options:["Augustus","Nero","Julius Caesar","Constantine"],answer:0,explain:"Augustus (Octavian) became the first Roman Emperor in 27 BCE after defeating Mark Antony and Cleopatra."},{id:"5004-125",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"World History and Economics",q:"Why did the Byzantine Empire fall?",options:["It was conquered by Rome","Ottoman Turks captured Constantinople in 1453 after centuries of decline","It never fell","It moved to America"],answer:1,explain:"The Byzantine (Eastern Roman) Empire fell when Mehmed II captured Constantinople in 1453, ending the 1000+ year empire."},{id:"5005-135",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Life Science",q:"What is evolution?",options:["Death of species","Learning new behaviors","Change in heritable traits of populations over generations","Individuals changing during lifetime"],answer:2,explain:"Evolution is change in allele frequencies in populations over time, not individual adaptation during a lifetime."},{id:"5005-136",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Life Science",q:"Why are keystone species important?",options:["They are the largest animals","They are always predators","They produce the most oxygen","They have disproportionate effect on ecosystem structure relative to abundance"],answer:3,explain:"Keystone species (e.g., sea otters, wolves) maintain biodiversity and ecosystem function despite low abundance."},{id:"5005-137",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Life Science",q:"Students think that evolution is 'just a theory.' How should a teacher clarify?",options:["Explain that in science, a theory is a well-supported explanation, not a guess","Tell them to stop asking","Agree with them","Skip the topic"],answer:0,explain:"Scientific theories (evolution, germ theory, cell theory) are robust explanations supported by extensive evidence\u2014not hypotheses or guesses."},{id:"5005-138",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Life Science",q:"What is a biosphere?",options:["A single organism","The global sum of all ecosystems","An atmosphere layer","A type of rock"],answer:1,explain:"The biosphere encompasses all living organisms and their environments on Earth."},{id:"5005-139",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Life Science",q:"What is genetic diversity?",options:["Same genes in all individuals","Ecosystem size","Variation in genes within a population","Number of species"],answer:2,explain:"Genetic diversity is the variety of alleles within a population, essential for adaptation and survival."},{id:"5005-140",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Life Science",q:"What is coevolution?",options:["Genetic drift","Extinction","Random mutation","Reciprocal evolutionary change between interacting species"],answer:3,explain:"Coevolution occurs when two species influence each other's evolution (e.g., flowers and pollinators, predators and prey)."},{id:"5005-141",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Life Science",q:"What is an ecosystem?",options:["Community of organisms plus their physical environment","Only non-living things","Only living things","A single species"],answer:0,explain:"Ecosystems include biotic (living) and abiotic (non-living) components interacting as a system."},{id:"5005-142",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Life Science",q:"What is carrying capacity?",options:["How much weight animals can carry","Maximum population size an environment can sustain","Population growth rate","Number of species"],answer:1,explain:"Carrying capacity (K) is the maximum population size that resources can support indefinitely."},{id:"5005-143",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Life Science",q:"Students think that organisms become 'better' over time. How to correct?",options:["Agree with them","Ignore it","Explain that evolution has no goal; 'better' means 'better adapted to current environment,' not progress","Say it's complicated"],answer:2,explain:"Evolution is adaptation to current conditions, not progression toward perfection. Environments change, and what was 'better' may become disadvantageous."},{id:"5005-144",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Physical Science",q:"What is Newton's first law?",options:["Gravity pulls objects down","F = ma","Every action has an equal reaction","An object at rest stays at rest unless acted on by a force"],answer:3,explain:"Newton's First Law (inertia): An object remains at rest or in uniform motion unless acted upon by an external force."},{id:"5005-145",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Physical Science",q:"Why does a object weigh less on the Moon?",options:["Moon's gravity is weaker (about 1/6 of Earth's)","There is no air","It floats","It has less mass"],answer:0,explain:"Weight = mass \xD7 gravity. The Moon's smaller mass produces weaker gravitational acceleration, so objects weigh less but have the same mass."},{id:"5005-146",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Physical Science",q:"Students think that heavier objects fall faster. Which demonstration corrects this?",options:["Calculate weight only","Drop a heavy and light object in vacuum chamber\u2014they fall together","Show a feather falling slowly","Tell them they're wrong"],answer:1,explain:"In vacuum, all objects fall at the same rate regardless of mass. This directly contradicts the misconception."},{id:"5005-147",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Physical Science",q:"What is the speed of sound in air approximately?",options:["300 m/s","100 m/s","343 m/s","300,000 km/s"],answer:2,explain:"Sound travels at approximately 343 m/s in air at 20\xB0C. Light travels at 3\xD710\u2078 m/s."},{id:"5005-148",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Physical Science",q:"What is an element on the periodic table?",options:["A solution","A compound","A mixture","A pure substance with one type of atom"],answer:3,explain:"Elements are fundamental substances consisting of atoms with the same number of protons."},{id:"5005-149",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Physical Science",q:"What happens to voltage in a series circuit with multiple resistors?",options:["Voltage divides across resistors proportionally to resistance","Voltage increases","Voltage becomes zero","Voltage stays the same across each"],answer:0,explain:"In series circuits, voltage drops across each resistor sum to the total. Each drop is V = IR for that resistor."},{id:"5005-150",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Physical Science",q:"Which model best represents electrical current?",options:["Light beams","Water flowing through pipes","Sound waves","Magnetic fields"],answer:1,explain:"The water flow analogy helps students visualize current (flow rate), voltage (pressure), and resistance (pipe narrowness)."},{id:"5005-151",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Physical Science",q:"What is the unit of frequency?",options:["Meter","Newton","Hertz","Joule"],answer:2,explain:"Frequency is measured in Hertz (Hz), meaning cycles per second."},{id:"5005-152",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Physical Science",q:"What is the chemical symbol for iron?",options:["Io","Ir","In","Fe"],answer:3,explain:"Iron's symbol is Fe (from Latin 'ferrum'). Iridium is Ir. Indium is In."},{id:"5005-153",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Physical Science",q:"Why does a lens focus light?",options:["Refraction bends light rays to converge at a focal point","Light stops at the lens","It reflects light","It absorbs light"],answer:0,explain:"Lenses refract (bend) light due to change in speed when passing through glass/plastic, converging rays to a focal point."},{id:"5004-126",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"United States History, Government, and Citizenship",q:"What is the Bill of Rights?",options:["The original Constitution","The first ten amendments protecting individual liberties","A state law","A list of taxes"],answer:1,explain:"The Bill of Rights (1791) comprises the first ten amendments, guaranteeing freedoms of speech, religion, press, assembly, and due process."},{id:"5004-127",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"United States History, Government, and Citizenship",q:"What is the significance of Brown v. Board of Education?",options:["It created the Supreme Court","It granted voting rights","It declared school segregation unconstitutional, overturning Plessy v. Ferguson","It established segregation"],answer:2,explain:"Brown v. Board (1954) ruled that 'separate but equal' educational facilities are inherently unequal, mandating desegregation."},{id:"5004-128",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"United States History, Government, and Citizenship",q:"Students think that the Constitution is only for adults. How to address?",options:["Tell them to read it","Agree with them","Ignore the misconception","Show that the Constitution protects all persons, including children (e.g., 14th Amendment due process)"],answer:3,explain:"Constitutional protections apply to all persons within US jurisdiction, not just citizens or adults."},{id:"5004-129",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"United States History, Government, and Citizenship",q:"What is the Electoral College?",options:["An body of electors chosen by states to elect the President","A group of voters who directly elect the President","A Congress committee","A court panel"],answer:0,explain:"The Electoral College consists of 538 electors chosen by states; a candidate needs 270 to win the presidency."},{id:"5004-130",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"United States History, Government, and Citizenship",q:"How many branches does the US government have?",options:["Four","Three","Two","Five"],answer:1,explain:"The US government has three branches: Legislative (Congress), Executive (President), Judicial (Supreme Court)."},{id:"5004-131",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"United States History, Government, and Citizenship",q:"What is judicial review?",options:["Judges reviewing elections","The President reviewing laws","Courts' power to declare laws unconstitutional","Congress reviewing court decisions"],answer:2,explain:"Established in Marbury v. Madison (1803), judicial review allows courts to invalidate laws conflicting with the Constitution."},{id:"5004-132",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"United States History, Government, and Citizenship",q:"Which activity best teaches students about civil disobedience?",options:["Ignoring the topic","Memorizing definitions","Writing essays only","Studying historical examples (Selma, Gandhi) and discussing moral reasoning"],answer:3,explain:"Historical case studies provide concrete examples of principled civil disobedience and its impact on social change."},{id:"5004-133",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"United States History, Government, and Citizenship",q:"What is the role of the Supreme Court?",options:["Interpreting laws and determining constitutionality","Commanding the military","Collecting taxes","Making laws"],answer:0,explain:"The Supreme Court is the highest judicial authority, interpreting laws and reviewing constitutionality of legislation and executive actions."},{id:"5004-134",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"United States History, Government, and Citizenship",q:"What is a Amendment?",options:["A treaty","A change or addition to the Constitution","A law","A court case"],answer:1,explain:"Amendments are formal changes to the Constitution, requiring proposal by Congress and ratification by states."},{id:"5004-135",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"United States History, Government, and Citizenship",q:"Why is the Commerce Clause important?",options:["It only applies to states","It regulates international trade only","It gives Congress power to regulate interstate commerce, expanding federal authority","It prevents all trade"],answer:2,explain:"The Commerce Clause (Article I, Section 8) has been interpreted broadly to allow federal regulation of activities affecting interstate commerce."},{id:"5002-126",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Reading",q:"What is the purpose of footnotes?",options:["To summarize chapters","To decorate pages","To list characters","To provide citations and additional information at the bottom of pages"],answer:3,explain:"Footnotes cite sources and add explanatory notes without interrupting the main text flow."},{id:"5002-127",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Reading",q:"Why might an author use stream of consciousness?",options:["To simulate the flow of a character's thoughts and perceptions","Because it is required","To make the story shorter","To confuse readers"],answer:0,explain:"Stream of consciousness creates interiority, immersing readers in a character's unfiltered mental experience."},{id:"5002-128",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Reading",q:"Students struggle with metaphor comprehension. Which strategy helps?",options:["Memorize definitions","Have students draw literal and figurative meanings side by side","Read faster","Skip metaphors"],answer:1,explain:"Visual comparison of literal vs. figurative meaning helps students grasp metaphorical thinking."},{id:"5002-129",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Reading",q:"What is the difference between a novel and a short story?",options:["They are the same","Short stories have no plot","Novels are longer with more complex plots and characters; short stories are condensed","Novels are always nonfiction"],answer:2,explain:"Novels develop plot and character over greater length; short stories focus on a single effect or moment."},{id:"5002-130",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Reading",q:"What is an allusion?",options:["A character type","A type of rhyme","A punctuation mark","A reference to another work, event, or person"],answer:3,explain:"Allusions invoke external references (Biblical, mythological, literary) to enrich meaning."},{id:"5002-131",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Reading",q:"Why is point of view important in understanding a text?",options:["It shapes what information is available and influences reader sympathy","It only affects length","It doesn't matter","It determines the genre"],answer:0,explain:"POV controls access to thoughts and facts, shaping interpretation and emotional response."},{id:"5002-132",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Reading",q:"Which approach best develops inferential thinking?",options:["Reading silently","Using 'reading between the lines' prompts that require evidence-based conclusions","Asking only literal questions","Memorizing plots"],answer:1,explain:"Inference requires combining textual clues with prior knowledge\u2014targeted prompts build this skill."},{id:"5002-133",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Reading",q:"What is satire?",options:["A news report","A love poem","Humor used to criticize flaws in society or individuals","A serious essay"],answer:2,explain:"Satire uses irony, exaggeration, or ridicule to expose and criticize vice or folly."},{id:"5002-134",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Reading",q:"What is the difference between setting and plot?",options:["They are the same","Neither matters","Plot is where; setting is when","Setting is time/place; plot is the sequence of events"],answer:3,explain:"Setting establishes context (time, place, social environment); plot is the causal sequence of events."},{id:"5002-135",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Reading",q:"Why do readers bring different interpretations to the same text?",options:["Because readers' backgrounds, experiences, and contexts shape meaning-making","Because authors write badly","Because texts are unclear","Interpretations should be identical"],answer:0,explain:"Reader-response theory emphasizes that meaning is co-constructed between text and reader's unique perspective."},{id:"5003-071",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Algebraic Thinking",q:"What is the value of x in 2x + 4 = 10?",options:["2","3","7","4"],answer:1,explain:"Subtract 4: 2x = 6. Divide by 2: x = 3."},{id:"5003-072",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Algebraic Thinking",q:"What is the slope-intercept form of a line?",options:["m = y/x","x = my + b","y = mx + b","y = bx + m"],answer:2,explain:"y = mx + b expresses slope (m) and y-intercept (b) explicitly."},{id:"5003-073",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Algebraic Thinking",q:"Students write x + 5 = 12 as x = 17. What misconception is this?",options:["Division error","Correct reasoning","Multiplication confusion","Adding instead of subtracting to isolate x"],answer:3,explain:"The student added 5 to both sides instead of subtracting, showing inverse operation confusion."},{id:"5003-074",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Algebraic Thinking",q:"What is the y-intercept of y = -3x + 7?",options:["7","-7","-3","3"],answer:0,explain:"The y-intercept is the constant term: 7. The slope is -3."},{id:"5003-075",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Algebraic Thinking",q:"Simplify: 3(x + 2) - 4",options:["3x - 2","3(x + 2)","3x + 2","3x + 6"],answer:2,explain:"Distribute: 3x + 6 - 4 = 3x + 2."},{id:"5003-076",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Algebraic Thinking",q:"What does the slope represent in a real-world context?",options:["Time elapsed","Starting value","Rate of change","Total distance"],answer:2,explain:"Slope = rise/run represents the rate of change (e.g., miles per hour, dollars per item)."},{id:"5003-077",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Algebraic Thinking",q:"Which representation best helps students understand solving equations?",options:["Watching videos","Reading definitions","Memorizing steps","Using balance scales to show equality is maintained"],answer:3,explain:"Balance models make the concept of equality tangible\u2014whatever you do to one side, you must do to the other."},{id:"5003-078",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Algebraic Thinking",q:"What is a coefficient?",options:["The number multiplying a variable","A constant","A variable","An exponent"],answer:0,explain:"In 5x, 5 is the coefficient\u2014the number multiplying the variable x."},{id:"5003-079",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Algebraic Thinking",q:"What is the solution to 4x = 20?",options:["80","5","4","16"],answer:1,explain:"Divide both sides by 4: x = 5."},{id:"5003-080",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Algebraic Thinking",q:"Which system has no solution?",options:["y = x + 1 and y = -x + 1","y = 3 and x = 2","y = 2x + 1 and y = 2x + 3","y = x and y = 2x"],answer:2,explain:"Parallel lines (same slope, different intercepts) never intersect\u2014no solution. Lines in B, C, D intersect."},{id:"5004-136",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"Geography, Anthropology, and Sociology",q:"What is latitude?",options:["East-west measurement","Altitude","Distance from poles only","North-south measurement from the equator"],answer:3,explain:"Latitude measures angular distance north or south of the equator (0\xB0 to 90\xB0)."},{id:"5004-137",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"Geography, Anthropology, and Sociology",q:"What is the Prime Meridian's significance?",options:["It is 0\xB0 longitude, the reference for measuring east-west position","It marks the date line","It divides hemispheres only","It is the equator"],answer:0,explain:"The Prime Meridian (0\xB0 longitude) through Greenwich, England, is the reference for time zones and geographic coordinates."},{id:"5004-138",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"Geography, Anthropology, and Sociology",q:"Students think that maps are objective representations. How to correct?",options:["Ignore projection issues","Discuss how map projections and choices involve perspective and bias","Tell them maps are lies","Agree with them"],answer:1,explain:"All maps involve distortion; choosing a projection involves value judgments about what to emphasize."},{id:"5004-139",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"Geography, Anthropology, and Sociology",q:"What is a continent?",options:["A country","An island","One of Earth's large continuous landmasses","Any landmass"],answer:2,explain:"Continents are Earth's major landmasses (traditionally 7: Asia, Africa, North America, South America, Antarctica, Europe, Australia)."},{id:"5004-140",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"Geography, Anthropology, and Sociology",q:"What is the Tropic of Capricorn?",options:["0\xB0 latitude","90\xB0N latitude","The equator","23.5\xB0S latitude"],answer:3,explain:"The Tropic of Capricorn is at approximately 23.5\xB0S, the southernmost point where the sun can be directly overhead."},{id:"5004-141",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"Geography, Anthropology, and Sociology",q:"What causes erosion?",options:["Wind and water moving soil and rock","Plant growth","Volcanic eruption only","Animal digging"],answer:0,explain:"Erosion is the transport of material by agents like water, wind, ice, or gravity."},{id:"5004-142",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"Geography, Anthropology, and Sociology",q:"Which approach best teaches students about environmental justice?",options:["Memorizing facts","Investigating local environmental issues and their distribution across communities","Ignoring local issues","Reading textbooks"],answer:1,explain:"Local investigations connect abstract concepts to students' lived experiences and civic agency."},{id:"5004-143",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"Geography, Anthropology, and Sociology",q:"What is the International Date Line?",options:["A boundary between countries","The equator","Approximately 180\xB0 longitude where the date changes","The Prime Meridian"],answer:2,explain:"The International Date Line (approx. 180\xB0) marks where one day ends and the next begins."},{id:"5004-144",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"Geography, Anthropology, and Sociology",q:"What is a climate zone?",options:["A weather forecast","A type of animal","A season","A region with characteristic temperature and precipitation patterns"],answer:3,explain:"Climate zones (tropical, temperate, polar, etc.) are defined by long-term patterns of temperature and precipitation."},{id:"5004-145",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"Geography, Anthropology, and Sociology",q:"What is the 'tragedy of the commons'?",options:["Overuse of shared resources by individuals acting in self-interest","Shared ownership","A common garden","A type of ecosystem"],answer:0,explain:"The tragedy of the commons describes how shared resources are depleted when individuals pursue self-interest without regulation."},{id:"5005-154",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Earth Science",q:"What is the mantle?",options:["The inner core","The thick layer between crust and core","The atmosphere","Earth's outer crust"],answer:1,explain:"The mantle is Earth's thickest layer, between the crust and core, composed of hot, semi-solid rock."},{id:"5005-155",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Earth Science",q:"Why do tectonic plates move?",options:["Ocean currents push them","They float on water","Mantle convection currents transfer heat and drive plate motion","Earth's rotation spins them"],answer:2,explain:"Heat from Earth's interior drives convection in the mantle, which drags plates above."},{id:"5005-156",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Earth Science",q:"Students think volcanoes only explode. How to correct?",options:["Tell them they're wrong","Agree with them","Skip volcanoes","Show examples of both explosive and effusive (flowing lava) eruptions"],answer:3,explain:"Volcanic activity ranges from gentle lava flows to violent explosions\u2014both are natural expressions of magma reaching the surface."},{id:"5005-157",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Earth Science",q:"What is a fault?",options:["A crack in Earth's crust where movement occurs","A mountain range","An earthquake","A type of volcano"],answer:0,explain:"Faults are fractures in Earth's crust along which blocks of rock move relative to each other."},{id:"5005-158",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Earth Science",q:"What is the asthenosphere?",options:["The inner core","The semi-fluid layer of the upper mantle below the lithosphere","The hard outer crust","The atmosphere"],answer:1,explain:"The asthenosphere is a ductile, partially molten layer that allows tectonic plates to move."},{id:"5005-159",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Earth Science",q:"How do mountains form at convergent boundaries?",options:["Earthquakes lift them","Erosion builds them","Collision of continental plates pushes rock upward","Volcanoes alone create all mountains"],answer:2,explain:"Convergent boundaries compress and thrust crust upward, forming fold mountains (e.g., Himalayas)."},{id:"5005-160",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Earth Science",q:"Which demonstration best shows seismic wave behavior?",options:["Shaking a rope","Throwing balls","Pouring water","Slinking a spring to show P and S waves"],answer:3,explain:"Slinky demonstrations effectively model longitudinal (P) and transverse (S) wave motion."},{id:"5005-161",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Earth Science",q:"What is the lithosphere?",options:["Earth's rigid outer layer including crust and upper mantle","The inner core","The ocean","The atmosphere"],answer:0,explain:"The lithosphere is Earth's rigid outer shell, broken into tectonic plates."},{id:"5005-162",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Earth Science",q:"Why do many subduction zones produce volcanic arcs?",options:["Continents melt completely when they collide","Water released from the subducting plate lowers the melting point of rock in the overlying mantle, producing magma","Every volcano is caused by a mantle plume","Ocean water directly turns into magma"],answer:1,explain:"As a plate subducts, water and other volatiles are released into the mantle wedge. These lower the rock\u2019s melting temperature, causing partial melting and magma that can rise to form volcanoes."},{id:"5005-163",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Life Science",q:"What is natural selection?",options:["Random mutation","Species extinction","Differential survival and reproduction based on heritable traits","Humans choosing traits"],answer:2,explain:"Natural selection is the process by which organisms better adapted to their environment tend to survive and reproduce more."},{id:"5005-164",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Life Science",q:"What is the role of biodiversity?",options:["It is unnecessary","It causes extinction","It only matters for plants","It increases ecosystem resilience and stability"],answer:3,explain:"Biodiversity provides ecosystem services, resilience to disturbance, and genetic resources."},{id:"5005-165",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Life Science",q:"Students think that evolution means 'progress.' How to correct?",options:["Show that evolution adapts organisms to current conditions, not toward 'perfection'","Ignore it","Say it's too complex","Agree with them"],answer:0,explain:"Evolution has no direction or goal\u2014adaptation to current environment is sufficient; 'progress' implies purpose."},{id:"5005-166",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Life Science",q:"What is an ecological niche?",options:["A predator","An organism's role and position in its ecosystem","A physical location","A type of habitat"],answer:1,explain:"A niche encompasses an organism's habitat, diet, behavior, and interactions\u2014its 'job' in the ecosystem."},{id:"5005-167",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Life Science",q:"What is an adaptation?",options:["A random change","A mutation","A trait that increases survival and reproduction in a specific environment","A learned behavior"],answer:2,explain:"Adaptations are heritable traits shaped by natural selection that enhance fitness in particular environments."},{id:"5005-168",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Life Science",q:"Why do antibiotics lose effectiveness over time?",options:["Bacteria become immune","Bacteria evolve resistance through choice","Antibiotics expire","Natural selection favors resistant bacteria, reducing antibiotic efficacy"],answer:3,explain:"Antibiotic resistance is evolution in action: random mutations confer resistance; antibiotics select for resistant strains."},{id:"5005-169",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Life Science",q:"Which lab best demonstrates photosynthesis?",options:["Observing oxygen bubbles from aquatic plants in light","Counting leaves","Measuring root length","Boiling plants"],answer:0,explain:"Aquatic plants (elodea) release visible oxygen bubbles during photosynthesis in light."},{id:"5005-170",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Life Science",q:"What is the food chain?",options:["A recycling process","Linear sequence of who eats whom in an ecosystem","A type of exercise","A geological formation"],answer:1,explain:"Food chains show energy flow: producers \u2192 primary consumers \u2192 secondary consumers \u2192 decomposers."},{id:"5005-171",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Life Science",q:"What is speciation?",options:["Migration","Mutation","Formation of new species from existing ones","Extinction"],answer:2,explain:"Speciation occurs when populations become reproductively isolated and diverge genetically over time."},{id:"5005-172",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Life Science",q:"Which approach best addresses the misconception that organisms 'try' to adapt?",options:["Tell them they're wrong","Agree with them","Skip adaptation topics","Emphasize that variation is random; selection is non-random; organisms don't choose adaptations"],answer:3,explain:"Clarifying the roles of random variation and non-random selection corrects teleological thinking about evolution."},{id:"5005-173",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Physical Science",q:"What is Newton's second law?",options:["F = ma","Objects at rest stay at rest","Gravity pulls down","Every action has an equal reaction"],answer:0,explain:"Newton's Second Law: Force equals mass times acceleration (F = ma)."},{id:"5005-174",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Physical Science",q:"What is the law of conservation of energy?",options:["Energy can be created","Energy cannot be created or destroyed, only transformed","Energy disappears","Energy is infinite"],answer:1,explain:"Energy is conserved in isolated systems\u2014it changes forms but total amount remains constant."},{id:"5005-175",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Physical Science",q:"Students think that energy is used up. How to correct?",options:["Agree with them","Tell them it's wrong","Show energy transformations (potential \u2192 kinetic \u2192 thermal) demonstrate conservation","Skip the topic"],answer:2,explain:"Experiments showing energy transformations (pendulums, roller coasters) demonstrate conservation despite 'loss' to friction/heat."},{id:"5005-176",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Physical Science",q:"What is the unit of work?",options:["Newton","Pascal","Watt","Joule"],answer:3,explain:"Work is measured in Joules (J). One joule = one newton-meter."},{id:"5005-177",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Physical Science",q:"What is density formula?",options:["Density = mass \xF7 volume","Density = volume \xF7 mass","Density = mass + volume","Density = mass \xD7 volume"],answer:0,explain:"Density (\u03C1) = mass (m) / volume (V). Units: kg/m\xB3 or g/cm\xB3."},{id:"5005-178",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Physical Science",q:"Why do objects float or sink?",options:["Shape only","Density relative to the fluid\u2014if less dense, it floats","Weight only","Color"],answer:1,explain:"Buoyancy depends on density comparison: objects less dense than fluid float; denser objects sink."},{id:"5005-179",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Physical Science",q:"Which experiment best demonstrates pressure?",options:["Measuring temperature","Mixing liquids","Pressing a thumbtack into wood\u2014point exerts more pressure than flat end","Weighing objects"],answer:2,explain:"Thumbtack demonstration shows pressure = force/area\u2014same force, smaller area (point) creates greater pressure."},{id:"5005-180",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Physical Science",q:"What is potential energy?",options:["Heat energy","Sound energy","Energy of motion","Stored energy due to position or condition"],answer:3,explain:"Potential energy is stored energy (gravitational, elastic, chemical) ready to be converted to kinetic."},{id:"5005-181",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Physical Science",q:"What is a conductor?",options:["Material that allows electricity to flow easily","A switch","Material that blocks electricity","A type of battery"],answer:0,explain:"Conductors (metals, saltwater) allow electric current to flow. Insulators resist flow."},{id:"5005-182",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Physical Science",q:"Why do mirages occur?",options:["Absorption by dust","Refraction of light through layers of air at different temperatures","Emission from hot ground","Reflection off water"],answer:1,explain:"Mirages result from light bending (refracting) through air layers of different densities caused by temperature gradients."},{id:"5003-081",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Geometry and Measurement, Data, Statistics, and Probability",q:"What is the area of a rectangle with length 5 and width 3?",options:["25","8","15","10"],answer:2,explain:"Area = length \xD7 width = 5 \xD7 3 = 15 square units."},{id:"5003-082",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Geometry and Measurement, Data, Statistics, and Probability",q:"What is the mean of 4, 6, 8, 10, 12?",options:["12","6","10","8"],answer:3,explain:"Mean = (4+6+8+10+12) \xF7 5 = 40 \xF7 5 = 8."},{id:"5003-083",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Geometry and Measurement, Data, Statistics, and Probability",q:"Students confuse area and perimeter. Which activity distinguishes them?",options:["Use grid paper: count squares for area, count edges for perimeter","Ignore the confusion","Memorize formulas","Draw shapes randomly"],answer:0,explain:"Grid paper makes area (squares covered) and perimeter (edge segments) visually distinct."},{id:"5003-084",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Geometry and Measurement, Data, Statistics, and Probability",q:"What is the volume of a cube with side 4 cm?",options:["16 cm\xB3","64 cm\xB3","32 cm\xB3","48 cm\xB3"],answer:1,explain:"Volume = side\xB3 = 4\xB3 = 64 cubic centimeters."},{id:"5003-085",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Geometry and Measurement, Data, Statistics, and Probability",q:"What is the mode of 2, 3, 3, 5, 7, 7, 7, 9?",options:["9","5","7","3"],answer:2,explain:"Mode is the most frequent value. 7 appears three times\u2014more than any other number."},{id:"5003-086",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Geometry and Measurement, Data, Statistics, and Probability",q:"What is the probability of rolling a 3 on a fair six-sided die?",options:["1/3","3/6","1/2","1/6"],answer:3,explain:"One favorable outcome (rolling 3) out of six equally likely outcomes."},{id:"5003-087",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Geometry and Measurement, Data, Statistics, and Probability",q:"Which tool best helps students visualize 3D geometry?",options:["Physical manipulatives (cubes, nets) that can be assembled and disassembled","Mental imagery","Videos only","Paper drawings"],answer:0,explain:"Manipulatives provide concrete experience with faces, edges, and vertices before abstract formulas."},{id:"5003-088",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Geometry and Measurement, Data, Statistics, and Probability",q:"What is the circumference of a circle with radius 7 (use \u03C0 \u2248 3.14)?",options:["153.86","43.96","35","21.98"],answer:1,explain:"Circumference = 2\u03C0r = 2 \xD7 3.14 \xD7 7 = 43.96."},{id:"5003-089",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Geometry and Measurement, Data, Statistics, and Probability",q:"What is the median of 3, 7, 9, 12, 15?",options:["3","7","9","15"],answer:2,explain:"Median is the middle value in ordered data. 9 is the third of five values."},{id:"5003-090",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Geometry and Measurement, Data, Statistics, and Probability",q:"What is the difference between independent and dependent events in probability?",options:["Dependent events are impossible","They are the same","Independent events always have equal probability","Independent events don't affect each other's probabilities; dependent events do"],answer:3,explain:"Drawing cards without replacement creates dependent events (probabilities change); rolling dice creates independent events."},{id:"5004-146",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"United States History, Government, and Citizenship",q:"What year did the Constitution go into effect?",options:["1789","1812","1804","1776"],answer:0,explain:"The Constitution went into effect in 1789 after ratification by the required nine states."},{id:"5004-147",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"United States History, Government, and Citizenship",q:"What is federalism?",options:["Rule by one person","Division of power between national and state governments","Power held by states only","International governance"],answer:1,explain:"Federalism divides sovereignty between a central government and constituent political units (states)."},{id:"5004-148",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"United States History, Government, and Citizenship",q:"Students think the Bill of Rights protects only famous rights. How to broaden understanding?",options:["Skip lesser-known amendments","Tell them to read all 10","Show how lesser-known amendments (8th, 9th, 10th) protect everyday rights","Agree with them"],answer:2,explain:"Showing the full scope (cruel/unusual punishment, unenumerated rights, state powers) reveals comprehensive protection."},{id:"5004-149",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"United States History, Government, and Citizenship",q:"What is the function of the Cabinet?",options:["To interpret laws","To elect the President","To make laws","To advise the President and head executive departments"],answer:3,explain:"The Cabinet comprises department heads who advise the President and administer federal programs."},{id:"5004-150",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"United States History, Government, and Citizenship",q:"How many amendments has the Constitution had?",options:["27","10","50","15"],answer:0,explain:"There are 27 amendments to the US Constitution, the most recent (27th) ratified in 1992."},{id:"5004-151",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"United States History, Government, and Citizenship",q:"What is the tension between federal and state power called?",options:["Impeachment","Federalism","Amendment process","Judicial review"],answer:1,explain:"Federalism creates ongoing negotiation between national supremacy and state sovereignty."},{id:"5004-152",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"United States History, Government, and Citizenship",q:"Which simulation best teaches civic engagement?",options:["Mock trial only","Watching news","Model legislature where students draft, debate, and vote on bills","Reading about government"],answer:2,explain:"Legislative simulation gives students authentic experience with lawmaking process and compromise."},{id:"5004-153",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"United States History, Government, and Citizenship",q:"What is a filibuster?",options:["A presidential veto","A formal type of ceremonial speech","A procedure used only in the House","A Senate tactic used to prolong debate or delay or prevent a vote"],answer:3,explain:"A filibuster is a Senate strategy for prolonging debate or delaying or blocking a vote. It does not always require continuous floor speaking; cloture can limit debate under Senate rules."},{id:"5004-154",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"United States History, Government, and Citizenship",q:"What is the significance of Gibbons v. Ogden?",options:["It affirmed federal supremacy over interstate commerce","It created the Supreme Court","It limited federal power","It abolished slavery"],answer:0,explain:"Gibbons v. Ogden (1824) broadly interpreted the Commerce Clause, establishing federal authority over interstate trade."},{id:"5002-136",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Writing, Speaking, and Listening",q:"Why is audience awareness important in writing?",options:["It doesn't matter","It determines tone, register, and level of explanation appropriate for readers","It changes the spelling","It only affects length"],answer:1,explain:"Audience shapes word choice, formality, and assumed prior knowledge\u2014critical for effective communication."},{id:"5002-137",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Writing, Speaking, and Listening",q:"Students write rambling paragraphs without clear structure. Which strategy helps?",options:["Skip paragraph structure","Tell them to write longer","Teach outline-based planning: claim \u2192 evidence \u2192 reasoning","Let them write freely"],answer:2,explain:"Structured planning (claim-evidence-reasoning) provides a scaffold that produces coherent paragraphs."},{id:"5002-138",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Writing, Speaking, and Listening",q:"What is the difference between informal and formal writing?",options:["Informal is always wrong","Formal has no rules","They are the same","Formal writing follows standard conventions; informal is conversational"],answer:3,explain:"Formal writing uses standard grammar, complete sentences, and objective tone; informal is relaxed and personal."},{id:"5002-139",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Writing, Speaking, and Listening",q:"Which approach best develops students' argumentative writing?",options:["Debate followed by writing arguments that address counterclaims","Writing without feedback","Reading sample essays","Memorizing essay formats"],answer:0,explain:"Debate builds reasoning skills; addressing counterclaims in writing demonstrates sophisticated argumentation."},{id:"5002-140",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Writing, Speaking, and Listening",q:"What is a topic sentence?",options:["The conclusion","The main idea of a paragraph, usually at the beginning","The title of an essay","A question"],answer:1,explain:"Topic sentences state a paragraph's main point and guide the reader's expectations."},{id:"5002-141",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Writing, Speaking, and Listening",q:"What is parallel structure?",options:["Writing in columns","Starting every sentence the same way","Using the same grammatical form for items in a list","Repeating the same word"],answer:2,explain:"Parallel structure maintains consistent grammatical patterns (e.g., 'running, swimming, and cycling' not 'running, swimming, and to cycle')."},{id:"5002-142",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Writing, Speaking, and Listening",q:"Why is sentence variety important in writing?",options:["Longer is always better","It isn't","Short sentences are preferred","It creates rhythm, emphasis, and readability; monotonous sentences bore readers"],answer:3,explain:"Variety in sentence length and structure maintains reader engagement and controls pacing and emphasis."},{id:"5005-183",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Earth Science",q:"What is the inner core made of?",options:["Solid iron and nickel","Water","Magma","Rock"],answer:0,explain:"Earth's inner core is solid iron-nickel alloy due to extreme pressure, despite temperatures similar to the sun's surface."},{id:"5005-184",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Earth Science",q:"Why does Earth have seasons?",options:["Ocean currents","Axial tilt causes varying solar intensity throughout the year","Moon's gravity","Distance from the sun"],answer:1,explain:"Earth's 23.5\xB0 axial tilt causes different hemispheres to receive more direct sunlight at different times of year."},{id:"5005-185",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Earth Science",q:"Students think seasons are caused by Earth's distance from the sun. How to correct?",options:["Tell them it's wrong","Agree with them","Use a globe and lamp to show tilt effect; note Earth is closest to sun in January (Northern winter)","Skip seasons"],answer:2,explain:"Demonstrating axial tilt with a globe and light source, plus noting perihelion occurs in January, corrects the misconception."},{id:"5005-186",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Earth Science",q:"What is a mineral?",options:["A metal","An organic material","Any rock","A naturally occurring, inorganic solid with definite chemical composition and crystalline structure"],answer:3,explain:"Minerals are natural, inorganic, crystalline solids with specific chemical formulas (e.g., quartz = SiO\u2082)."},{id:"5005-187",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Earth Science",q:"Which observation best teaches students about rock formation?",options:["Examining actual rock samples and classifying by texture and composition","Reading descriptions","Drawing rocks","Watching videos only"],answer:0,explain:"Hands-on specimen examination builds observational skills and connects theory to real geologic materials."},{id:"5005-188",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Earth Science",q:"What is precipitation?",options:["Rivers flowing","Any water falling from clouds (rain, snow, sleet, hail)","Clouds forming","Snow only"],answer:1,explain:"Precipitation includes rain, snow, sleet, and hail\u2014any form of water falling from the atmosphere."},{id:"5005-189",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Earth Science",q:"What is the atmosphere's main gas?",options:["Carbon dioxide","Oxygen","Nitrogen (about 78%)","Hydrogen"],answer:2,explain:"Earth's atmosphere is approximately 78% nitrogen, 21% oxygen, and 1% other gases."},{id:"5005-190",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Earth Science",q:"Why is the ozone layer important?",options:["It produces oxygen","It creates weather","It reflects sunlight","It absorbs harmful ultraviolet radiation from the sun"],answer:3,explain:"The ozone layer (stratosphere) absorbs UV-B and UV-C radiation, protecting life from DNA damage."},{id:"5003-091",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Numbers and Operations",q:"What is 15% of 200?",options:["30","50","20","15"],answer:0,explain:"15% \xD7 200 = 0.15 \xD7 200 = 30."},{id:"5003-092",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Numbers and Operations",q:"Why is 0.999... equal to 1?",options:["It depends on rounding","Infinite decimal 0.999... represents the limit approaching 1, which equals 1","It is a coincidence","They are different numbers"],answer:1,explain:"0.999... = \u03A3(9\xD710\u207B\u207F) for n=1 to \u221E = 1. This is a standard result in real analysis."},{id:"5003-093",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Numbers and Operations",q:"Students think that dividing always makes numbers smaller. What counterexample works?",options:["Dividing by 1","Dividing by zero","Dividing by a fraction less than 1 (e.g., 6 \xF7 1/2 = 12)","Dividing by 10"],answer:2,explain:"Dividing by proper fractions increases magnitude, directly contradicting the 'division always shrinks' misconception."},{id:"5003-094",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Numbers and Operations",q:"What is the GCF of 12 and 18?",options:["3","2","9","6"],answer:3,explain:"GCF(12,18): factors of 12 are 1,2,3,4,6,12; factors of 18 are 1,2,3,6,9,18. Greatest common: 6."},{id:"5003-095",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Numbers and Operations",q:"What is the difference between rational and irrational numbers?",options:["Rational numbers can be expressed as fractions; irrational cannot","They are the same","Irrational numbers don't exist","Rational numbers are always positive"],answer:0,explain:"Rational = p/q (finite or repeating decimals). Irrational = non-repeating, non-terminating decimals (\u221A2, \u03C0)."},{id:"5003-096",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Numbers and Operations",q:"Which model best helps students understand negative numbers?",options:["Memory drills","Number line with positive/negative directions","Counting only positive numbers","Abstract symbols"],answer:1,explain:"Number lines make negative numbers concrete\u2014left of zero represents debt, below zero, etc."},{id:"5003-097",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Numbers and Operations",q:"What is 2/3 + 1/4?",options:["2/7","3/12","11/12","3/7"],answer:2,explain:"LCD = 12. 2/3 = 8/12, 1/4 = 3/12. Sum = 11/12."},{id:"5004-155",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"World History and Economics",q:"What is the Industrial Revolution's origin country?",options:["United States","France","Germany","Great Britain"],answer:3,explain:"The Industrial Revolution began in Great Britain in the late 18th century, driven by coal, iron, and textile innovations."},{id:"5004-156",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"World History and Economics",q:"What was the immediate trigger of World War I?",options:["The assassination of Archduke Franz Ferdinand and the crisis that followed","A single religious dispute","An international economic depression","A natural disaster in Europe"],answer:0,explain:"The assassination in Sarajevo triggered the July Crisis and activation of alliance commitments. Militarism, imperial competition, nationalism, and alliances were important long-term causes rather than the single immediate trigger."},{id:"5004-157",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"World History and Economics",q:"Which approach best teaches students about economic systems?",options:["Ignoring the topic","Comparing how different societies organize production and distribution","Reading textbooks","Memorizing definitions"],answer:1,explain:"Comparative analysis reveals how economic systems answer 'what, how, for whom to produce.'"},{id:"5004-158",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"World History and Economics",q:"What is globalization?",options:["Military expansion","A type of government","Increasing interconnectedness of economies, cultures, and populations worldwide","Isolation of nations"],answer:2,explain:"Globalization describes growing interdependence through trade, communication, and cultural exchange."},{id:"5004-159",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"World History and Economics",q:"Which ancient civilization invented writing?",options:["Romans","Greeks","Egyptians","Sumerians (cuneiform)"],answer:3,explain:"Sumerians in Mesopotamia developed cuneiform writing around 3400 BCE\u2014among the earliest writing systems."},{id:"5004-160",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"World History and Economics",q:"What was the significance of the Silk Road?",options:["It connected East and West, enabling cultural and economic exchange","It only existed in Europe","It was only for silk","It was a military route"],answer:0,explain:"The Silk Road network facilitated trade of goods, ideas, religions, and technologies between Asia and Europe."},{id:"5004-161",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"World History and Economics",q:"Students think that economic history is just about money. How to broaden perspective?",options:["Skip economics","Show how economic systems shape politics, culture, and social structures","Tell them to focus only on money","Agree with them"],answer:1,explain:"Economic history reveals connections between trade, power, inequality, and cultural change\u2014interdisciplinary thinking."},{id:"5002-143",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Writing, Speaking, and Listening",q:"What is the purpose of a concluding paragraph?",options:["To list sources","To introduce new ideas","To summarize main points and reinforce the thesis","To ask questions"],answer:2,explain:"Conclusions restate the thesis in light of evidence presented and provide closure without introducing new information."},{id:"5002-144",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Writing, Speaking, and Listening",q:"Why is evidence important in argumentative writing?",options:["It is optional","It confuses readers","It makes writing longer","It supports claims and builds credibility with the audience"],answer:3,explain:"Evidence (facts, statistics, examples) transforms assertions into persuasively supported arguments."},{id:"5002-145",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Writing, Speaking, and Listening",q:"Which activity best develops listening skills?",options:["Active listening exercises: paraphrasing, questioning, identifying key points","Multi-tasking while listening","Passive listening","Ignoring speakers"],answer:0,explain:"Structured listening practice builds comprehension, empathy, and critical evaluation of spoken messages."},{id:"5002-146",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Writing, Speaking, and Listening",q:"What is a citation?",options:["A type of grammar","An acknowledgment of sources used in writing","A title","A conclusion"],answer:1,explain:"Citations give credit to original authors and allow readers to locate sources\u2014essential for academic integrity."},{id:"5002-147",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Writing, Speaking, and Listening",q:"What is a summary?",options:["A quotation","A detailed analysis","A brief restatement of main ideas in your own words","A personal opinion"],answer:2,explain:"Summaries capture essential points concisely and objectively, without adding interpretation or detail."},{id:"5002-148",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Writing, Speaking, and Listening",q:"Why is voice important in persuasive writing?",options:["It doesn't matter","Voice is only for creative writing","Formal voice is always better","Voice (tone, personality) builds connection and credibility with readers"],answer:3,explain:"Appropriate voice establishes authorial presence and builds trust with the audience."},{id:"5002-149",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Writing, Speaking, and Listening",q:"Students use weak vocabulary repeatedly. Which strategy helps?",options:["Build word walls and practice using synonyms in context","Memorize dictionaries","Avoid complex words","Use thesaurus blindly"],answer:0,explain:"Word walls with contextual usage build vocabulary retention better than rote memorization."},{id:"5002-150",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Writing, Speaking, and Listening",q:"What is the difference between facts and opinions?",options:["They are the same","Facts are verifiable; opinions are beliefs or judgments","Opinions are always wrong","Facts are subjective"],answer:1,explain:"Facts can be proven true or false through evidence; opinions express beliefs, feelings, or judgments."},{id:"5005-191",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Life Science",q:"What is the role of enzymes?",options:["They transport oxygen","They store energy","They act as biological catalysts, speeding up reactions without being consumed","They are structural"],answer:2,explain:"Enzymes lower activation energy, enabling reactions to proceed rapidly under mild conditions."},{id:"5005-192",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Life Science",q:"Students describe cells as simple building blocks. Which instructional response would best deepen their understanding?",options:["Ask students to memorize a list of organelles","Focus only on the cell membrane","Tell students that cells are complicated without providing examples","Use a systems model showing how organelles coordinate metabolism, transport, and communication"],answer:3,explain:"A systems model shows that organelles interact rather than operate as an isolated list of parts. Memorization alone does not reveal how cellular processes are coordinated."},{id:"5005-193",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Life Science",q:"What is osmosis?",options:["Diffusion of water across a semipermeable membrane","Movement of solutes","Cell division","Active transport"],answer:0,explain:"Osmosis is passive water movement from low solute concentration to high solute concentration."},{id:"5005-194",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Life Science",q:"What is the cell membrane's main function?",options:["Protein synthesis","Regulating what enters and leaves the cell","DNA storage","Energy production"],answer:1,explain:"The cell membrane is selectively permeable, controlling substance passage via channels and pumps."},{id:"5005-195",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Life Science",q:"Why do red blood cells lack a nucleus?",options:["They don't need DNA","They reproduce without DNA","Loss of nucleus maximizes space for hemoglobin, increasing oxygen-carrying capacity","They are dead cells"],answer:2,explain:"Mammalian RBCs eject nuclei during maturation to maximize hemoglobin content and flexibility."},{id:"5005-196",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Life Science",q:"Which model best represents the cell membrane?",options:["Solid barrier","Paper filter","Brick wall","Fluid mosaic model: phospholipid bilayer with embedded proteins"],answer:3,explain:"The fluid mosaic model captures membrane dynamics: fluid lipids with mobile protein 'islands.'"},{id:"5005-197",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Life Science",q:"What is ATP?",options:["The cell's energy currency","A protein","A carbohydrate","A vitamin"],answer:0,explain:"ATP (adenosine triphosphate) stores and transfers energy for cellular work through phosphate bond hydrolysis."},{id:"5005-198",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Life Science",q:"What is DNA's shape?",options:["Circle","Double helix","Triangle","Single strand"],answer:1,explain:"DNA is a double helix\u2014two strands wound around each other, held by hydrogen bonds between bases."},{id:"5005-199",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Life Science",q:"What is the significance of the phospholipid bilayer's amphipathic nature?",options:["It makes the membrane rigid","It has no significance","Hydrophilic heads face water; hydrophobic tails face inward\u2014creates stable barrier","It allows all substances through"],answer:2,explain:"Amphipathic structure (polar heads, nonpolar tails) drives spontaneous bilayer formation\u2014the foundation of cell membranes."},{id:"5005-200",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Physical Science",q:"What is the unit of force?",options:["Watt","Pascal","Joule","Newton"],answer:3,explain:"Force is measured in Newtons (N). One newton accelerates one kilogram at one meter per second squared."},{id:"5005-201",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Physical Science",q:"Why does friction oppose motion?",options:["Surface irregularities interlock, resisting relative motion","Friction creates energy","Friction is imaginary","It pushes objects forward"],answer:0,explain:"Microscopic surface roughness creates resistance when surfaces slide past each other."},{id:"5005-202",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Physical Science",q:"Students think that motion requires constant force. What Newton's law corrects this?",options:["Law of gravity","First law\u2014objects in motion stay in motion without force","Second law\u2014force causes acceleration","Third law\u2014action-reaction"],answer:1,explain:"Newton's First Law shows force is needed to change motion, not to maintain it (inertia)."},{id:"5005-203",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Physical Science",q:"What is the formula for kinetic energy?",options:["KE = mc\xB2","KE = Fd","KE = \xBDmv\xB2","KE = mgh"],answer:2,explain:"Kinetic energy = \xBD \xD7 mass \xD7 velocity\xB2. It depends on both mass and the square of speed."},{id:"5005-204",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Physical Science",q:"What is Newton's third law?",options:["Gravity pulls down","F = ma","Objects at rest stay at rest","For every action, there is an equal and opposite reaction"],answer:3,explain:"Newton's Third Law: forces occur in pairs\u2014equal in magnitude, opposite in direction."},{id:"5005-205",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Physical Science",q:"What is the relationship between mass and weight?",options:["Mass is amount of matter; weight is gravitational force on that mass","Weight is constant everywhere","They are the same","Mass changes with location"],answer:0,explain:"Mass is invariant; weight varies with gravitational field strength (W = mg)."},{id:"5005-206",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Physical Science",q:"Which demonstration best shows conservation of momentum?",options:["Rolling balls down ramps","Colliding carts on a track, measuring before and after velocities","Dropping balls","Pulling strings"],answer:1,explain:"Collision experiments with motion sensors show momentum before = momentum after (in isolated systems)."},{id:"5005-207",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Physical Science",q:"What is a wave's wavelength?",options:["Wave height","Wave frequency","Distance between successive crests","Wave speed"],answer:2,explain:"Wavelength is the distance between two consecutive corresponding points (crest to crest)."},{id:"5005-208",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Physical Science",q:"Which sequence orders electromagnetic waves from lowest to highest energy?",options:["Gamma \u2192 X-ray \u2192 ultraviolet \u2192 visible \u2192 infrared \u2192 microwave \u2192 radio","Visible \u2192 radio \u2192 gamma \u2192 microwave \u2192 ultraviolet \u2192 infrared \u2192 X-ray","All electromagnetic waves have the same photon energy","Radio \u2192 microwave \u2192 infrared \u2192 visible \u2192 ultraviolet \u2192 X-ray \u2192 gamma"],answer:3,explain:"Photon energy increases with frequency. Radio waves have the lowest frequencies in the list, followed by microwaves, infrared, visible, ultraviolet, X-rays, and gamma rays."},{id:"5005-209",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Physical Science",q:"Why does light bend when entering water?",options:["Change in speed causes refraction","Water reflects light","Light loses energy","Water absorbs light"],answer:0,explain:"Refraction occurs because light slows in denser media, changing direction at the interface."},{id:"5005-210",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Earth Science",q:"What is the greenhouse effect?",options:["Cooling of Earth","Atmospheric trapping of heat by greenhouse gases","Ozone depletion","Acid rain"],answer:1,explain:"Greenhouse gases (CO\u2082, methane) trap infrared radiation, warming Earth's surface naturally\u2014and increasingly due to human activity."},{id:"5005-211",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Earth Science",q:"How do scientists know about Earth's interior?",options:["Direct observation","Drilling deep holes","Seismic wave analysis\u2014waves travel differently through different materials","Satellite imaging"],answer:2,explain:"Seismologists infer interior structure from how P and S waves refract, reflect, and shadow-zone patterns."},{id:"5005-212",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Earth Science",q:"Students think that climate change is natural and not human-caused. How to address?",options:["Agree with them","Tell them they're wrong","Skip the topic","Show ice core data, temperature records, and carbon isotope evidence linking current warming to fossil fuel burning"],answer:3,explain:"Multiple independent lines of evidence (ice cores, isotope ratios, warming rates) confirm anthropogenic climate change."},{id:"5005-213",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Earth Science",q:"What causes day and night?",options:["Earth's rotation on its axis","Clouds blocking sun","Sun's movement around Earth","Moon's movement"],answer:0,explain:"Earth rotates once every 24 hours, causing alternating daylight and darkness."},{id:"5005-214",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Earth Science",q:"What is the water cycle's main energy source?",options:["Earth's interior","Solar energy (sun)","Ocean currents","Wind"],answer:1,explain:"Solar radiation drives evaporation, powering the hydrologic cycle."},{id:"5005-215",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Earth Science",q:"Which hands-on activity best demonstrates erosion?",options:["Reading about erosion","Watching videos","Simulating rainfall on soil samples with different vegetation","Drawing erosion patterns"],answer:2,explain:"Controlled erosion experiments show how vegetation reduces soil loss from water impact."},{id:"5005-216",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Earth Science",q:"What is a storm?",options:["Calm weather","Ocean current","A type of rock","Disturbed atmospheric conditions with wind, precipitation, and lightning"],answer:3,explain:"Storms are weather disturbances characterized by strong winds, precipitation, and sometimes thunder/lightning."},{id:"5002-151",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Reading",q:"What is a primary source?",options:["An original document or artifact from the time period being studied","A movie","A textbook","A secondary summary"],answer:0,explain:"Primary sources are firsthand accounts or evidence created during the time under study (diaries, photographs, legal documents)."},{id:"5002-152",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Reading",q:"Why is context crucial for literary analysis?",options:["It is not important","Context shapes meaning\u2014historical, cultural, and biographical factors influence interpretation","Context only matters for old books","Texts are self-contained"],answer:1,explain:"Context illuminates authorial intent, audience expectations, and cultural assumptions embedded in texts."},{id:"5002-153",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Reading",q:"Students can decode words but lack comprehension. Which strategy helps?",options:["Skipping difficult texts","Reading faster","Building background knowledge and teaching vocabulary in context","More decoding practice"],answer:2,explain:"Decoding alone isn't enough\u2014comprehension requires vocabulary knowledge and world knowledge to construct meaning."},{id:"5002-154",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Reading",q:"What is foreshadowing?",options:["Character development","Setting description","A type of rhyme","Hints or clues about future events in a story"],answer:3,explain:"Foreshadowing creates anticipation and cohesion by planting subtle clues about what will happen later."},{id:"5002-155",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Reading",q:"What is an antagonist?",options:["The character or force opposing the protagonist","The hero","A narrator","A setting"],answer:0,explain:"The antagonist opposes the protagonist's goals, creating conflict that drives the narrative."},{id:"5002-156",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Reading",q:"Why do authors use imagery?",options:["Because it is required","To create vivid sensory experiences that engage readers' imaginations","To confuse readers","To make writing longer"],answer:1,explain:"Imagery appeals to the senses, making abstract ideas concrete and memorable."},{id:"5002-157",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Reading",q:"Which approach best teaches students to analyze arguments?",options:["Reading argumentative essays only","Memorizing fallacies","Practicing identification of claims, evidence, and reasoning in real texts","Ignoring logic"],answer:2,explain:"Analysis of authentic arguments builds critical thinking skills applicable across disciplines."},{id:"5002-158",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Reading",q:"What is narrative structure?",options:["A type of poem","Random event ordering","Character names","The organization of plot elements (exposition, rising action, climax, falling action, resolution)"],answer:3,explain:"Narrative structure provides the framework for storytelling, organizing events into coherent sequences."},{id:"5002-159",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Reading",q:"What is a simile?",options:["Comparison using 'like' or 'as'","Exaggeration","Personification","Direct comparison without 'like' or 'as'"],answer:0,explain:"Similes compare using 'like' or 'as' (e.g., 'brave as a lion'). Metaphors compare directly."},{id:"5002-160",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Reading",q:"Why do interpretations of texts vary across cultures?",options:["Cultures don't affect reading","Because cultural backgrounds shape what readers notice, value, and how they construct meaning","Because texts are unclear","Because one interpretation is always right"],answer:1,explain:"Reader-response theory recognizes that meaning emerges from interaction between text and reader's cultural framework."},{id:"5005-217",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Life Science",q:"What is mitosis?",options:["DNA replication only","Cell death","Cell division producing two identical daughter cells","Cell division producing four different cells"],answer:2,explain:"Mitosis produces two genetically identical daughter cells for growth and repair."},{id:"5005-218",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Life Science",q:"What is the difference between genotype and phenotype?",options:["Neither exists","They are the same","Phenotype is genes; genotype is appearance","Genotype is genetic makeup; phenotype is observable traits"],answer:3,explain:"Genotype (e.g., Bb) is the genetic code; phenotype (e.g., brown eyes) is the expressed trait."},{id:"5005-219",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Life Science",q:"Students think that dominant traits are more common. How to correct?",options:["Show examples where recessive traits are more common (e.g., blue eyes in some populations)","Agree with them","Tell them it's wrong","Skip genetics"],answer:0,explain:"Dominant means expressed when present\u2014not necessarily more frequent in a population."},{id:"5005-220",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Life Science",q:"What is DNA's role?",options:["Energy storage","Storing genetic information and directing protein synthesis","Cell structure","Hormone production"],answer:1,explain:"DNA codes for proteins through transcription and translation, determining organismal traits."},{id:"5005-221",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Life Science",q:"What is a gene?",options:["A type of mutation","A cell organelle","A segment of DNA coding for a specific protein or trait","A whole chromosome"],answer:2,explain:"Genes are functional units of heredity\u2014DNA sequences that code for proteins or RNA molecules."},{id:"5005-222",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Life Science",q:"What is genetic drift?",options:["Mutation only","Natural selection","Directed evolution","Random changes in allele frequencies, especially in small populations"],answer:3,explain:"Genetic drift is chance fluctuation in allele frequencies\u2014more pronounced in small populations."},{id:"5005-223",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Life Science",q:"Which model best represents DNA structure?",options:["Double helix model showing antiparallel strands with base pairing","Flat diagram only","Single strand model","Linear bead string"],answer:0,explain:"3D models showing complementary base pairing (A-T, G-C) and antiparallel orientation clarify DNA's structure-function relationship."},{id:"5005-224",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Life Science",q:"What are the four bases of DNA?",options:["E, F, G, H","Adenine, Thymine, Guanine, Cytosine","A, B, C, D","X, Y, Z, W"],answer:1,explain:"DNA bases: Adenine (A), Thymine (T), Guanine (G), Cytosine (C). A pairs with T; G pairs with C."},{id:"5005-225",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Life Science",q:"What is the purpose of meiosis?",options:["Asexual reproduction","Cell differentiation","Producing gametes with half the chromosome number for sexual reproduction","Growth and repair"],answer:2,explain:"Meiosis reduces chromosome number by half (diploid \u2192 haploid), enabling genetic diversity through recombination."},{id:"5005-226",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Life Science",q:"Students think that mutations are always harmful. How to correct?",options:["Skip mutations","Tell them they're wrong","Agree with them","Show that mutations are the source of all genetic variation\u2014some neutral, some beneficial"],answer:3,explain:"Mutations are random; most are neutral, some harmful, some beneficial. Without mutations, evolution would stall."},{id:"5004-162",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"Geography, Anthropology, and Sociology",q:"What is a culture?",options:["Shared beliefs, values, customs, and behaviors of a group","A physical trait","A type of government","Only food"],answer:0,explain:"Culture encompasses learned behaviors, symbols, language, norms, and material products shared by a group."},{id:"5004-163",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"Geography, Anthropology, and Sociology",q:"What is social stratification?",options:["Geographic layers","Hierarchical arrangement of individuals into categories based on power, wealth, and status","Equal distribution","Random sorting"],answer:1,explain:"Stratification creates social layers (caste, class) that determine access to resources and opportunities."},{id:"5004-164",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"Geography, Anthropology, and Sociology",q:"Students think that migration is always voluntary. How to address?",options:["Agree with them","Tell them they're wrong","Discuss push-pull factors: refugees, economic necessity, climate displacement alongside choice","Ignore migration"],answer:2,explain:"Migration includes both voluntary movement and forced displacement due to conflict, persecution, or environmental change."},{id:"5004-165",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"Geography, Anthropology, and Sociology",q:"What is globalization's main feature?",options:["Reduced travel","Isolation","National self-sufficiency","Increased interconnectedness across borders in trade, culture, and communication"],answer:3,explain:"Globalization accelerates exchange of goods, ideas, and people across national boundaries."},{id:"5004-166",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"Geography, Anthropology, and Sociology",q:"What is acculturation?",options:["Cultural change resulting from contact between different groups","Cultural preservation","Cultural extinction","Isolation"],answer:0,explain:"Acculturation involves mutual cultural adaptation when groups maintain contact\u2014distinct from assimilation."},{id:"5004-167",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"Geography, Anthropology, and Sociology",q:"Which activity best teaches cultural geography?",options:["Listing countries","Analyzing how physical environment shapes cultural practices (housing, agriculture, clothing)","Memorizing maps","Ignoring environment"],answer:1,explain:"Examining environment-culture interactions shows how geography shapes human societies."},{id:"5004-168",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"Geography, Anthropology, and Sociology",q:"What is a language family?",options:["A single language","A dialect","Group of languages descended from a common ancestral language","Writing systems"],answer:2,explain:"Language families (Indo-European, Sino-Tibetan, etc.) show historical relationships through shared vocabulary and grammar."},{id:"5004-169",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"Geography, Anthropology, and Sociology",q:"What is a demographic transition?",options:["Population increase only","Population decrease","Migration patterns","Shift from high birth/death rates to low birth/death rates as a society develops"],answer:3,explain:"The demographic transition model describes stages: pre-industrial \u2192 transitional \u2192 industrial \u2192 post-industrial."},{id:"5004-170",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"Geography, Anthropology, and Sociology",q:"What is the 'resource curse'?",options:["Resource-rich countries sometimes experience less development due to corruption and economic distortion","Resources are always beneficial","Abundance of resources helps all equally","The curse is biblical"],answer:0,explain:"The resource curse describes how dependence on natural resource exports can hinder diversification and governance."},{id:"5005-227",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Physical Science",q:"What is electricity?",options:["Stationary charge","Flow of electric charge (electrons) through a conductor","Light emission","Magnetic field"],answer:1,explain:"Electricity is the movement of charged particles, typically electrons, through a conductive material."},{id:"5005-228",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Physical Science",q:"Why do transformers change voltage?",options:["They convert DC to AC","They store energy","Electromagnetic induction transfers energy between coils at different ratios","They generate electricity"],answer:2,explain:"Transformers use changing magnetic fields to step voltage up or down\u2014essential for efficient power transmission."},{id:"5005-229",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Physical Science",q:"Students think that circuits need a battery to work. How to expand understanding?",options:["Skip alternative sources","Agree with them","Tell them they're wrong","Show alternative energy sources (solar cells, generators, thermocouples)"],answer:3,explain:"Any energy conversion device (solar, thermal, mechanical) can drive current\u2014not just batteries."},{id:"5005-230",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Physical Science",q:"What is Ohm's law?",options:["V = IR","E = mc\xB2","F = ma","P = IV"],answer:0,explain:"Ohm's Law: Voltage = Current \xD7 Resistance (V = IR). It describes the relationship between electrical quantities."},{id:"5005-231",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Physical Science",q:"What is an insulator?",options:["A battery","Material that resists electric current flow","A switch","Material that conducts electricity well"],answer:1,explain:"Insulators (rubber, glass, plastic) have tightly bound electrons that resist current flow."},{id:"5005-232",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Physical Science",q:"What is the difference between series and parallel circuits?",options:["Parallel cannot work","They are the same","Series: one path; Parallel: multiple paths\u2014components fail independently in parallel","Series has more resistance"],answer:2,explain:"In series, current is the same through all components. In parallel, voltage is the same across each branch."},{id:"5005-233",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Physical Science",q:"Which demonstration best shows electromagnetic induction?",options:["Rubbing balloons","Static electricity","Heating a wire","Moving a magnet through a coil of wire and observing current flow"],answer:3,explain:"Faraday's law: changing magnetic flux through a coil induces electromotive force (current)."},{id:"5005-234",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Physical Science",q:"What is alternating current (AC)?",options:["Current that periodically reverses direction","Static electricity","Direct current from batteries","Current that flows in one direction"],answer:0,explain:"AC alternates direction (typically 60 Hz in US homes); DC flows steadily in one direction."},{id:"5005-235",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Physical Science",q:"What is the unit of resistance?",options:["Ampere","Ohm (\u03A9)","Volt","Watt"],answer:1,explain:"Resistance is measured in Ohms (\u03A9), named after Georg Ohm."},{id:"5005-236",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Physical Science",q:"Why is power transmitted at high voltage?",options:["To increase current","To make lights brighter","To reduce energy loss (P_loss = I\xB2R)\u2014high voltage allows lower current for same power","It is required by law"],answer:2,explain:"High-voltage transmission reduces current, minimizing resistive heating losses in power lines."},{id:"5005-237",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Earth Science",q:"What is the hydrosphere?",options:["Earth's rocks","Earth's air","Living things","All of Earth's water (oceans, lakes, rivers, groundwater, ice)"],answer:3,explain:"The hydrosphere includes all water on Earth in liquid, solid, and gas forms."},{id:"5005-238",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Earth Science",q:"What causes tides?",options:["Gravitational pull of the moon and sun on Earth's oceans","Earth's rotation only","Ocean currents","Wind"],answer:0,explain:"Tides result from differential gravitational attraction\u2014the moon's gravity pulls ocean water toward it."},{id:"5005-239",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Earth Science",q:"Students think that the moon causes tides because it is large. How to clarify?",options:["The Moon is larger than the Sun","Tidal effects depend strongly on distance because they arise from differences in gravitational pull across Earth; the nearby Moon therefore has a strong tidal influence","Tides occur only because Earth rotates","The Sun has no gravitational effect on Earth"],answer:1,explain:"Tides arise from the gradient in gravitational attraction across Earth. The tidal effect scales approximately with mass divided by distance cubed, so the Moon\u2019s proximity gives it a strong influence despite its smaller mass."},{id:"5005-240",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Earth Science",q:"What is an aquifer?",options:["A glacier","A lake","An underground layer of water-bearing permeable rock","A river"],answer:2,explain:"Aquifers store and transmit groundwater through porous rock or sediment\u2014critical water resources."},{id:"5005-241",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Earth Science",q:"What is the atmosphere?",options:["Earth's water","Ocean depths","Earth's crust","The layer of gases surrounding Earth"],answer:3,explain:"The atmosphere is a blanket of gases (nitrogen, oxygen, trace gases) held by gravity around Earth."},{id:"5005-242",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Earth Science",q:"What is the Coriolis effect's impact on weather patterns?",options:["It deflects winds and ocean currents, creating circulation patterns (trade winds, cyclones)","No impact","It creates calm weather","It only affects poles"],answer:0,explain:"Earth's rotation deflects moving air and water, creating global wind belts and ocean gyres."},{id:"5005-243",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Earth Science",q:"Which demonstration best shows plate tectonics?",options:["Drawing maps","Using foam board or clay models to simulate convergent, divergent, and transform boundaries","Watching earthquakes","Reading about volcanoes"],answer:1,explain:"Hands-on modeling helps students visualize plate interactions and their surface expressions."},{id:"5005-244",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Earth Science",q:"What is karst topography?",options:["Desert formations","Mountain ranges","Landscape shaped by dissolution of soluble rocks (limestone) creating caves and sinkholes","Glacial valleys"],answer:2,explain:"Karst forms where acidic water dissolves limestone, creating caves, sinkholes, and underground drainage."},{id:"5002-161",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Reading",q:"What is a plot?",options:["A theme","The setting","A character's name","The sequence of events in a story"],answer:3,explain:"Plot is the organized sequence of events that make up a narrative, typically including exposition, rising action, climax, falling action, and resolution."},{id:"5002-162",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Reading",q:"Why does genre matter in reading comprehension?",options:["Different genres have different conventions, expectations, and structures that guide interpretation","All texts are the same","Genre doesn't matter","Genre only affects length"],answer:0,explain:"Knowing genre (mystery, biography, science fiction) sets reader expectations and shapes interpretation strategies."},{id:"5002-163",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Reading",q:"Students read passively without engaging with text. Which strategy builds active reading?",options:["More reading time","Teaching annotation: marking key ideas, questioning, connecting to prior knowledge","Reading silently","Reading faster"],answer:1,explain:"Annotation transforms passive consumption into active dialogue with the text."},{id:"5002-164",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Reading",q:"What is conflict in literature?",options:["A character trait","A setting detail","The struggle between opposing forces that drives the narrative","A type of poem"],answer:2,explain:"Conflict (person vs. person, self, society, nature) creates tension and drives plot forward."},{id:"5002-165",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Reading",q:"What is a protagonist?",options:["A setting","A narrator","The villain","The main character"],answer:3,explain:"The protagonist is the central character whose goals drive the narrative."},{id:"5002-166",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Reading",q:"Why is tone different from mood?",options:["Tone is the author's attitude; mood is the feeling evoked in the reader","Neither matters","They are the same","Mood is author's; tone is reader's"],answer:0,explain:"Tone = author's attitude (formal, ironic, earnest). Mood = emotional atmosphere felt by reader."},{id:"5002-167",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Reading",q:"Which approach best develops vocabulary from context?",options:["Memorizing word lists","Using word clues (definition, example, contrast) in surrounding text","Ignoring vocabulary","Looking up every unknown word"],answer:1,explain:"Context clue analysis builds independent word-learning skills applicable across all subjects."},{id:"5002-168",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Reading",q:"What is symbolism?",options:["A punctuation mark","A type of rhyme","Using objects, characters, or events to represent abstract ideas","A character name"],answer:2,explain:"Symbols carry deeper meaning beyond their literal sense (e.g., a dove symbolizes peace)."},{id:"5002-169",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Reading",q:"Why do authors use unreliable narrators?",options:["To confuse readers","It is required by convention","Because they don't know how to write","To create dramatic irony and prompt readers to question what they're being told"],answer:3,explain:"Unreliable narrators (due to bias, ignorance, or deception) require readers to read critically and infer truth."},{id:"5005-245",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Life Science",q:"What is the nitrogen cycle?",options:["Nitrogen circulates between atmosphere, soil, and organisms through fixation, nitrification, and denitrification","Nitrogen only exists in air","Plants absorb nitrogen directly","Nitrogen is unused"],answer:0,explain:"Nitrogen-fixing bacteria convert N\u2082 to usable forms; plants incorporate it; decomposition returns it to soil."},{id:"5005-246",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Life Science",q:"Students think that predators control prey populations completely. What nuance should be added?",options:["Predation doesn't matter","Prey populations are controlled by multiple factors: food availability, disease, climate, and predation","Prey control predators","Predators are the only factor"],answer:1,explain:"Population dynamics involve complex interactions\u2014predation is one factor among many in regulating populations."},{id:"5005-247",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Life Science",q:"What is the carbon cycle?",options:["Carbon is static","Only animals cycle carbon","Carbon moves between atmosphere (CO\u2082), organisms (biomass), oceans, and rocks through photosynthesis, respiration, and decomposition","Carbon only exists in air"],answer:2,explain:"The carbon cycle moves carbon through photosynthesis, respiration, decomposition, combustion, and sedimentation."},{id:"5005-248",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Life Science",q:"What is a food web?",options:["A geological feature","A type of plant","A single food chain","An interconnected network of food chains showing energy flow"],answer:3,explain:"Food webs show complex feeding relationships\u2014most organisms eat and are eaten by multiple species."},{id:"5005-249",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Life Science",q:"What is ecological succession?",options:["Change in a community\u2019s species composition over time after disturbance or formation of new habitat","A fixed sequence that always ends in one permanent climax community","The immediate extinction of every species after disturbance","Random change with no influence from environment or species interactions"],answer:0,explain:"Ecological succession is change in community composition through colonization, competition, disturbance, and environmental conditions. It does not always follow one fixed path or end in a single permanent climax community."},{id:"5005-250",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Life Science",q:"Which lab best demonstrates limiting factors?",options:["List the species in several unrelated habitats","Grow identical plants while changing one resource at a time, using a control and repeated trials, then measure growth","Measure room temperature once","Count animals without changing or measuring a possible limiting resource"],answer:1,explain:"A controlled experiment varies one resource while holding other conditions constant and uses replication. Comparing plant growth against a control reveals whether light, water, or nutrients limit growth."},{id:"5005-251",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Life Science",q:"What is an invasive species?",options:["An endangered species","A extinct species","A non-native species that spreads rapidly and disrupts ecosystems","A native species"],answer:2,explain:"Invasive species outcompete natives, alter habitats, and reduce biodiversity due to lack of natural predators."},{id:"5005-252",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Life Science",q:"What is conservation of energy in ecosystems?",options:["Energy is created","Energy is infinite","Energy disappears","Energy flows through ecosystems; it is transferred and transformed but not destroyed"],answer:3,explain:"Energy enters as sunlight, flows through trophic levels, and is lost as heat\u2014never created or destroyed."},{id:"5005-253",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Life Science",q:"Students think that ecosystems are static. How to correct?",options:["Show time-lapse studies of succession, population cycles, and environmental change","Skip ecosystem dynamics","Tell them they're wrong","Agree with them"],answer:0,explain:"Ecosystems are dynamic\u2014constantly changing through succession, disturbance, and adaptation."},{id:"5004-171",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"United States History, Government, and Citizenship",q:"What is the Constitution's preamble?",options:["The bill of rights","The introductory statement outlining the Constitution's purposes","The amendments","A list of laws"],answer:1,explain:"The Preamble states the Constitution's goals: 'form a more perfect union, establish justice, insure domestic tranquility...'"},{id:"5004-172",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"United States History, Government, and Citizenship",q:"What is the significance of the 19th Amendment?",options:["It created the Supreme Court","It ended slavery","It granted women the right to vote","It established proportional representation"],answer:2,explain:"The 19th Amendment (1920) prohibited voting discrimination based on sex, securing women's suffrage."},{id:"5004-173",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"United States History, Government, and Citizenship",q:"How should a teacher explain the limited political participation present at the nation\u2019s founding?",options:["The Constitution itself created one uniform property requirement for all voters","Voting eligibility was largely controlled by states, many of which restricted participation by sex, race, property, or legal status","Every adult citizen could vote from the beginning","Political participation was unrelated to state law"],answer:1,explain:"The original Constitution left most voter qualifications to the states. Many states restricted voting through property, sex, race, and status rules, while slavery also denied political rights to enslaved people."},{id:"5004-174",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"United States History, Government, and Citizenship",q:"What is the Tenth Amendment?",options:["Powers not delegated to the federal government are reserved to the states or people","Freedom of speech","Protection against search and seizure","Right to bear arms"],answer:0,explain:"The 10th Amendment establishes federalism by reserving undelegated powers to states and citizens."},{id:"5004-175",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"United States History, Government, and Citizenship",q:"What is a census?",options:["A tax","A count of the population conducted every 10 years","A treaty","A court case"],answer:1,explain:"The decennial census determines congressional apportionment and federal funding distribution."},{id:"5004-176",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"United States History, Government, and Citizenship",q:"What is due process?",options:["A tax code","A voting method","Legal requirement that the government respect all legal rights owed to a person","A type of punishment"],answer:2,explain:"Due process (5th and 14th Amendments) ensures fair treatment through the judicial system."},{id:"5004-177",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"United States History, Government, and Citizenship",q:"Which activity best teaches students about constitutional amendments?",options:["Ignoring amendments","Memorizing all 27","Reading the Constitution cover to cover","Researching a specific amendment's impact on a current issue"],answer:3,explain:"Applying amendment knowledge to contemporary issues makes constitutional study relevant and engaging."},{id:"5004-178",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"United States History, Government, and Citizenship",q:"What is the Supreme Court's role in checks and balances?",options:["Reviewing constitutionality of legislative and executive actions","Collecting taxes","Making laws","Commanding military"],answer:0,explain:"Judicial review allows the Supreme Court to check other branches by invalidating unconstitutional actions."},{id:"5004-179",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"United States History, Government, and Citizenship",q:"How many justices are on the Supreme Court?",options:["13","9","7","11"],answer:1,explain:"The Supreme Court consists of one Chief Justice and eight Associate Justices, set by Congress."},{id:"5004-180",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"United States History, Government, and Citizenship",q:"What is the significance of Marbury v. Madison for civic education?",options:["It created the Electoral College","It created Congress","It established judicial review\u2014showing courts can check other branches, a cornerstone of American governance","It ended slavery"],answer:2,explain:"Marbury v. Madison (1803) is foundational for understanding the judicial branch's role in the system of checks and balances."},{id:"5003-098",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Algebraic Thinking",q:"What is the solution to x + 7 = 15?",options:["7","15","22","8"],answer:3,explain:"Subtract 7 from both sides: x = 15 - 7 = 8."},{id:"5003-099",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Algebraic Thinking",q:"What is the difference between an expression and an equation?",options:["An expression is a combination of terms; an equation states two expressions are equal","Equations have variables; expressions don't","They are the same","Expressions have equals signs"],answer:0,explain:"Expression: 3x + 2. Equation: 3x + 2 = 11. The equals sign distinguishes them."},{id:"5003-100",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Algebraic Thinking",q:"Students think that variables are always unknown numbers. How to broaden understanding?",options:["Tell them they're wrong","Show variables can represent any number in a domain, parameters, or quantities that vary","Agree with them","Use only numbers"],answer:1,explain:"Variables represent unknown or changing quantities\u2014not just missing numbers to solve for."},{id:"5003-101",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Algebraic Thinking",q:"What is the slope of the line passing through (2, 3) and (4, 7)?",options:["1","4","2","3"],answer:2,explain:"Slope is the change in y divided by the change in x: (7 - 3) \xF7 (4 - 2) = 4 \xF7 2 = 2. Reversing only one subtraction would incorrectly produce a negative value."},{id:"5003-102",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Algebraic Thinking",q:"Solve: 3x - 6 = 12",options:["2","18","3","6"],answer:3,explain:"Add 6: 3x = 18. Divide by 3: x = 6."},{id:"5003-103",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Algebraic Thinking",q:"What does the 'm' represent in y = mx + b?",options:["The slope (rate of change)","The x-intercept","A variable","The y-intercept"],answer:0,explain:"In slope-intercept form, m represents the slope\u2014the change in y per unit change in x."},{id:"5003-104",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Algebraic Thinking",q:"Which representation best helps students understand linear relationships?",options:["Calculating only","Connecting tables, graphs, equations, and verbal descriptions","Formulas only","Memorizing y = mx + b"],answer:1,explain:"Multiple representations build deeper understanding: algebraic, graphical, numerical, and verbal."},{id:"5003-105",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Algebraic Thinking",q:"What is a system of equations?",options:["An inequality","One equation","Two or more equations with the same variables, solved simultaneously","A polynomial"],answer:2,explain:"Systems are solved by substitution, elimination, or graphing to find values satisfying all equations."},{id:"5003-106",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Algebraic Thinking",q:"A pattern begins 4, 7, 10, 13. Which rule describes the nth term?",options:["3n + 1","4n - 1","n + 3","4n + 3"],answer:0,explain:"The sequence increases by 3 and begins with 4 when n = 1, so 3n + 1 generates 4, 7, 10, and 13. The other rules do not reproduce all four terms."},{id:"5003-107",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Algebraic Thinking",q:"What does the discriminant b\xB2 - 4ac indicate for a quadratic equation?",options:["The number and type of solutions","The coordinates of the vertex","The y-intercept","The slope of its graph"],answer:0,explain:"For ax\xB2 + bx + c = 0, a positive discriminant gives two distinct real solutions, zero gives one repeated real solution, and a negative value gives two nonreal complex solutions. It does not directly determine the slope, intercept, or vertex."},{id:"5004-181",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"World History and Economics",q:"What was the Renaissance's origin city?",options:["Rome","Florence","London","Paris"],answer:1,explain:"The Renaissance began in Florence, Italy in the 14th century, fueled by wealthy patronage and classical learning."},{id:"5004-182",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"World History and Economics",q:"What caused the Protestant Reformation?",options:["Military conquest","Economic prosperity","Martin Luther's challenge to Catholic Church practices and authority","Scientific discoveries"],answer:2,explain:"Luther's 95 Theses (1517) questioned indulgences and papal authority, sparking religious reform and division."},{id:"5004-183",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"World History and Economics",q:"Students think that economic history is only about money. How to broaden?",options:["Ignore economics","Agree with them","Tell them they're wrong","Show how economic systems shape politics, culture, technology, and social relations"],answer:3,explain:"Economic history intersects with all aspects of human life\u2014technology, culture, power, and inequality."},{id:"5004-184",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"World History and Economics",q:"What is mercantilism's core principle?",options:["Wealth measured by precious metals; colonies exist to benefit the mother country","Free trade","Barter economy","Equal exchange"],answer:0,explain:"Mercantilism viewed trade as zero-sum: accumulating gold/silver through exports exceeding imports."},{id:"5004-185",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"World History and Economics",q:"Which civilization developed democracy?",options:["Roman","Greek (Athens)","Persian","Egyptian"],answer:1,explain:"Athens developed direct democracy around 500 BCE, where citizens voted directly on legislation."},{id:"5004-186",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"World History and Economics",q:"What was the main effect of the Columbian Exchange?",options:["Isolation of continents","Decline in population","Transfer of plants, animals, diseases, and people between Old and New Worlds","No significant change"],answer:2,explain:"The Columbian Exchange transformed global diets, populations, and ecosystems through biological transfer."},{id:"5004-187",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"World History and Economics",q:"Which approach best teaches cause and effect in world history?",options:["Listing events chronologically","Memorizing dates","Reading summaries","Analyzing primary sources to trace multiple causes and consequences"],answer:3,explain:"Primary source analysis reveals complexity of causation better than rote memorization or chronological listing."},{id:"5004-188",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"World History and Economics",q:"What is the significance of the Berlin Conference?",options:["It divided Africa among European powers, ignoring ethnic and cultural boundaries","It ended slavery","It created the UN","It unified Europe"],answer:0,explain:"The Berlin Conference (1884-85) partitioned Africa without African input, creating artificial borders that persist today."},{id:"5005-254",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Physical Science",q:"Why do we feel weightless in orbit?",options:["There is no gravity","We are in continuous free fall\u2014gravity provides centripetal acceleration, but no normal force opposes it","Space has no mass","Gravity is canceled"],answer:1,explain:"Astronauts feel weightless because they and their spacecraft are in free fall together\u2014no surface pushes up."},{id:"5005-255",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Physical Science",q:"Students think that force is needed to keep an object moving. Which law corrects this?",options:["Newton's Second Law","Newton's Third Law","Newton's First Law\u2014objects in motion stay in motion without force","Law of gravity"],answer:2,explain:"Newton's First Law shows force is needed to change motion, not maintain it\u2014inertia maintains velocity."},{id:"5005-256",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Physical Science",q:"What is the relationship between frequency and wavelength?",options:["They are the same","No relationship","Directly proportional","Inversely proportional (c = f\u03BB)"],answer:3,explain:"Frequency \xD7 wavelength = speed of light. Higher frequency means shorter wavelength."},{id:"5005-257",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Physical Science",q:"What is the speed of light?",options:["300,000 km/s","Infinite","343 m/s","150,000 km/s"],answer:0,explain:"Light travels at approximately 3 \xD7 10\u2078 m/s (300,000 km/s) in vacuum."},{id:"5005-258",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Physical Science",q:"What is the difference between heat and temperature?",options:["They are the same","Heat is energy transfer; temperature is average kinetic energy of particles","Neither exists","Temperature is energy; heat is measurement"],answer:1,explain:"Heat flows from hot to cold; temperature measures how hot something is (average molecular kinetic energy)."},{id:"5005-259",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Physical Science",q:"Which demonstration best shows sound waves?",options:["Heating metal","Dropping objects","Using a tuning fork in water to show vibration creates waves","Shining light"],answer:2,explain:"Tuning fork in water creates visible ripples, modeling how sound propagates as longitudinal pressure waves."},{id:"5005-260",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Physical Science",q:"What is the pH scale's range?",options:["0-10","1-100","-1 to 1","0-14"],answer:3,explain:"pH ranges from 0 (very acidic) to 14 (very basic), with 7 being neutral."},{id:"5005-261",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Earth Science",q:"How do scientists date rocks relatively?",options:["Using principles like superposition (older layers below) and fossil succession","Using carbon dating only","Measuring temperature","Counting grains"],answer:0,explain:"Relative dating uses stratigraphic principles: superposition, cross-cutting relationships, and faunal succession."},{id:"5005-262",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Earth Science",q:"Students think that geologic time is too vast to understand. How to make it accessible?",options:["Tell them to memorize dates","Use analogies: Earth's history as a 24-hour day; humans appear in the last second","Skip geologic time","Agree with them"],answer:1,explain:"Analogies (compressed time scales) help students grasp deep time without being overwhelmed by billions of years."},{id:"5005-263",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Earth Science",q:"What is the crust made of?",options:["Water","Gas","Solid rock (granite on continents, basalt on ocean floors)","Magma"],answer:2,explain:"The crust is Earth's thin solid outer shell\u2014granite-rich on continents, basaltic on ocean floors."},{id:"5005-264",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Earth Science",q:"Why do earthquakes happen at transform boundaries?",options:["Volcanic activity","Subduction only","Divergence creates earthquakes","Plates slide past each other, building stress that releases as earthquakes"],answer:3,explain:"At transform boundaries (e.g., San Andreas Fault), plates slide laterally\u2014friction builds stress until sudden slip causes earthquakes."},{id:"5005-265",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Earth Science",q:"Students think earthquakes are random and unpredictable. How to address?",options:["Show earthquake patterns along plate boundaries and seismic gaps where stress is accumulating","Agree with them","Tell them they're wrong","Skip earthquake science"],answer:0,explain:"Earthquakes cluster at plate boundaries; recognizing patterns helps assess seismic risk."},{id:"5005-266",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Earth Science",q:"What is a fault line?",options:["An ocean trench","A fracture in Earth's crust where blocks of rock move past each other","A type of volcano","A crack in the sky"],answer:1,explain:"Fault lines mark boundaries where tectonic stress causes displacement\u2014locations of most earthquakes."},{id:"5002-170",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Reading",q:"What is imagery in literature?",options:["A type of poetry","A photograph","Language that appeals to the senses to create mental pictures","A character trait"],answer:2,explain:"Imagery uses descriptive language to engage sight, sound, smell, touch, or taste, creating vivid mental pictures."},{id:"5002-171",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Reading",q:"Why is author's purpose important to identify?",options:["All authors have the same purpose","It only affects title","It doesn't matter","It guides interpretation\u2014whether to inform, persuade, entertain, or explain shapes reading strategy"],answer:3,explain:"Recognizing author's purpose (PIE: Persuade, Inform, Entertain) helps readers adjust their approach and expectations."},{id:"5002-172",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Reading",q:"Students memorize plot events but cannot analyze themes. What skill gap exists?",options:["They lack abstraction skills\u2014moving from concrete events to universal ideas","They don't understand vocabulary","They read too much","They need more practice reading"],answer:0,explain:"Theme identification requires abstract thinking\u2014students must extrapolate from specific events to general messages."},{id:"5002-173",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Reading",q:"What is direct characterization?",options:["Showing through actions only","Author explicitly tells reader about a character's traits","Character describes themselves","Reader infers from dialogue"],answer:1,explain:"Direct characterization states traits outright ('She was brave'). Indirect shows through behavior, dialogue, thoughts."},{id:"5002-174",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Reading",q:"How does setting influence a story's mood?",options:["Only dialogue matters","Mood is unrelated to setting","Atmospheric details (weather, time of day, location) create emotional tone","Setting has no effect"],answer:2,explain:"A stormy night in a decaying mansion creates dread; a sunny meadow creates peace\u2014setting shapes emotional atmosphere."},{id:"5002-175",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Reading",q:"Which strategy best helps students distinguish fact from opinion in texts?",options:["Ignoring the distinction","Reading more texts","Memorizing definitions","Practicing verification: can this be proven? Is it subjective judgment?"],answer:3,explain:"Critical evaluation through verifiability tests builds media literacy and analytical reading skills."},{id:"5002-176",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Reading",q:"What is point of view in narrative?",options:["The perspective from which the story is told (first, second, third person)","The story's ending","A character's opinion","The physical location of characters"],answer:0,explain:"POV determines what information the reader receives and shapes empathy and interpretation."},{id:"5002-177",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Reading",q:"Why might two readers disagree about a text's meaning?",options:["Because the text is badly written","Because meaning is co-constructed\u2014readers bring different backgrounds, values, and experiences","Because one reader is wrong","Meaning is always objective"],answer:1,explain:"Reader-response theory acknowledges that texts invite multiple valid interpretations based on reader context."},{id:"5003-108",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Numbers and Operations",q:"Why does dividing by a fraction mean multiplying by its reciprocal?",options:["Fractions cannot be divided","It is arbitrary","Division asks 'how many times does the divisor fit?' Reciprocal conversion makes this computable","It only works with whole numbers"],answer:2,explain:"a \xF7 b/c = a \xD7 c/b because dividing by a fraction asks how many parts of that size fit into the dividend."},{id:"5003-109",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Numbers and Operations",q:"Students think that negative \xD7 negative = negative. How to correct?",options:["Tell them it's wrong","Skip negative multiplication","Give more practice","Use number patterns: (-3)\xD73=-9, (-3)\xD72=-6, (-3)\xD71=-3, (-3)\xD70=0, so (-3)\xD7(-1)=3"],answer:3,explain:"Pattern continuation shows the rule must be consistent: subtracting a negative adds."},{id:"5003-110",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Numbers and Operations",q:"What is 2/5 expressed as a percentage?",options:["40%","20%","50%","25%"],answer:0,explain:"Dividing 2 by 5 gives 0.4, and multiplying by 100 converts the decimal to 40%. The distractor 20% reverses the numerator and denominator relationship."},{id:"5003-111",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Numbers and Operations",q:"What is the greatest common factor of 24 and 36?",options:["6","12","24","8"],answer:1,explain:"Factors of 24: 1,2,3,4,6,8,12,24. Factors of 36: 1,2,3,4,6,9,12,18,36. GCF = 12."},{id:"5003-112",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Numbers and Operations",q:"Why is 1 not considered a prime number?",options:["1 is too small","1 is even","By definition, primes have exactly two distinct positive factors; 1 has only one","It is divisible by 1"],answer:2,explain:"Excluding 1 preserves the uniqueness of prime factorization\u2014a cornerstone of number theory."},{id:"5003-113",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Numbers and Operations",q:"Which model best teaches fraction equivalence?",options:["Long division","Memorizing rules","Decimal conversion only","Using fraction bars or circles to show 1/2 = 2/4 = 3/6 visually"],answer:3,explain:"Visual models make equivalence concrete\u2014same quantity represented with different denominators."},{id:"5003-114",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Numbers and Operations",q:"What is 0.75 as a fraction in simplest form?",options:["3/4","75/100","7/5","1/4"],answer:0,explain:"0.75 = 75/100 = 3/4 after simplifying by dividing numerator and denominator by 25."},{id:"5003-115",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Numbers and Operations",q:"What is the square root of 81?",options:["7","9","8","11"],answer:1,explain:"9 \xD7 9 = 81, so \u221A81 = 9."},{id:"5003-116",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Numbers and Operations",q:"Why is the product of any number and zero always zero?",options:["The result depends on the number","Zero has no place value","n \xD7 0 means n groups of zero, so the sum is zero","Zero changes every number to one"],answer:2,explain:"Multiplication can represent equal groups: n \xD7 0 is n groups containing zero objects. Adding zero n times gives zero, so the product is always zero."},{id:"5004-189",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"Geography, Anthropology, and Sociology",q:"What is a culture trait?",options:["A geographic location","A physical feature","A genetic characteristic","A single element of learned behavior shared by members of a society"],answer:3,explain:"Culture traits are individual elements of culture\u2014language, dress, food customs, religious practices."},{id:"5004-190",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"Geography, Anthropology, and Sociology",q:"What is diffusion in cultural geography?",options:["The spread of cultural traits from one society to another","Genetic migration","Cultural isolation","Cultural extinction"],answer:0,explain:"Cultural diffusion spreads ideas, technologies, and practices through trade, migration, and communication."},{id:"5004-191",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"Geography, Anthropology, and Sociology",q:"Students think that culture is static. How to correct?",options:["Ignore cultural change","Show how cultures evolve through innovation, contact, and adaptation over time","Agree with them","Tell them they're wrong"],answer:1,explain:"Culture is dynamic\u2014shaped by technology, migration, conquest, and internal innovation."},{id:"5004-192",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"Geography, Anthropology, and Sociology",q:"What is ethnic identity?",options:["A political affiliation","A geographic location","Shared cultural heritage including language, religion, ancestry, and traditions","A genetic category"],answer:2,explain:"Ethnicity is cultural identity based on shared heritage, not biological determinism."},{id:"5004-193",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"Geography, Anthropology, and Sociology",q:"What is urbanization's main driver?",options:["Population decline","Rural tourism","Agricultural expansion","Industrialization and economic opportunity concentrating in cities"],answer:3,explain:"Industrialization creates urban jobs, drawing rural populations into cities for employment."},{id:"5004-194",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"Geography, Anthropology, and Sociology",q:"How are migration and displacement related?",options:["Migration includes voluntary and forced movement; displacement specifically means people are forced from their homes","They are unrelated processes","Displacement is always voluntary","Migration occurs only across international borders"],answer:0,explain:"Migration is a broad term for movement from one place to another and may be voluntary or forced. Displacement is a form of forced movement caused by conflict, disaster, persecution, or similar pressures."},{id:"5004-195",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"Geography, Anthropology, and Sociology",q:"Which approach best teaches cultural awareness?",options:["Memorizing cultural facts","Reflective activities examining one's own cultural assumptions and biases","Reading about other cultures","Ignoring differences"],answer:1,explain:"Self-reflection on one's own cultural lens builds deeper intercultural competence than factual knowledge alone."},{id:"5004-196",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"Geography, Anthropology, and Sociology",q:"What is social mobility?",options:["Cultural diffusion","Physical movement","The ability to move between social strata (upward or downward)","A geographic concept"],answer:2,explain:"Social mobility measures change in socioeconomic status across generations or within a lifetime."},{id:"5004-197",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"Geography, Anthropology, and Sociology",q:"What is a census used for?",options:["Tracking weather","Counting animals","Measuring land area","Counting population to allocate resources and political representation"],answer:3,explain:"Censuses inform legislative representation, funding distribution, and policy planning."},{id:"5004-198",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"Geography, Anthropology, and Sociology",q:"What is the demographic transition model's final stage?",options:["Low birth and death rates with population stability or decline","High birth and death rates","Rapid population growth","No population change"],answer:0,explain:"Stage 4 (post-industrial): low fertility and mortality lead to population stabilization or gradual decline."},{id:"5005-267",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Life Science",q:"What is an organelle?",options:["A cell type","A specialized structure within a cell that performs a specific function","A type of tissue","An organ system"],answer:1,explain:"Organelles (mitochondria, ribosomes, nucleus) are membrane-bound or non-membrane structures with specific cellular functions."},{id:"5005-268",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Life Science",q:"Why are mitochondria called the 'powerhouse' of the cell?",options:["They store energy as fat","They generate heat","They produce ATP through cellular respiration, providing usable energy","They create electrical impulses"],answer:2,explain:"Mitochondria convert glucose and oxygen into ATP via aerobic respiration\u2014the cell's primary energy currency."},{id:"5005-269",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Life Science",q:"Students think plant cells have mitochondria only because they photosynthesize. How to correct?",options:["Agree with them","Skip cellular respiration","Tell them they're wrong","Explain that plants need mitochondria to convert photosynthetic sugar into ATP for cellular work"],answer:3,explain:"Plants perform both photosynthesis (making sugar) and cellular respiration (breaking sugar down for ATP)."},{id:"5005-270",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Life Science",q:"What is the endoplasmic reticulum's role?",options:["Protein and lipid synthesis and transport within the cell","Cell division","Energy storage","Waste disposal"],answer:0,explain:"Rough ER (with ribosomes) synthesizes proteins; smooth ER synthesizes lipids and detoxifies."},{id:"5005-271",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Life Science",q:"What is the endosymbiotic theory?",options:["All cells are identical","Mitochondria and chloroplasts originated as free-living bacteria engulfed by ancestral cells","Cells do not evolve","Cells evolved from viruses"],answer:1,explain:"Endosymbiosis explains why mitochondria and chloroplasts have their own DNA and double membranes."},{id:"5005-272",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Life Science",q:"Which analogy best represents the cell as a factory?",options:["Cell = building","No analogy works","Nucleus = management; ribosomes = workers; mitochondria = power plant; membrane = security","Random comparison"],answer:2,explain:"Factory analogies map cellular components to functional roles, making abstract concepts tangible."},{id:"5005-273",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Life Science",q:"What is the Golgi apparatus function?",options:["Cell division","Energy production","DNA replication","Modifying, sorting, and packaging proteins for secretion or delivery"],answer:3,explain:"The Golgi modifies proteins from the ER, packages them into vesicles, and ships them to their destinations."},{id:"5005-274",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Life Science",q:"What is a lysosome?",options:["A digestive organelle containing enzymes to break down waste","A protein maker","A structural component","An energy producer"],answer:0,explain:"Lysosomes contain hydrolytic enzymes that digest worn-out organelles, food particles, and pathogens."},{id:"5005-275",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Life Science",q:"Why do cells specialize (differentiate)?",options:["All cells are identical","Different genes are expressed in different cells, enabling multicellular organisms to perform diverse functions","Random chance","Specialization is inefficient"],answer:1,explain:"Cell differentiation allows division of labor\u2014neurons conduct signals, muscle cells contract, red blood cells carry oxygen."},{id:"5005-276",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Physical Science",q:"What is inertia?",options:["Energy of motion","The force of gravity","An object's resistance to changes in motion","Speed of light"],answer:2,explain:"Inertia is the tendency of objects to resist changes in velocity\u2014directly related to mass."},{id:"5005-277",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Physical Science",q:"Why does a ball thrown upward eventually fall back down?",options:["It runs out of energy","It loses momentum spontaneously","Air pressure pushes it down","Gravity continuously accelerates it downward, slowing its rise then pulling it back"],answer:3,explain:"Gravity acts constantly downward, decelerating the ball on ascent and accelerating it on descent."},{id:"5005-278",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Physical Science",q:"Students think heavier objects fall faster in the absence of air resistance. Which demonstration best tests the claim?",options:["Drop a feather and coin in an evacuated tube and observe that they fall together","Drop a flat sheet of paper and a book in air without discussing drag","Compare the objects\u2019 weights without dropping them","Use a story about Galileo without collecting evidence"],answer:0,explain:"In an evacuated tube, air resistance is removed and objects of different masses accelerate equally under gravity. The feather-and-coin vacuum demonstration should not be conflated with the disputed Leaning Tower story."},{id:"5005-279",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Physical Science",q:"What is momentum?",options:["Energy of position","Product of mass and velocity (p = mv)","Force of gravity","Rate of acceleration"],answer:1,explain:"Momentum quantifies motion quantity\u2014both how much matter moves and how fast it moves."},{id:"5005-280",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Physical Science",q:"What is friction?",options:["Magnetic attraction","A force that aids motion","A force that opposes relative motion between surfaces","Gravity on slopes"],answer:2,explain:"Friction resists sliding motion between surfaces, converting kinetic energy into heat."},{id:"5005-281",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Physical Science",q:"What is potential energy stored in a compressed spring called?",options:["Thermal energy","Gravitational energy","Kinetic energy","Elastic potential energy"],answer:3,explain:"Elastic potential energy is stored when a spring is compressed or stretched, ready to be released as kinetic energy."},{id:"5005-282",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Physical Science",q:"Which experiment best demonstrates conservation of momentum?",options:["Colliding toy carts on a track and measuring velocities before and after","Dropping objects","Pulling strings","Rolling balls downhill"],answer:0,explain:"Cart collisions with motion sensors show total momentum before equals total momentum after (in isolated systems)."},{id:"5005-283",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Physical Science",q:"What is work in physics?",options:["Any effort","Force applied over a distance (W = F \xD7 d)","Power output","Energy creation"],answer:1,explain:"Work = force \xD7 distance \xD7 cos(\u03B8). You do work on an object when you move it against a force."},{id:"5005-284",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Physical Science",q:"What is the unit of energy?",options:["Watt","Newton","Joule","Pascal"],answer:2,explain:"One joule = one newton-meter. Energy, work, and heat are all measured in joules."},{id:"5005-285",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Physical Science",q:"Why does a rocket accelerate upward despite no air to push against?",options:["It pushes against air","Gravity reverses","Rocket is lighter than air","Newton's third law: exhaust gases push down, rocket pushes up\u2014no air needed"],answer:3,explain:"Rocket propulsion works in vacuum\u2014action-reaction pair between rocket and expelled gas, not air resistance."},{id:"5002-178",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Writing, Speaking, and Listening",q:"What is the first step in the writing process?",options:["Prewriting/planning (generating ideas, organizing)","Publishing","Proofreading","Editing"],answer:0,explain:"Prewriting includes brainstorming, outlining, and planning\u2014foundational steps before drafting."},{id:"5002-179",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Writing, Speaking, and Listening",q:"Why is peer review valuable in writing instruction?",options:["It wastes class time","It develops critical reading skills and provides authentic audience feedback","Teachers should do all reviewing","Students cannot give useful feedback"],answer:1,explain:"Peer review builds evaluative skills and gives writers perspectives beyond the teacher's."},{id:"5002-180",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Writing, Speaking, and Listening",q:"Students write complete sentences but lack coherence between them. Which skill needs development?",options:["Sentence length","Grammar","Transitional reasoning\u2014connecting ideas logically so paragraphs flow","Spelling"],answer:2,explain:"Coherence requires logical connectors and clear relationships between ideas, not just correct sentences."},{id:"5002-181",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Writing, Speaking, and Listening",q:"What is a revision checklist used for?",options:["Counting words","Grading peer work","Decorating papers","Systematically checking content, organization, and mechanics before submission"],answer:3,explain:"Checklists ensure writers systematically address all aspects: thesis, evidence, organization, grammar."},{id:"5002-182",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Writing, Speaking, and Listening",q:"What is the purpose of an outline?",options:["To organize ideas hierarchically before writing","To decorate an essay","To replace writing","To count words"],answer:0,explain:"Outlines structure arguments logically, ensuring coherent progression from claim to evidence to conclusion."},{id:"5002-183",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Writing, Speaking, and Listening",q:"Why is the writing process recursive rather than linear?",options:["Because editing comes first","Because writers often revisit earlier stages (revising leads to rethinking, which leads to reorganizing)","Because there is no process","Because it follows strict steps"],answer:1,explain:"Writing involves ongoing cycles of drafting, evaluating, and revising\u2014not a simple one-way sequence."},{id:"5002-184",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Writing, Speaking, and Listening",q:"Students struggle with spoken presentations. Which scaffold helps most?",options:["Allowing phone reading","Having them read from paper","Teaching structured speaking: clear thesis, signposting, eye contact, practiced delivery","Skipping presentations"],answer:2,explain:"Explicit instruction in presentation skills (structure, delivery, engagement) builds oral communication competence."},{id:"5002-185",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Writing, Speaking, and Listening",q:"What is a call to action in persuasive writing?",options:["An introduction","A bibliography entry","A question","A statement urging readers to take specific action based on the argument"],answer:3,explain:"Calls to action give readers a concrete next step, making persuasion actionable rather than abstract."},{id:"5002-186",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Writing, Speaking, and Listening",q:"What is active listening?",options:["Fully concentrating, understanding, responding, and remembering what is said","Waiting to speak","Listening while doing other tasks","Hearing without responding"],answer:0,explain:"Active listening involves engagement, reflection, and responsive feedback\u2014not passive hearing."},{id:"5002-187",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Writing, Speaking, and Listening",q:"Why is writing process instruction important across disciplines?",options:["Only English teachers need it","All subjects require students to plan, draft, revise, and communicate ideas clearly","Writing is only for language arts","Process doesn't matter"],answer:1,explain:"Writing processes transfer across subjects\u2014science reports, history essays, math explanations all require composition skills."},{id:"5004-199",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"World History and Economics",q:"What triggered the French Revolution?",options:["Foreign invasion","Religious war","Financial crisis, social inequality, and Enlightenment ideas challenging monarchy","Natural disaster"],answer:2,explain:"France's financial crisis, unfair taxation, and Enlightenment ideals sparked the 1789 revolution."},{id:"5004-200",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"World History and Economics",q:"What was the main economic impact of colonialism on Africa?",options:["Economic self-sufficiency","Trade equality","Industrialization","Extraction of resources, disruption of local economies, and artificial border creation"],answer:3,explain:"Colonial economies were structured to export raw materials to Europe, undermining local production and creating dependency."},{id:"5004-201",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"World History and Economics",q:"Students think history is just memorizing dates. How to shift to historical thinking?",options:["Teach sourcing, corroboration, contextualization, and causation through primary documents","Skip critical analysis","Tell them to memorize more","Agree with them"],answer:0,explain:"Historical thinking emphasizes analysis of evidence, not chronological recall\u2014skills transferable to civic life."},{id:"5004-202",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"World History and Economics",q:"What is the legacy of the Silk Road?",options:["Isolation of civilizations","Cultural, religious, and technological exchange between East and West","Only trade in silk","Military conquest"],answer:1,explain:"The Silk Road transmitted Buddhism, Islam, papermaking, gunpowder, and ideas across continents."},{id:"5004-203",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"World History and Economics",q:"Who led the Chinese Communist Revolution in 1949?",options:["Sun Yat-sen","Kangxi Emperor","Mao Zedong","Deng Xiaoping"],answer:2,explain:"Mao Zedong established the People's Republic of China in 1949 after defeating Nationalist forces."},{id:"5004-204",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"World History and Economics",q:"What is the significance of the Magna Carta for global governance?",options:["It created democracy","It was irrelevant","It abolished monarchy everywhere","It established that rulers are subject to law\u2014a principle adopted worldwide"],answer:3,explain:"The Magna Carta's rule-of-law principle influenced constitutions worldwide, including the US Bill of Rights."},{id:"5004-205",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"World History and Economics",q:"Which activity best teaches students about historical causation?",options:["Analyzing a chain of events to distinguish proximate vs. root causes","Reading summaries","Listing battles","Memorizing dates"],answer:0,explain:"Distinguishing immediate triggers from deep structural causes develops nuanced historical understanding."},{id:"5004-206",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"World History and Economics",q:"What is the Enlightenment?",options:["An economic system","An intellectual movement emphasizing reason, individual rights, and scientific inquiry","A war","A religious movement"],answer:1,explain:"The Enlightenment (17th-18th centuries) championed reason, liberty, and progress, influencing democratic revolutions."},{id:"5004-207",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"World History and Economics",q:"Why did industrialization begin in Britain first?",options:["Britain had no competing industries","Britain was Europe\u2019s largest country by land area","Britain combined accessible coal, investment capital, stable institutions, colonial markets, and agricultural change","Britain had Europe\u2019s largest population"],answer:2,explain:"Industrialization began in Britain because several favorable resources and institutions converged. Population or land area alone cannot explain why mechanized industry developed there first."},{id:"5003-117",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Algebraic Thinking",q:"What is the slope of a horizontal line?",options:["Undefined","1","-1","0"],answer:3,explain:"Horizontal lines have zero rise, so slope = 0/run = 0."},{id:"5003-118",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Algebraic Thinking",q:"What does the y-intercept represent in a real-world linear model?",options:["Starting value when x = 0","Zero point","Rate of change","Maximum value"],answer:0,explain:"In y = mx + b, b is the initial value\u2014the output when input is zero (e.g., starting balance, base fee)."},{id:"5003-119",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Algebraic Thinking",q:"A student translates 'three more than twice a number' as 3(x + 2). Which feedback best addresses the error?",options:["Confirm that 3(x + 2) is correct","Explain that 'twice a number' is 2x and 'three more than' means add 3, so the expression is 2x + 3","Replace x with 3 and keep the expression","Tell the student to reverse every operation word"],answer:1,explain:"The phrase 'twice a number' forms 2x, and 'three more than' adds 3, giving 2x + 3. The expression 3(x + 2) incorrectly multiplies the entire sum by 3."},{id:"5003-120",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Algebraic Thinking",q:"How do you find the x-intercept of y = 3x - 9?",options:["Multiply by -9","Set x = 0 and solve for y","Set y = 0 and solve for x","Divide by 3"],answer:2,explain:"X-intercept occurs where y = 0: 0 = 3x - 9 \u2192 x = 3."},{id:"5003-121",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Algebraic Thinking",q:"What is the solution to 5x = 35?",options:["40","30","5","7"],answer:3,explain:"Divide both sides by 5: x = 35 \xF7 5 = 7."},{id:"5003-122",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Algebraic Thinking",q:"When do two linear equations have no solution?",options:["When they have the same slope but different y-intercepts (parallel lines)","Always","When they intersect","When they have different slopes"],answer:0,explain:"Parallel lines never meet\u2014same steepness (slope) but different starting positions (y-intercept)."},{id:"5003-123",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Algebraic Thinking",q:"Which representation best helps students understand slope as a rate?",options:["Formulas only","Real-world scenarios: cost per item, speed, density\u2014connecting abstract slope to tangible rates","Graphing only","Memorizing m = rise/run"],answer:1,explain:"Contextualizing slope as 'per' relationships (dollars per pound, miles per hour) builds conceptual understanding."},{id:"5003-124",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Algebraic Thinking",q:"Solve: 4(x - 2) = 20",options:["12","3","7","5"],answer:2,explain:"Divide by 4: x - 2 = 5. Add 2: x = 7."},{id:"5003-125",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Algebraic Thinking",q:"What is the slope of the line through (0, 3) and (4, 11)?",options:["1","4","3","2"],answer:3,explain:"The slope is (11 - 3) \xF7 (4 - 0) = 8 \xF7 4 = 2. Using only the change in y would incorrectly give 8 instead of a rate of change."},{id:"5003-126",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Algebraic Thinking",q:"What is the significance of a negative slope in context?",options:["As x increases, y decreases\u2014a decreasing linear relationship","The line rises from left to right","There is no relationship between x and y","The line is vertical"],answer:0,explain:"A negative slope means y decreases at a constant rate as x increases. This is a decreasing linear relationship, not inverse variation, which usually has the form y = k/x."},{id:"5005-286",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Earth Science",q:"What is the geosphere?",options:["Living things","Earth's solid rocky material (crust, mantle, core)","Earth's water","Earth's air"],answer:1,explain:"The geosphere encompasses all solid Earth\u2014rocks, minerals, and landforms from crust to core."},{id:"5005-287",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Earth Science",q:"How do scientists determine Earth's age?",options:["Fossil counting","Biblical genealogy","Radiometric dating of oldest rocks and meteorites (~4.6 billion years)","Historical records"],answer:2,explain:"Radioactive isotope decay rates in zircon crystals and meteorites provide absolute ages for Earth's formation."},{id:"5005-288",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Earth Science",q:"Students think that rocks never change once formed. How to correct?",options:["Agree with them","Tell them they're wrong","Skip metamorphic rocks","Show the rock cycle: igneous \u2192 sedimentary \u2192 metamorphic \u2192 melted \u2192 igneous, continuously recycling"],answer:3,explain:"The rock cycle demonstrates that rocks are not permanent\u2014they transform through Earth's dynamic processes."},{id:"5005-289",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Earth Science",q:"What is the Mohs hardness scale based on?",options:["Scratch resistance\u2014relative ability of one mineral to scratch another","Density","Color","Weight"],answer:0,explain:"Mohs scale ranks minerals 1-10 by which can scratch which\u2014talc(1) scratches nothing; diamond(10) scratches all."},{id:"5005-290",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Earth Science",q:"What is a mineral's luster?",options:["Its crystal shape","How light reflects off its surface (metallic or non-metallic)","Its color only","Its hardness"],answer:1,explain:"Luster describes light reflection: metallic (shiny like metal) or non-metallic (glassy, dull, pearly, etc.)."},{id:"5005-291",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Earth Science",q:"Why do sedimentary rocks form in layers?",options:["Wind patterns","Ocean currents only","Successive deposits settle over time, creating stratification with oldest at bottom","Random deposition"],answer:2,explain:"Stratification results from sequential deposition\u2014each layer represents a different time period."},{id:"5005-292",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Earth Science",q:"Which hands-on activity best teaches rock identification?",options:["Looking at photos","Reading descriptions","Memorizing names","Using a rock kit with hand lens to observe texture, color, hardness, and streak"],answer:3,explain:"Hands-on examination with tools develops observational skills and mineral identification competence."},{id:"5005-293",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Earth Science",q:"What is magma?",options:["Molten rock beneath Earth's surface","Cooled lava","Solid rock","Gas in volcanoes"],answer:0,explain:"Magma is molten rock stored in the mantle; when it reaches the surface, it is called lava."},{id:"5005-294",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Earth Science",q:"What is erosion?",options:["Rock formation","Transport of weathered material by water, wind, ice, or gravity","Volcanic eruption","Earthquake"],answer:1,explain:"Erosion moves weathered material\u2014distinct from weathering, which breaks rock in place."},{id:"5005-295",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Earth Science",q:"Why are some mountains volcanic while others are folded?",options:["Random chance","Weather creates mountains","Volcanic mountains form at subduction zones/mantle plumes; folded mountains form at convergent continental boundaries","All mountains are the same"],answer:2,explain:"Different tectonic settings produce different mountain types: subduction creates volcanoes; continent-continent collision creates folds."},{id:"5003-127",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Geometry and Measurement, Data, Statistics, and Probability",q:"What is the area of a triangle with base 10 and height 6?",options:["60","36","16","30"],answer:3,explain:"Area = \xBD \xD7 base \xD7 height = \xBD \xD7 10 \xD7 6 = 30 square units."},{id:"5003-128",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Geometry and Measurement, Data, Statistics, and Probability",q:"Why is the volume formula for a pyramid \u2153 \xD7 base \xD7 height?",options:["A pyramid fills one-third the volume of a prism with the same base and height","It equals a cone","No geometric reason","It is arbitrary"],answer:0,explain:"Calculus or Cavalieri's principle shows pyramid volume is one-third of the enclosing prism."},{id:"5003-129",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Geometry and Measurement, Data, Statistics, and Probability",q:"Students confuse area and perimeter formulas. Which manipulative helps distinguish them?",options:["Memorize both formulas","Use grid paper: count unit squares for area, count unit segments for perimeter","Ignore the confusion","Draw randomly"],answer:1,explain:"Grid paper makes area (filled squares) and perimeter (border segments) visually distinct."},{id:"5003-130",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Geometry and Measurement, Data, Statistics, and Probability",q:"What is the circumference of a circle with diameter 10 (use \u03C0 \u2248 3.14)?",options:["100","78.5","31.4","15.7"],answer:2,explain:"Circumference = \u03C0d = 3.14 \xD7 10 = 31.4 units."},{id:"5003-131",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Geometry and Measurement, Data, Statistics, and Probability",q:"What is the median of 5, 10, 15, 20, 25?",options:["25","5","20","15"],answer:3,explain:"Median is the middle value in ordered data: 15 is the third of five values."},{id:"5003-132",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Geometry and Measurement, Data, Statistics, and Probability",q:"What is the probability of drawing an ace from a standard 52-card deck?",options:["4/52 = 1/13","52/4","13/52 = 1/4","1/52"],answer:0,explain:"4 aces in 52 cards: P(ace) = 4/52 = 1/13 \u2248 7.7%."},{id:"5003-133",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Geometry and Measurement, Data, Statistics, and Probability",q:"Which tool best helps students understand volume of irregular shapes?",options:["Formulas only","Water displacement method: measure volume change when object is submerged","Memory techniques","Counting cubes only"],answer:1,explain:"Water displacement provides a practical measurement technique for volumes that lack simple formulas."},{id:"5003-134",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Geometry and Measurement, Data, Statistics, and Probability",q:"What is the range of 4, 8, 12, 16, 20?",options:["8","12","16","4"],answer:2,explain:"Range = maximum - minimum = 20 - 4 = 16."},{id:"5003-135",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Geometry and Measurement, Data, Statistics, and Probability",q:"Why is the sum of angles in a triangle always 180\xB0?",options:["Only true for right triangles","It is a convention","It varies by triangle type","Drawing a line parallel to one side through the opposite vertex shows angles form a straight line"],answer:3,explain:"Parallel line proof: alternate interior angles are equal, so the three angles form a straight angle (180\xB0)."},{id:"5004-208",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"United States History, Government, and Citizenship",q:"What is the federal budget process?",options:["The President submits a budget request; Congress develops and passes spending legislation; the President signs or vetoes it","The President alone determines all federal spending","The Supreme Court writes the annual budget","State governments approve the federal budget"],answer:0,explain:"The executive branch submits a budget request, but Congress exercises the power of the purse by enacting appropriations and other spending legislation. The President then signs or vetoes the legislation."},{id:"5004-209",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"United States History, Government, and Citizenship",q:"What is the significance of the 14th Amendment?",options:["It set voting age to 18","It granted citizenship to all born in the US and guaranteed equal protection under law","It granted women's suffrage","It abolished slavery"],answer:1,explain:"The 14th Amendment (1868) defined citizenship, guaranteed due process and equal protection\u2014foundation for civil rights litigation."},{id:"5004-210",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"United States History, Government, and Citizenship",q:"Students think government is only about politics and voting. How to broaden understanding?",options:["Tell them they're wrong","Agree with them","Show government's daily impact: schools, roads, safety, environment, commerce regulation","Focus only on elections"],answer:2,explain:"Government touches every aspect of life\u2014helping students see policy connections builds civic engagement."},{id:"5004-211",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"United States History, Government, and Citizenship",q:"What is the role of interest groups?",options:["They make laws","They command the military","They interpret laws","They advocate for specific policies and influence legislators"],answer:3,explain:"Interest groups lobby legislators, mobilize voters, and file amicus briefs to influence policy outcomes."},{id:"5004-212",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"United States History, Government, and Citizenship",q:"How many senators represent each state?",options:["Two","Varies by state","Based on population","One"],answer:0,explain:"Each state has exactly two senators, ensuring equal representation regardless of population size."},{id:"5004-213",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"World History and Economics",q:"What is the role of the Federal Reserve?",options:["Making laws","Managing monetary policy: controlling money supply and interest rates","Running the military","Collecting taxes"],answer:1,explain:"The Fed controls interest rates and money supply to manage inflation, employment, and economic growth."},{id:"5004-214",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"United States History, Government, and Citizenship",q:"Which simulation best teaches students about the legislative process?",options:["Watching C-SPAN","Memorizing procedures","Having students draft, debate, amend, and vote on a mock bill through both houses","Reading about Congress"],answer:2,explain:"Legislative simulation gives students authentic experience with compromise, debate, and procedural rules."},{id:"5004-215",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"United States History, Government, and Citizenship",q:"What is the Electoral College's current total number of electors?",options:["435","50","100","538"],answer:3,explain:"538 electors = 435 Representatives + 100 Senators + 3 (DC). Majority needed: 270."},{id:"5004-216",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"United States History, Government, and Citizenship",q:"Who was the longest-serving U.S. president?",options:["Franklin D. Roosevelt","Thomas Jefferson","George Washington","Abraham Lincoln"],answer:0,explain:"FDR served four terms (1933-1945) before the 22nd Amendment limited presidents to two terms."},{id:"5004-217",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"United States History, Government, and Citizenship",q:"What is the tension between national and state power called in modern context?",options:["Amendment process","Federalism disputes over reserved vs. enumerated powers","Impeachment","Judicial review"],answer:1,explain:"Federalism tensions continue over issues like healthcare, education standards, and marijuana legalization."},{id:"5005-296",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Physical Science",q:"What is the atomic number of carbon?",options:["14","8","6","12"],answer:2,explain:"Carbon has 6 protons, so its atomic number is 6. Its atomic mass is approximately 12."},{id:"5005-297",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Physical Science",q:"Why do isotopes of the same element have different masses?",options:["Different number of protons","They are different elements","Different electron configurations","Different number of neutrons in the nucleus"],answer:3,explain:"Isotopes have same proton count (same element) but varying neutron counts, creating mass differences."},{id:"5005-298",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Physical Science",q:"Students ask why solid objects do not pass through one another if atoms contain large regions without nuclei. Which explanation is best?",options:["Electron clouds interact through electromagnetic forces that resist overlap, giving matter its solidity","Atoms are completely solid balls with no internal structure","Gravity alone prevents atoms from overlapping","The empty-space description means ordinary objects should pass through each other"],answer:0,explain:"Atomic volume is defined largely by electron clouds, and electromagnetic interactions strongly resist their overlap. Therefore a simplified \u201Cmostly empty space\u201D picture does not imply that macroscopic objects can pass through one another."},{id:"5005-299",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Physical Science",q:"What is an ion?",options:["A subatomic particle","An atom with unequal protons and electrons (charged)","A type of molecule","A neutral atom"],answer:1,explain:"Ions form when atoms gain (anions, negative) or lose (cations, positive) electrons."},{id:"5005-300",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Physical Science",q:"What is the periodic table organized by?",options:["Random order","Decreasing mass","Increasing atomic number (proton count)","Alphabetical order"],answer:2,explain:"Elements are ordered by atomic number; rows (periods) and columns (groups) reveal periodic properties."},{id:"5005-301",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Physical Science",q:"Why do elements in the same group have similar properties?",options:["They are all metals","They are the same size","They have same mass","They have the same number of valence electrons, determining chemical behavior"],answer:3,explain:"Valence electrons determine bonding behavior\u2014elements in the same column share valence electron configurations."},{id:"5005-302",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Physical Science",q:"Which model best represents atomic structure for students?",options:["Cloud/model showing nucleus with electron probability regions, acknowledging quantum uncertainty","Solid sphere model","Solar system model only","Bohr model only"],answer:0,explain:"Quantum mechanical model (electron clouds) is more accurate than Bohr's orbits, though both have pedagogical value."},{id:"5005-303",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Physical Science",q:"What is a chemical bond?",options:["Nuclear fusion","Electromagnetic attraction holding atoms together in compounds","A physical connection","A magnetic force"],answer:1,explain:"Chemical bonds (ionic, covalent, metallic) result from electron interactions between atoms."},{id:"5005-304",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Physical Science",q:"What is an element's atomic mass approximately equal to?",options:["Number of shells","Number of electrons only","Number of protons plus neutrons","Number of electrons"],answer:2,explain:"Atomic mass \u2248 protons + neutrons (electrons contribute negligible mass)."},{id:"5005-305",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Physical Science",q:"Why do noble gases rarely form compounds?",options:["They are reactive","They have no electrons","They are too heavy","They have full valence electron shells, making them chemically stable"],answer:3,explain:"Noble gases (He, Ne, Ar, etc.) have complete outer shells\u2014stable configurations requiring high energy to disrupt."},{id:"5002-188",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Reading",q:"What is a theme statement?",options:["A complete sentence expressing the central message about life or human nature","A plot summary","A setting description","A character name"],answer:0,explain:"Theme statements go beyond topics: 'Courage requires facing fear' not just 'courage.'"},{id:"5002-189",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Reading",q:"Why is structure important in expository writing?",options:["Structure doesn't matter","Organization helps readers follow arguments and locate information efficiently","Structure is decorative","It makes text longer"],answer:1,explain:"Logical structure (cause-effect, compare-contrast, problem-solution) guides readers through complex information."},{id:"5002-190",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Reading",q:"A student decodes grade-level text accurately and understands the vocabulary but cannot make inferences, summarize, or notice when meaning breaks down. Which skill is most likely weak?",options:["Reading speed","Decoding","Reading comprehension strategies: inferencing, synthesizing, and monitoring understanding","Letter recognition"],answer:2,explain:"The evidence rules out basic decoding and vocabulary problems and points to weak higher-order comprehension strategies. Explicit instruction in inference, summarization, and comprehension monitoring directly targets the observed difficulties."},{id:"5002-191",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Reading",q:"What is dramatic irony?",options:["Situational comedy","Verbal sarcasm","Irony in poetry","Audience knows something characters don't, creating tension"],answer:3,explain:"Dramatic irony occurs when the audience understands meaning that characters miss\u2014classic in Shakespeare and Greek tragedy."},{id:"5002-192",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Reading",q:"What is a legend?",options:["A traditional story based on historical events, often with legendary figures","A factual history","A scientific report","A poem"],answer:0,explain:"Legends blend historical kernels with fictional embellishment (e.g., King Arthur, Robin Hood)."},{id:"5002-193",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Reading",q:"How does author's word choice (diction) affect meaning?",options:["All words are interchangeable","Connotation, formality, and specificity of words shape tone, mood, and precision of meaning","Word choice doesn't matter","Only syntax matters"],answer:1,explain:"Diction choices ('slim' vs. 'gaunt' vs. 'scrawny') carry different connotations and create distinct impressions."},{id:"5002-194",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Reading",q:"Which strategy best develops inferential comprehension?",options:["Memorizing passages","Reading faster","Teaching students to ask 'what does this suggest?' and support inferences with textual evidence","Literal questioning only"],answer:2,explain:"Inference requires combining textual clues with background knowledge\u2014explicit strategy instruction builds this skill."},{id:"5002-195",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Reading",q:"What is allegory?",options:["A biography","A joke","A type of rhyme","A narrative where characters and events symbolize abstract ideas or moral qualities"],answer:3,explain:"Allegories like 'Animal Farm' use extended metaphor\u2014characters represent types or ideas throughout the narrative."},{id:"5002-196",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Reading",q:"What is a cliffhanger?",options:["An unresolved situation at the end of a chapter or episode that creates suspense","A character type","A setting","A type of poem"],answer:0,explain:"Cliffhangers create anticipation by ending at a moment of high tension or unanswered questions."},{id:"5005-306",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Life Science",q:"What is photosynthesis?",options:["Cell division","Process by which plants convert sunlight, CO\u2082, and water into glucose and oxygen","Breaking down glucose","Respiration"],answer:1,explain:"Photosynthesis: 6CO\u2082 + 6H\u2082O + light \u2192 C\u2086H\u2081\u2082O\u2086 + 6O\u2082\u2014plants manufacture their own food."},{id:"5005-307",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Life Science",q:"Why are photosynthesis and cellular respiration complementary?",options:["They are unrelated processes","Both processes create oxygen without using matter","Some products of one are reactants of the other, so matter cycles while energy flows through the system and eventually dissipates as heat","Both processes consume oxygen in the same way"],answer:2,explain:"Photosynthesis stores light energy in chemical bonds and produces glucose and oxygen; respiration uses those materials and releases carbon dioxide and water. Matter can cycle between the processes, but energy flows one way and is eventually released as heat."},{id:"5005-308",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Life Science",q:"Students think plants get food from soil. How to correct?",options:["Agree with them","Tell them they're wrong","Skip photosynthesis","Demonstrate that plants gain mass from CO\u2082 (air), not soil\u2014soil provides water and minerals, not 'food'"],answer:3,explain:"Plant mass comes primarily from atmospheric CO\u2082 fixed into organic molecules via photosynthesis, not from soil nutrients."},{id:"5005-309",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Life Science",q:"What is the role of chlorophyll?",options:["Capturing light energy for photosynthesis","Absorbing water","Making roots","Producing CO\u2082"],answer:0,explain:"Chlorophyll absorbs light (mainly blue and red wavelengths), converting photon energy to chemical energy."},{id:"5005-310",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Life Science",q:"What is the equation for cellular respiration?",options:["6CO\u2082 + 6H\u2082O + light \u2192 C\u2086H\u2081\u2082O\u2086 + 6O\u2082","C\u2086H\u2081\u2082O\u2086 + 6O\u2082 \u2192 6CO\u2082 + 6H\u2082O + ATP (energy)","No equation exists","6O\u2082 + glucose \u2192 ATP only"],answer:1,explain:"Cellular respiration breaks down glucose with oxygen to release ATP, CO\u2082, and water."},{id:"5005-311",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Life Science",q:"Why can some bacteria use anaerobic respiration in oxygen-poor environments?",options:["Anaerobic respiration always yields more energy than aerobic respiration","All such bacteria are incapable of using oxygen under any condition","They can use terminal electron acceptors such as nitrate or sulfate instead of oxygen","They perform photosynthesis instead of metabolism"],answer:2,explain:"In anaerobic respiration, an electron transport chain uses an acceptor other than oxygen, such as nitrate or sulfate. This differs from fermentation, which does not use an external terminal electron acceptor."},{id:"5005-312",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Life Science",q:"Which lab best demonstrates gas exchange in plants?",options:["Boiling leaves","Counting stomata","Measuring root length","Observing oxygen bubble production from aquatic plants in light vs. darkness"],answer:3,explain:"Elodea bubble counts in light vs. dark clearly show photosynthesis produces oxygen only when light is available."},{id:"5005-313",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Life Science",q:"What is fermentation?",options:["Anaerobic energy production without oxygen, yielding less ATP","Cell division","Photosynthesis","Aerobic respiration"],answer:0,explain:"Fermentation (lactic acid or alcoholic) produces 2 ATP per glucose without oxygen\u2014much less efficient than aerobic respiration."},{id:"5005-314",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Life Science",q:"What is the primary energy carrier in cells?",options:["Glucose","ATP (adenosine triphosphate)","DNA","Water"],answer:1,explain:"ATP stores and transfers energy for cellular work through phosphate bond hydrolysis."},{id:"5005-315",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Life Science",q:"Why is the Calvin cycle called 'light-independent' if it needs light indirectly?",options:["It occurs at night only","It doesn't need anything","It uses ATP and NADPH from light reactions but doesn't directly require photons","It is completely independent"],answer:2,explain:"The Calvin cycle fixes CO\u2082 using energy carriers produced by light reactions\u2014indirectly dependent but not directly powered by photons."},{id:"5004-218",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"World History and Economics",q:"What is the Renaissance?",options:["A political revolution","A religious war","An economic depression","A cultural rebirth (14th-17th centuries) emphasizing humanism, art, and scientific inquiry"],answer:3,explain:"The Renaissance revived classical learning and emphasized human potential, Individualism, and empirical observation."},{id:"5004-219",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"World History and Economics",q:"What was the main economic impact of the Industrial Revolution?",options:["Shift from hand production to machine manufacturing, urbanization, and global trade expansion","Decreased production","Isolation of economies","Return to agriculture"],answer:0,explain:"Industrialization transformed production methods, created factory systems, and accelerated urban growth worldwide."},{id:"5004-220",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"World History and Economics",q:"Students think the World Wars were inevitable. How to nuance this?",options:["Skip WWI/WWII","Show how specific decisions, alliances, and crises escalated tensions\u2014contingency matters in history","Tell them wars are random","Agree with them"],answer:1,explain:"Historical contingency: specific choices and events could have been different\u2014avoiding deterministic thinking."},{id:"5004-221",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"World History and Economics",q:"What caused the Great Depression?",options:["Overproduction only","Foreign invasion","Stock market crash, bank failures, reduced consumer spending, and international trade collapse","Natural disaster"],answer:2,explain:"The Great Depression resulted from multiple interconnected factors: financial collapse, banking panics, and global trade breakdown."},{id:"5004-222",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"World History and Economics",q:"Which civilization built Machu Picchu?",options:["Aztec","Olmec","Maya","Inca"],answer:3,explain:"Machu Picchu was built by the Inca Empire in the 15th century in the Andes mountains of Peru."},{id:"5004-223",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"World History and Economics",q:"What is the significance of the Marshall Plan?",options:["It rebuilt Western Europe after WWII and contained Soviet influence through economic aid","It started the Cold War","It was purely military","It destroyed European economies"],answer:0,explain:"The Marshall Plan (1948) provided $13 billion in aid to rebuild Europe, preventing communist appeal through prosperity."},{id:"5004-224",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"World History and Economics",q:"Which approach best teaches students about economic systems historically?",options:["Ignoring economics","Comparing how societies throughout history have answered 'what, how, for whom to produce'","Memorizing definitions","Listing countries"],answer:1,explain:"Historical comparison of economic systems (feudal, mercantilist, capitalist, socialist) shows diverse solutions to resource allocation."},{id:"5004-225",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"World History and Economics",q:"What was the Cold War?",options:["A trade agreement","A religious movement","Ideological and geopolitical tension between US-led West and Soviet-led East without direct large-scale warfare","A military conflict"],answer:2,explain:"The Cold War (1947-1991) was ideological competition fought through proxy wars, arms races, and espionage."},{id:"5004-226",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"World History and Economics",q:"Who was Cleopatra?",options:["A Greek philosopher","An Egyptian pharaoh of the Old Kingdom","A Roman emperor","The last active ruler of the Ptolemaic Kingdom of Egypt"],answer:3,explain:"Cleopatra VII (51-30 BCE) was Egypt's last pharaoh, known for alliances with Julius Caesar and Mark Antony."},{id:"5004-227",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"World History and Economics",q:"Why did the Ottoman Empire decline?",options:["Military defeats, economic stagnation, nationalist movements, and inability to modernize relative to European powers","Alien invasion","Single cause","Volcanic eruption"],answer:0,explain:"Ottoman decline resulted from compounded military losses, economic competition, and internal nationalist movements over centuries."},{id:"5003-136",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Numbers and Operations",q:"What is 1/3 expressed as a decimal?",options:["0.33","0.333...","0.5","0.3"],answer:1,explain:"1/3 = 0.333... (repeating decimal, often written as 0.3\u0305)."},{id:"5003-137",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Numbers and Operations",q:"Students think that 0.5 is bigger than 0.25 because 5 > 25. How to correct?",options:["Skip decimals","Tell them they're wrong","Use visual models: 0.5 = 1/2 = 50/100; 0.25 = 1/4 = 25/100\u201450/100 > 25/100","Give more practice"],answer:2,explain:"Decimal place value matters: 0.5 means 5 tenths (0.50), while 0.25 means 2 tenths + 5 hundredths (0.25)."},{id:"5003-138",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Numbers and Operations",q:"What is the sum of the interior angles of a triangle?",options:["270\xB0","90\xB0","360\xB0","180\xB0"],answer:3,explain:"Any triangle's interior angles sum to 180\xB0\u2014a fundamental geometric theorem."},{id:"5003-139",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Numbers and Operations",q:"What is 3/4 + 1/2?",options:["5/4 or 1 1/4","1/2","4/8","4/6"],answer:0,explain:"3/4 + 1/2 = 3/4 + 2/4 = 5/4 = 1 1/4."},{id:"5003-140",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Numbers and Operations",q:"Why can you not add fractions with different denominators directly?",options:["You can always add them","Different denominators mean different-sized parts\u2014you must find a common unit (LCD) first","Fractions cannot be added","Adding is always wrong"],answer:1,explain:"Like terms must have same units: 1/3 + 1/4 requires common denominator (12) to combine meaningfully."},{id:"5003-141",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Numbers and Operations",q:"Which model best teaches fraction division?",options:["Give algorithms only","Memorize 'flip and multiply'","Use area models or visual partitions to show 'how many groups of size 1/4 fit in 3/4'","Skip fraction division"],answer:2,explain:"Visual models show why 'invert and multiply' works\u2014it counts how many divisor-sized pieces fit into the dividend."},{id:"5003-142",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Numbers and Operations",q:"What is 25% of 80?",options:["15","30","25","20"],answer:3,explain:"25% \xD7 80 = 0.25 \xD7 80 = 20. Also: 25% = 1/4, and 80 \xF7 4 = 20."},{id:"5003-143",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Numbers and Operations",q:"What is the reciprocal of 3/5?",options:["5/3","3/5","-3/5","0"],answer:0,explain:"Reciprocal is found by flipping numerator and denominator: (3/5)\u207B\xB9 = 5/3."},{id:"5003-144",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Numbers and Operations",q:"Why is the set of rational numbers closed under division (except by zero)?",options:["It is not closed","Dividing any two rationals (a/b \xF7 c/d = ad/bc) produces another rational number","Division always gives irrationals","Only sometimes true"],answer:1,explain:"Rationals are closed under division (by nonzero): ratio of ratios is still a ratio of integers."},{id:"5005-316",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Earth Science",q:"What is the biosphere?",options:["Earth's atmosphere","Earth's rocks","The global sum of all ecosystems\u2014where life exists","Ocean depths"],answer:2,explain:"The biosphere includes all living organisms and their environments on Earth's surface."},{id:"5005-317",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Earth Science",q:"Why do continental shelves have high biodiversity?",options:["They have no predators","Temperature is constant","They are deep","Shallow, sunlit waters over nutrient-rich shelves support abundant plant and animal life"],answer:3,explain:"Continental shelves are shallow, receive sunlight for photosynthesis, and have nutrient upwelling\u2014ideal for marine life."},{id:"5005-318",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Earth Science",q:"Students think that all earthquakes happen at plate boundaries. How to correct?",options:["Show intraplate earthquakes (e.g., New Madrid, 1811-12) caused by ancient fault reactivation","Skip earthquake science","Tell them they're wrong","Agree with them"],answer:0,explain:"While most quakes occur at boundaries, some happen within plates where ancient stresses reactivate."},{id:"5005-319",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Earth Science",q:"What is a tsunami?",options:["An ocean current","A series of ocean waves caused by underwater displacement (earthquake, landslide, volcanic eruption)","A type of wave from wind","A tidal phenomenon"],answer:1,explain:"Tsunamis are generated by sudden vertical displacement of seafloor, sending energy through the entire water column."},{id:"5005-320",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Earth Science",q:"What is the atmosphere's ozone layer's function?",options:["Creates weather","Blocks all sunlight","Absorbs harmful UV radiation from the sun","Produces oxygen for breathing"],answer:2,explain:"The ozone layer (stratosphere) absorbs UV-B and UV-C radiation, protecting life from DNA damage."},{id:"5005-321",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Earth Science",q:"Why does the Coriolis effect deflect objects to the right in the Northern Hemisphere?",options:["Magnetic fields","It is a coincidence","Gravity pulls sideways","Earth's rotation creates apparent deflection in moving objects relative to the rotating surface"],answer:3,explain:"Earth rotates eastward; objects moving north/south maintain their eastward velocity, appearing to deflect right in the Northern Hemisphere."},{id:"5005-322",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Earth Science",q:"Which demonstration best shows the water cycle?",options:["Seal a small amount of water in a clear bag and place it in sunlight so students can observe evaporation, condensation, and droplets returning downward","Read definitions of evaporation and condensation","Observe clouds outdoors without recording changes","Copy a labeled water-cycle diagram"],answer:0,explain:"The sealed-bag model lets students directly observe water changing state and cycling within a closed system. Reading or copying diagrams does not provide the same observable evidence."},{id:"5005-323",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Earth Science",q:"What is a rift valley?",options:["A mountain range","A linear depression formed by diverging tectonic plates","An ocean trench","A volcanic cone"],answer:1,explain:"Rift valleys form where continental crust stretches and thins before breaking apart (e.g., East African Rift)."},{id:"5005-324",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Earth Science",q:"Why can earthquakes occur within tectonic plates as well as at their boundaries?",options:["Tectonic plates never deform internally","Earthquakes cannot occur within plates","Stresses can reactivate old faults within a plate, although most earthquakes occur near boundaries","Only underwater faults produce earthquakes"],answer:2,explain:"Most earthquakes occur at plate boundaries, but intraplate stress can reactivate ancient faults, as in the New Madrid seismic zone. Therefore plate interiors are less active, not earthquake-free."},{id:"5003-145",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Geometry and Measurement, Data, Statistics, and Probability",q:"What is the area of a rectangle with length 8 and width 5?",options:["26","13","80","40"],answer:3,explain:"Area = length \xD7 width = 8 \xD7 5 = 40 square units."},{id:"5003-146",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Geometry and Measurement, Data, Statistics, and Probability",q:"Why does the area formula for a parallelogram equal base \xD7 height?",options:["A parallelogram can be rearranged into a rectangle with the same base and height","It equals a triangle's area","It is a convention","No geometric reason"],answer:0,explain:"Cutting a triangle from one side and moving it to the other transforms the parallelogram into an equivalent rectangle."},{id:"5003-147",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Geometry and Measurement, Data, Statistics, and Probability",q:"Students confuse circumference and area of circles. Which comparison helps?",options:["Skip circles","Show that circumference = distance around (linear), area = space inside (square units)\u2014use string and grid paper","Give more practice","Memorize formulas"],answer:1,explain:"Concrete manipulation: wrapping string around a circle vs. counting unit squares inside builds distinction."},{id:"5003-148",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Geometry and Measurement, Data, Statistics, and Probability",q:"What is the mean of 12, 15, 18, 21, 24?",options:["21","12","18","15"],answer:2,explain:"The mean is the sum divided by the number of values: (12 + 15 + 18 + 21 + 24) \xF7 5 = 90 \xF7 5 = 18. Choosing 21 would confuse the mean with one of the upper data values."},{id:"5003-149",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Geometry and Measurement, Data, Statistics, and Probability",q:"What is the volume of a cube with side length 4?",options:["16","48","12","64"],answer:3,explain:"Volume = s\xB3 = 4\xB3 = 64 cubic units."},{id:"5003-150",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Geometry and Measurement, Data, Statistics, and Probability",q:"What is the probability of rolling a sum of 7 with two dice?",options:["1/6","5/36","1/36","7/36"],answer:0,explain:"Six combinations make 7: (1,6)(2,5)(3,4)(4,3)(5,2)(6,1). P = 6/36 = 1/6."},{id:"5003-151",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Geometry and Measurement, Data, Statistics, and Probability",q:"Which tool best teaches students about volume relationships between shapes?",options:["Drawing shapes","Pouring water from pyramid into prism to show volume = \u2153 base \xD7 height","Formulas only","Memory techniques"],answer:1,explain:"Hands-on water displacement demonstrates the 1:3 volume relationship between pyramid and prism directly."},{id:"5003-152",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Geometry and Measurement, Data, Statistics, and Probability",q:"What is the median of 10, 20, 30, 40, 50, 60?",options:["30","50","35","40"],answer:2,explain:"With even number of values, median = average of middle two: (30+40)/2 = 35."},{id:"5003-153",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Geometry and Measurement, Data, Statistics, and Probability",q:"What is the mode of 3, 5, 5, 7, 9, 9, 9, 11?",options:["5","3","11","9"],answer:3,explain:"Mode is most frequent value. 9 appears three times\u2014most frequent."},{id:"5003-154",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Geometry and Measurement, Data, Statistics, and Probability",q:"A data set is 4, 6, 6, 7, and 22. Which measure best describes a typical value when the high outlier is considered?",options:["Median, because it is less affected by the outlier","Mean, because it is always one of the data values","Range, because it identifies the center","Mode, because it uses every value"],answer:0,explain:"The median is 6 and is resistant to the unusually high value 22. The mean is pulled upward by the outlier, while range measures spread rather than center."},{id:"5005-325",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Life Science",q:"Students think mitosis produces genetically different cells. How to correct?",options:["Tell them they're wrong","Show that mitosis makes identical copies; meiosis creates variation through crossing over","Agree with them","Skip cell division"],answer:1,explain:"Mitosis produces identical daughter cells; meiosis shuffles genes via crossing over and independent assortment."},{id:"5005-326",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Life Science",q:"Why are decomposers essential in ecosystems?",options:["They are optional","They produce oxygen","They recycle nutrients from dead organisms back into the ecosystem","They consume producers"],answer:2,explain:"Decomposers break down organic matter, returning nutrients to soil for producers to use\u2014closing the nutrient cycle."},{id:"5005-327",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Life Science",q:"What is a food chain?",options:["A geological process","A type of plant","Energy source","Linear sequence showing energy transfer from producer to consumers"],answer:3,explain:"Food chains show energy flow: producers \u2192 primary consumers \u2192 secondary consumers \u2192 decomposers."},{id:"5005-328",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Life Science",q:"What is adaptation?",options:["Trait that improves organism's survival and reproduction in its environment","Random change","Physical exercise","Climate change"],answer:0,explain:"Adaptations evolve through natural selection\u2014traits that enhance fitness become more common in populations."},{id:"5005-329",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Life Science",q:"Why do invasive species threaten ecosystems?",options:["They are colorful","They lack natural predators, outcompete natives, and disrupt ecological balance","They eat too much","They are always larger"],answer:1,explain:"Invasive species exploit empty niches, outcompete native species lacking defenses, and alter food webs dramatically."},{id:"5002-197",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Reading",q:"What is an oxymoron?",options:["Exaggeration","Comparison with like/as","Combination of contradictory terms (e.g., 'bittersweet')","Repetition"],answer:2,explain:"Oxymoron joins opposing concepts: 'deafening silence,' 'living death,' 'bittersweet memory.'"},{id:"5002-198",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Reading",q:"Why is thesis statement crucial in argumentative writing?",options:["It is decorative","Only needed for fiction","Thesis doesn't matter","It states the central claim and previews the argument's direction"],answer:3,explain:"Thesis statements guide both writer and reader\u2014without a clear claim, arguments lack coherence and direction."},{id:"5002-199",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Reading",q:"Students can decode words but cannot explain what they read. What intervention helps?",options:["Strategy instruction: predicting, questioning, clarifying, summarizing (PQ4R)","Reading faster","More decoding practice","Skipping comprehension"],answer:0,explain:"Comprehension strategy instruction builds metacognitive awareness\u2014students learn to monitor and repair understanding."},{id:"5002-200",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Reading",q:"What is personification?",options:["Sound repetition","Giving human qualities to non-human things","Exaggeration","Comparison using like/as"],answer:1,explain:"Personification attributes human traits to objects: 'The wind whispered,' 'The sun smiled.'"},{id:"5002-201",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Reading",q:"How does conflict drive a narrative?",options:["Conflict is unnecessary","Only action matters","Conflict creates tension and drives character development and plot resolution","Conflict confuses readers"],answer:2,explain:"All stories need conflict\u2014internal or external\u2014to create stakes, motivation, and narrative momentum."},{id:"5002-202",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Reading",q:"Which strategy best develops vocabulary for struggling readers?",options:["Skipping unknown words","Memorizing word lists","Ignoring vocabulary","Contextual learning: reading widely, word consciousness activities, and morphological analysis"],answer:3,explain:"Vocabulary grows best through rich exposure, word-part analysis (prefixes, roots, suffixes), and contextual inference."},{id:"5002-203",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Reading",q:"What is hyperbole?",options:["Extreme exaggeration for effect","Understatement","Sound repetition","Comparison with like/as"],answer:0,explain:"Hyperbole is deliberate exaggeration: 'I've told you a million times,' 'This bag weighs a ton.'"},{id:"5002-204",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Reading",q:"Why might an author use flashback structure?",options:["Because they forgot chronological order","To provide background context that explains current motivations and plot developments","Flashbacks are never useful","To confuse readers"],answer:1,explain:"Flashbacks reveal backstory that illuminates character motivations, creating dramatic irony and depth."},{id:"5004-228",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"United States History, Government, and Citizenship",q:"What are the three branches of US government?",options:["Senate, House, Supreme Court","Federal, State, Local","Legislative, Executive, Judicial","President, Congress, Courts"],answer:2,explain:"Constitutional separation: Legislative (makes laws), Executive (enforces), Judicial (interprets)."},{id:"5004-229",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"United States History, Government, and Citizenship",q:"Students think the Bill of Rights restricts only the federal government. Which explanation best corrects the misconception?",options:["The Bill of Rights directly governs every private action","State governments may ignore the Bill of Rights","The Fourteenth Amendment has been used to apply most Bill of Rights protections to state governments","The Bill of Rights was repealed after the Civil War"],answer:2,explain:"Through selective incorporation under the Fourteenth Amendment, courts have applied most Bill of Rights protections to state and local governments. These protections still generally concern government action, not ordinary private conduct."},{id:"5004-230",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"United States History, Government, and Citizenship",q:"What is the purpose of the Preamble?",options:["To state the Constitution's guiding purposes and principles","To define taxes","To declare war","To list laws"],answer:0,explain:"'We the People' preamble states goals: form a more perfect union, establish justice, ensure domestic tranquility."},{id:"5004-231",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"United States History, Government, and Citizenship",q:"Which simulation best teaches students about amendment process?",options:["Ignoring the process","Having students propose, debate, and vote on class constitutional amendments","Reading text","Memorizing amendments"],answer:1,explain:"Simulating amendment procedures gives students hands-on experience with deliberation, compromise, and constitutional change."},{id:"5004-232",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"United States History, Government, and Citizenship",q:"What is filibuster?",options:["A court ruling","A method of counting ballots","A Senate tactic used to prolong debate or delay or prevent a vote","An executive order"],answer:2,explain:"A filibuster is a Senate strategy for prolonging debate or delaying or blocking a vote. For most legislation, cloture generally requires 60 votes; different rules apply to some nominations and procedures."},{id:"5004-233",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"United States History, Government, and Citizenship",q:"How long is a US Senate term?",options:["8 years","2 years","4 years","6 years"],answer:3,explain:"Senators serve 6-year terms with one-third elected every two years for continuity."},{id:"5004-234",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"United States History, Government, and Citizenship",q:"What is the tension between federal and state power called in modern context?",options:["Federalism disputes over reserved vs. enumerated powers","Amendment process","Judicial review","Impeachment"],answer:0,explain:"Federalism tensions continue over issues like healthcare, education standards, and marijuana legalization."},{id:"5003-155",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Numbers and Operations",q:"What is the LCM of 6 and 8?",options:["2","24","14","48"],answer:1,explain:"Multiples of 6: 6,12,18,24... Multiples of 8: 8,16,24... First common is 24."},{id:"5003-156",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Numbers and Operations",q:"Why is GCF used to simplify fractions?",options:["GCF makes fractions bigger","It is arbitrary","Dividing numerator and denominator by GCF reduces fraction to lowest terms","Simplification has no purpose"],answer:2,explain:"Dividing both parts by their GCF removes all common factors, producing the irreducible form."},{id:"5003-157",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Numbers and Operations",q:"Students think that dividing always makes numbers smaller. How to correct?",options:["Tell them they're wrong","Agree with them","Skip fraction division","Show dividing by fractions < 1 makes numbers larger: 4 \xF7 \xBD = 8"],answer:3,explain:"Dividing by numbers less than 1 increases the quotient\u20144 halves fit into 4, so 4 \xF7 \xBD = 8."},{id:"5003-158",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Numbers and Operations",q:"What is 3/8 expressed as a decimal?",options:["0.375","0.25","0.38","0.83"],answer:0,explain:"Dividing the numerator by the denominator gives 3 \xF7 8 = 0.375. The distractor 0.38 is only a rough rounding and is not the exact decimal requested."},{id:"5003-159",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Numbers and Operations",q:"Why is the product of any number and 1 equal to that number?",options:["It depends on the number","1 is the multiplicative identity\u2014taking one group of something gives you that amount","It is arbitrary","1 is special"],answer:1,explain:"Identity property: n \xD7 1 = n. One whole group of n items contains n items."},{id:"5003-160",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Numbers and Operations",q:"Which model best teaches percentage equivalence?",options:["Long division","Memorizing rules","Using 100-grid squares to show 50% = 50/100 = 1/2 visually","Decimal conversion only"],answer:2,explain:"Visual grids make percentage-fraction-decimal equivalence concrete\u201450 shaded squares = half = 0.5."},{id:"5003-161",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Numbers and Operations",q:"What is 2.5 as a fraction in simplest form?",options:["25/100","2/5","1/5","5/2 or 2\xBD"],answer:3,explain:"Converting 2.5 to a fraction gives 25/10, which simplifies by 5 to 5/2, or 2 1/2. The fraction 2/5 instead equals 0.4."},{id:"5003-162",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Numbers and Operations",q:"Which statement about rational and irrational numbers is true?",options:["Every decimal is irrational","A terminating or repeating decimal is rational","The square root of every whole number is rational","Irrational numbers can be written as ratios of integers"],answer:1,explain:"A terminating or repeating decimal can be expressed as a fraction and is therefore rational. Irrational numbers have nonterminating, nonrepeating decimal expansions and cannot be written as ratios of integers."},{id:"5005-330",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Physical Science",q:"What is Newton's first law of motion?",options:["Every action has an equal reaction","An object at rest stays at rest, and an object in motion stays in motion unless acted upon by a force","F = ma","Gravity pulls everything down"],answer:1,explain:"Law of inertia: objects maintain their state of motion unless a net external force acts on them."},{id:"5005-331",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Physical Science",q:"Why does a car skid on ice?",options:["Gravity disappears","Cars are lighter on ice","Ice reduces friction\u2014the force needed to change the car's motion is absent","Engine stops working"],answer:2,explain:"Ice minimizes friction, removing the force that normally allows tires to grip and change direction."},{id:"5005-332",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Physical Science",q:"Students think that force is needed to keep an object moving. How to correct?",options:["Skip Newton's laws","Tell them Newton's law","Agree with them","Demonstrate in near-frictionless environment (air track) that object continues moving without force"],answer:3,explain:"In reality, friction stops moving objects\u2014but in absence of friction, objects would continue forever (Newton's 1st law)."},{id:"5005-333",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Physical Science",q:"What is kinetic energy formula?",options:["KE = \xBDmv\xB2","KE = Fd","KE = mgh","KE = mc\xB2"],answer:0,explain:"Kinetic energy depends on mass and the square of velocity: KE = \xBDmv\xB2."},{id:"5005-334",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Physical Science",q:"Which demonstration best shows conservation of energy?",options:["Counting atoms","Rolling ball down ramp: potential energy converts to kinetic, total energy remains constant (ignoring friction)","Measuring mass","Weighing objects"],answer:1,explain:"Ramp experiments clearly show PE converting to KE\u2014total energy conserved if friction is minimal."},{id:"5005-335",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Physical Science",q:"What is power?",options:["Energy","Force","Rate of doing work (P = W/t), measured in watts","Mass"],answer:2,explain:"Power measures how fast work is done: 1 watt = 1 joule per second."},{id:"5005-336",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Physical Science",q:"Why does a pendulum eventually stop swinging?",options:["Gravity disappears","Energy is destroyed","Pendulums lose energy spontaneously","Air resistance and friction convert mechanical energy to heat\u2014energy is conserved but transformed"],answer:3,explain:"Energy is never destroyed\u2014it transforms from mechanical to thermal energy through air resistance and pivot friction."},{id:"5004-235",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"Geography, Anthropology, and Sociology",q:"What is population density?",options:["Number of people per unit area (usually per square kilometer)","Death rate","Birth rate","Total population"],answer:0,explain:"Density = population \xF7 area\u2014measures how crowded a region is."},{id:"5004-236",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"Geography, Anthropology, and Sociology",q:"Students think population growth is always bad. How to nuance?",options:["Tell them they're wrong","Discuss trade-offs: labor force vs. resource strain, innovation vs. environmental impact","Ignore the issue","Agree with them"],answer:1,explain:"Population dynamics have complex consequences\u2014balanced discussion requires examining both costs and benefits."},{id:"5004-237",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"Geography, Anthropology, and Sociology",q:"What is a push factor in migration?",options:["Tourist attraction","Reason to stay","Condition that drives people away from their current location","Reason to visit"],answer:2,explain:"Push factors (war, poverty, persecution) compel people to leave; pull factors (opportunity, safety) attract them elsewhere."},{id:"5004-238",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"Geography, Anthropology, and Sociology",q:"What is cultural diffusion?",options:["Cultural isolation","Genetic migration","Cultural extinction","Spread of cultural traits from one society to another through trade, migration, communication"],answer:3,explain:"Cultural diffusion spreads ideas, technologies, and practices through contact between societies."},{id:"5004-239",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"Geography, Anthropology, and Sociology",q:"Which project best teaches students about global interconnectedness?",options:["Tracing product supply chains from raw materials to consumer to understand global interdependence","Ignoring globalization","Reading textbooks","Memorizing facts"],answer:0,explain:"Supply chain analysis reveals how daily goods connect consumers to distant workers, resources, and environments."},{id:"5004-240",code:"5004",subtest:"Social Studies",category:"Geography, Anthropology, and Sociology",q:"Why do some regions remain economically underdeveloped?",options:["Random chance","Historical exploitation, lack of infrastructure, governance challenges, and geographic barriers compound over time","All regions develop equally","Climate is the only factor"],answer:1,explain:"Development gaps result from complex interactions: colonial legacies, institutional quality, geography, and resource access."},{id:"5005-337",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Life Science",q:"What is the basic unit of life?",options:["Atom","Organ","Cell","Tissue"],answer:2,explain:"Cell theory: cells are the fundamental structural and functional units of all living organisms."},{id:"5005-338",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Life Science",q:"Why do organisms need water?",options:["Water provides decorative color to cells","Only plants require water","Water dissolves many ionic and polar substances, supports biochemical reactions, and helps regulate temperature","Water dissolves every known substance"],answer:2,explain:"Because water is polar, it is a good solvent for many ionic and polar substances and provides a medium for metabolic reactions. It also helps regulate temperature, but it is not a universal solvent that dissolves everything."},{id:"5005-339",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Life Science",q:"Students think evolution means 'survival of the strongest.' How to correct?",options:["Teach that evolution favors organisms best adapted to their environment\u2014not necessarily strongest","Skip evolution","Tell them they're wrong","Agree with them"],answer:0,explain:"Fitness = reproductive success in a specific environment\u2014being well-adapted matters more than raw strength."},{id:"5005-340",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Life Science",q:"Why are bacteria considered important despite causing disease?",options:["Only viruses matter","They decompose waste, fix nitrogen, aid digestion, and produce antibiotics","Bacteria are useless","They are all harmful"],answer:1,explain:"Most bacteria are beneficial\u2014essential for nutrient cycling, human health, and industrial processes."},{id:"5005-341",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Life Science",q:"Which lab activity best demonstrates natural selection?",options:["Memorizing definitions","Reading about Darwin","Simulating predator-prey with colored beads on different backgrounds showing differential survival","Ignoring selection"],answer:2,explain:"Predator-prey simulations with colored beads show how camouflage affects survival rates\u2014natural selection in action."},{id:"5005-342",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Life Science",q:"What is the role of the nervous system?",options:["Blood circulation","Bone formation","Digestion","Coordination and control through electrical signals"],answer:3,explain:"The nervous system detects stimuli, processes information, and coordinates responses via neurons and electrical impulses."},{id:"5005-343",code:"5005",subtest:"Science",category:"Life Science",q:"Why do genetic mutations matter for evolution?",options:["Mutations create genetic variation\u2014the raw material upon which natural selection acts","Mutations are always harmful","Mutations are random and irrelevant","Mutations don't affect evolution"],answer:0,explain:"Mutations introduce new alleles into populations\u2014without genetic variation, evolution cannot occur."},{id:"5003-163",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Algebraic Thinking",q:"What is the slope of y = 3x + 2?",options:["5","3","2","6"],answer:1,explain:"In y = mx + b form, slope m = 3."},{id:"5003-164",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Algebraic Thinking",q:"What does the slope represent in y = 2x + 5 where x = hours worked, y = dollars earned?",options:["Starting pay","Total pay","Hourly wage ($2 per hour)","Tax rate"],answer:2,explain:"Slope represents the rate of change: $2 per hour worked. Y-intercept ($5) is the starting amount."},{id:"5003-165",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Algebraic Thinking",q:"Students solve 2(x+3)=10 correctly but don't understand why. What approach helps?",options:["Tell them to memorize","Skip algebra","Give more problems","Use algebra tiles to show 2 groups of (x+3) equals 10 total"],answer:3,explain:"Manipulatives make algebraic operations concrete\u2014tiles show why distributing and solving works physically."},{id:"5003-166",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Algebraic Thinking",q:"Solve: 3x - 7 = 14",options:["7","5","21","3"],answer:0,explain:"Add 7: 3x = 21. Divide by 3: x = 7."},{id:"5003-167",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Algebraic Thinking",q:"What is the solution to x + 8 = 15?",options:["120","7","8","23"],answer:1,explain:"Subtract 8 from both sides: x = 15 - 8 = 7."},{id:"5003-168",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Algebraic Thinking",q:"When does a system of equations have exactly one solution?",options:["Always","When lines are parallel","When lines have different slopes (intersect at one point)","Never"],answer:2,explain:"Different slopes guarantee intersection at exactly one point\u2014unique solution."},{id:"5003-169",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Algebraic Thinking",q:"Which tool best helps students understand inverse operations?",options:[" Memorization","Formulas","Skipping operations","Balance scale: showing operations as adding/removing weights to maintain equality"],answer:3,explain:"Balance scales visualize equation-solving as maintaining equality while isolating the variable."},{id:"5003-170",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Algebraic Thinking",q:"What is the equation of a line with slope 4 passing through (0, -2)?",options:["y = 4x - 2","y = 4x + 2","y = -2x + 4","y = 4x"],answer:0,explain:"Point-slope form with (0,-2): y = 4x - 2. Y-intercept is -2."},{id:"5003-171",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Algebraic Thinking",q:"Evaluate 2x + 3 when x = 4",options:["14","11","7","8"],answer:1,explain:"Substitute: 2(4) + 3 = 8 + 3 = 11."},{id:"5003-172",code:"5003",subtest:"Mathematics",category:"Algebraic Thinking",q:"Why are proportional relationships represented by y = kx?",options:["All relationships are linear","It is arbitrary","Constant ratio k means y is always k times x\u2014straight line through origin","Proportions don't exist"],answer:2,explain:"Proportionality means constant rate: y/x = k for all values, producing a line through (0,0) with slope k."},{id:"5002-205",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Writing, Speaking, and Listening",q:"What is the purpose of a transition word?",options:["To fill space","Decoration","Transitions are optional","To show logical relationships between ideas and paragraphs"],answer:3,explain:"Transitions (however, therefore, consequently) guide readers through argument structure and logical flow."},{id:"5002-206",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Writing, Speaking, and Listening",q:"Why is audience awareness critical in writing?",options:["Different audiences require different tone, evidence, and organization","All audiences are the same","Only teachers read student writing","Audience doesn't matter"],answer:0,explain:"Audience determines register, examples, and argument strategy\u2014a technical report differs from a narrative essay."},{id:"5002-207",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Writing, Speaking, and Listening",q:"Students write paragraphs with unrelated sentences. Which skill needs instruction?",options:["Grammar","Topic sentence development and supporting detail relevance","Spelling","Sentence length"],answer:1,explain:"Paragraph coherence requires a controlling idea (topic sentence) with all sentences developing that central point."},{id:"5002-208",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Writing, Speaking, and Listening",q:"What is a hook in essay writing?",options:["A grammatical error","A conclusion","An engaging opening that captures reader interest","A bibliography entry"],answer:2,explain:"Hooks (statistics, anecdotes, questions) draw readers in and establish engagement with the topic."},{id:"5002-209",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Writing, Speaking, and Listening",q:"What is a thesis statement?",options:["An introduction","A conclusion","A question","A clear claim that states the main argument of an essay"],answer:3,explain:"Thesis statements answer the prompt directly and preview the essay's structure\u2014usually in the introduction."},{id:"5002-210",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Writing, Speaking, and Listening",q:"Why is editing different from proofreading?",options:["Editing revises content and structure; proofreading checks mechanics (spelling, grammar, punctuation)","Proofreading comes first","Editing is optional","They are the same"],answer:0,explain:"Editing improves organization, clarity, and argument; proofreading catches surface errors after content is finalized."},{id:"5002-211",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Writing, Speaking, and Listening",q:"Which method best teaches students rhetorical appeals?",options:["Memorizing definitions","Analyzing speeches for ethos (credibility), pathos (emotion), logos (logic) in authentic texts","Reading about rhetoric","Ignoring appeals"],answer:1,explain:"Rhetorical analysis of actual speeches (MLK, Churchill) makes abstract concepts concrete and applicable."},{id:"5002-212",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Writing, Speaking, and Listening",q:"What is active voice?",options:["Passive construction","Object performs the action","Subject performs the action (The dog bit the man)","No subject needed"],answer:2,explain:"Active voice is direct and clear: subject does the action. Passive voice ('the man was bitten by the dog') is often wordier."},{id:"5002-213",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Writing, Speaking, and Listening",q:"What is a concluding paragraph's main function?",options:["Define terms","Introduce new ideas","List sources","Restate thesis in new words and summarize main points"],answer:3,explain:"Conclusions reinforce the argument's main points without introducing new evidence\u2014leaving reader with clear takeaway."},{id:"5002-214",code:"5002",subtest:"Reading and Language Arts",category:"Writing, Speaking, and Listening",q:"Why is revision cyclical rather than linear?",options:["Each revision may reveal new organizational issues requiring earlier-stage rethinking","Revision is unnecessary","First draft is always final","Writing has no process"],answer:0,explain:"Revision often reveals structural problems discovered only during editing\u2014creating recursive cycles of improvement."}];
